The rapidly spreading cumulative outbreak of coronavirus (Covid-19) in the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom, January 1 to March 15, 2020
The increasing outbreak of coronavirus cases (COVID-19) shows a similar trend in the countries of the European Economic Area/EU, and the United Kingdom confirms that while in different countries, we are at a different stage of the pandemic, the COVID-19 pandemic is progressing rapidly in all countries.
Based on Italy’s experience, other countries, hospitals and intensive care units should increase their readiness to increase the number of patients with COVID-19 who need intensive care.
On December ۳۱ ۲۰۱۱۹۱۹, a group of cases of pneumonia with an unknown cause was reported in Wuhan, China's Houbi province.
On January 9, 2020, the Chinese Centers for Disease Control and Prevention reported the cause as a type of new coronavirus called Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
Since then, SARS-CoV-2 was referred to as coronavirus (covid-19).
Documents obtained to date show that about eighty percent of people with COVID-19 have a mild illness, i.e
In about 14 percent of cases, COVID-19 becomes a more severe illness that requires hospitalization, while the remaining six percent experience a state of crisis that requires intensive care.
The death toll of patients in the hospital due to COVID-19 is about 4%.
In this study, we evaluate the increasing prevalence of the coronavirus in each EU/EU/EEA (EU/EEA) and the United Kingdom (UE/EEA) (UK) and compare them with Hubei Province, China.
We also compare the current number of cases of COVID-19 in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom, with the number in Italy from January 31st to March 15, 2020.
Cases of the COVID-19 virus in the countries of the European/EEA and the United Kingdom
After China, COVID-19 was published in more geography, and the dynamics of the pandemic in other parts of the world are now following the country.
On March 11, 2020, the Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO) announced that COVID-19 was a pandemic.
Spiteri and colleagues in the journal Eurosurveillance on March 5, 2020 reported the first confirmed case of COVID-19 in Europe based on the World Health Organization’s definition.
In the European/EU/EEA, three cases approved by France were reported on January ۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۰ those who had returned from Wuhan, China's Hubi province.
On March 15, 2020, cases of COVID-19 were identified in all ۳۰ European/EEA/UK countries (United Kingdom), according to December ۳۱ ۲۰۱۹۱۹۹۳۳۳۹۶۶۸۷۷۲۷۷۷۲۷۷۲۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۷۲۷
Obtaining the cumulative number and cumulative prevalence of COVID-19 cases
At the European Center for Disease Control (ECDC), cases of COVID-19 in various countries around the world are obtained only from official sources such as the Ministry of Health, the National and Regional Health Organization and the World Health Organization, which are updated every day at 8:00 a.m.
This data was used to evaluate the COVID-19 trend in the European Union/EEA and the United Kingdom and compare it with the disease process in Italy.
As an indicator for the prevalence of active cases of COVID-19, we calculated the cumulative prevalence of COVID-19 cases within 14 days, as a result of the normal COVID-19 period in the European/EEA/EEA countries and the United Kingdom from January 1 to March 15, 2020.
Also, the cumulative number of items announced from each country on March ۲۰۲۰ at ۸۰۰ a.m
COVID-19 Trends in EU/EEA/EU countries
۱۴٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫۴٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫�
In general, for the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom, the cumulative outbreak of COVID-19 began an increase around February ۲۱ ۲۱ ۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۰ sharply (complementative requirements).
This mainly stems from the rapid increase in the number of cases reported from Italy, but other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom showed a similar increase from the COVID-19 cumulative outbreak (communicipatory content).
Figure ۲ indicates the cumulative number of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA/EEA countries and the United Kingdom compared to cases in Italy for a period of January ۳۱ to March ۲۲۲۰.
This figure determines that on March ۱۵ at 8 a.m., the total number of cases reported in 15 other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom is comparable to the number of cases in Italy three weeks earlier or less.
Our results indicate that the number of COVID-19 detected cases is increasing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom.
Trends observed in a COVID-19 cumulative outbreak show that the pandemic is currently progressing at nearly the same pace in all countries.
While countries are at different stages of the disease, the response of public health organizations is variable, and there are also many different definitions and protocols in countries to select patients who need to be tested for COVID-19 approval, including increasing the speed of testing.
In early March 2020, doctors in areas affected by the virus in Italy described a situation in which about 10% of patients with COVID-19 required intensive care, and media sources reported that hospitals and intensive care units in these areas reached their maximum capacity.
Information about the admission of COVID-19 cases in the hospital or intensive care unit is currently available at the EU/EEA level only for 6% and 1% (data have not been shown).
In order to complete current regulatory data that focuses on the number of reported cases and the number of deaths, this information must be collected in a systematic manner.
A study conducted during 2010-1911 found that access to intensive care and middle care beds in Europe is very variable, ranging from ۲۹۲ beds per 100,000 people in Germany up to ۴۲ beds per 100,000 people in Portugal.
That is to say, countries may have less or more resources than Italy (12.5 intensive care beds and middle care for every 1,000 population in 11–201)
Modeling scenaries related to saturation of health care capacity, with estimates for various EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom regarding the prevalence of COVID-19 cases that have been hospitalized in relation to the occupancy of more than 90% of the capacity of intensive care beds have been provided in the 6th COVID-19 ECCDC Rapid Risk Assessment Update.
Since disease cases are categorized in certain areas from European/EEA countries and the United Kingdom, hospitals and intensive care units usually provide services to the population of a region, information about cases as well as intensive care beds should preferably be available in the list of territorial units for the statistical level 2 (NUTS-2).
Italy’s experience and the current trends in other countries show that the COVID-19 pandemic is progressing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom.
Therefore, hospitals and intensive care units should prepare themselves for a stable SARS-CoV-2 transmission scenario and an increase in the number of patients with COVID-19 who require health care, especially intensive care, such as what’s taking place in Italy’s infected areas.
As noted in the recent ECDC’s Rapid Risk Assessment, a quick, preventive and inclusive approach to delay the spread of SARS-COV-2 is necessary with a rapid shift from the inhibition-to-reduction approach, as predicting the rapid increase in the number of cases may cause decision-making authorities and hospitals to not have enough time to understand, accept and adapt their response based on this process.
A rapid risk assessment lists public health measures to reduce the impact of the pandemic.
A brief opportunity for the remaining countries to increase control measures to reduce SARS-CoV-2 emissions and reduce the pressure on the health care sector.
The failure to do so will most likely to face the health care system of other EU/EEA countries with a wave of patients who will need intensive care in the coming days or weeks.
The outbreak of the 2019 (Covid-19) from Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) has so far killed more than 3,000 people and infected ۸۳۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۱۸۳۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰
SARS-CoV-2, like its similar virus, SARS, which caused the infection of thousands of people in 2003, may have been transmitted from bats and caused similar symptoms through a similar mechanism.
However, COVID-19 is less severe than SARS, but it is more able to be transferred, affecting older people more than young people and men.
In response to a large number of published content about the emerging disease, this paper attempts to provide a timely and comprehensive review of this rapidly expanding research topic.
We will cover the basics of epidemiology, pathology, virology, diagnosis, prognosis, and prevent this disease.
Although many questions still require answers, we hope that this review article will help to understand and eradicate this deadly disease.
On January 25, the Spring Festival has become an unprecedented and unforgettable memory for all of the Chinese who were forced to stay at home all the holidays and several weeks later due to a new viral outbreak.
This virus is very similar to the Coronavirus (CoV), which caused the prevalence of acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) in 2003; As a result, the virus was named by the World Health Organization (WHO) as SARS-CoV-2 on February 11, 2020, and the associated disease (COV-19).
This epidemic began in Wuhan, China, and spread rapidly across the country and about 50 other countries around the world.
By March 2, 2020, more than 80,0,000 confirmed cases of COVID-19 were reported, of which more than 4,000 patients had been discharged and more than 3,000 patients had died.
The World Health Organization (WHO) warned that COVID-19 is “the number one enemy of the people” and potentially more powerful than terrorism.
According to PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/), more than ۲۰۰ articles on COVID-19, including virology, epidemiology, pathology, pathology, diagnosis, and treatment, since the first report on January ۷ ۲۲۲۲۲۰ in less than two months, have been published.
In this review article, we will try to summarize the progress of research in this new and rapidly developing subject area.
If possible, we’re going to try to compare COVID-19 with SARS and other infectious disease caused by CoV, Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS, which occurred in 2012).
We’ll also talk about what we’ve learned so far in terms of preventing and curating the disease, as well as some other essential questions.
CoVs have traditionally been considered non-lethal pathogens for humans, mostly leading to about 15 per cent of a common cold.
But in this century, we encountered highly pathogenic human CoVs twice, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which caused the outbreak in China in 2003 and Saudi Arabia in 2012 and soon spread to many other countries with the rate of exepulsion and terrible mortality.
Therefore, the current COVID-19 is the third outbreak of CoV in the recorded history of humanity.
As shown in Figure 1.1, a cluster of unspecified ethophatic cases were first reported from Wuhan to the Chinese National Health Commission on December ۳۱ ۲۰۱۹.
Seven days later, the CoV sequence was released.
On January 15, 2020, the first death toll from Wuhan was reported.
At the same time, the epidemic quickly spread to neighboring cities, provinces and countries.
On January ۲ ۲ ۲٫ ۲٫ ۲۰ٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰ٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫
On January ۲۲۳ ۲٫ ۲۳٫ ۲۳٫ ۲۳٫ ۲۳٫ residents of Wuhan were quarantined at home
On January ۲۲۴, the first clinical study of the disease was reported that out of ۴۱ patients with confirmed cases, only ۲۲۱ people had direct contact with the Wuhan seafood market, which was considered the site of the onset of the infection from an unknown animal source.
The World Health Organization (WHO) on January ۳۰ declared the outbreak a global health emergency.
As long as the report prepares, the disease has spread throughout China and nearly 50 other countries around the world (Figure ۲).
Since the situation is evolving rapidly, the final scope and severity of the spread of the disease is still an issue that needs to be determined.
On February 11, 2020, a study conducted in several centers on ۸۸۸۶۸۶۶۲۲۲۲۱ COVID-19-approved cases shows a more up-to-date picture of the epidemic as follows (https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/UlBI-H_rBI-HX_rHPXa1qHA2bHAdA).
People with SARS-CoV-2 have a variety of ages, but mainly 30 to 65.
Nearly half of the affected people (47.7%) were over the age of ۵۰, a very small number were less than 20 years of age, and only ۱۴ affected people were younger than 10 years of age.
People with SARS-CoV-2 are mostly male (0.31/100,000) to women (0.27/100,000).
COVID-19 spread in various clusters mainly in and around Hubei.
The beginning period to the diagnosis of COVID-19 was an average of five days (two to nine days).
The average incubation period was ۴۸ days (۳۰ to ۷۲ days).
The period of the beginning to death was an average of ۹۵ (۴۸ to ۱۳ days).
The base janic (R0) was 3.77 (95% CI: 3.51-4.05) and the R0 set was 2.23 to 4.82.
The number of affected people increased exponentially before January 23, 2020, which coincides with the busy transportation time before the Spring Festival in China.
The mortality rate of patients with confirmed cases was 1.44% (95% CI: 1.10-1.86%) and the adjusted mortality rate of all patients was 3.06% (95% CI: 2.02-4.59%).
The three main risk factors for COVID-19 included gender (male), age (more than sixty years old) and severe pneumonia.
CoVs are sub-families of large and leafed viruses that contain a single strand of sensory RNA.
These viruses can be divided into four floors of alpha, beta, gamma, and delta, which have been characterized by alpha and beta types of CoVs can spread to humans.
Leafed pineapile glycoprotein (S) binds to host cell angiotensin and converts enzyme 2 (ACE2) and dipistyl peptidease 4 (DPP4) for SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, respectively, and the fusion of the membrane occurs.
The RNA genome of the virus is released in cytoplasm; after the replication of the virus genome, genomic RNA with glicoprotein and nucleoxide proteins forms virion-containing bags that are then attached to the plasma membrane to release the virus.
SARS-CoV-2's first genomic sequence was reported on January 10, 2020.
With the genetic adaptation of more than ۹ٹ/۹ٸ٫ ۱۰ ۱۰ sequencing samples collected from the main site of the outbreak, i.e. the Havanan seafood market in Wuhan, it was found that SARS-CoV-2 is a new type of beta-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 is genetically more like SARS-CoV than MERS-CoV.
Through a passing electron microscoroscope, SARS-CoV-2 particles were found in the ultra-thin part of the human air duct coated tissue.
It was found that human ACE2 acts as the SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV receiver.
However, the S protein from SARS-CoV-2 is weaker than SARS-CoV to human ACE2, which corresponds to the fact that SARS-CoV-2 causes less infection than SARS-CoV in patients.
SARS-CoV-2 can also form a new short protein encoded by orf3b and a secret protein encoded by orf8.
Orf3b in SARS-CoV-2 may play a role in the pathogenesis of the virus prevents IFNβ. However, orf8 does not have any known functional or motif domain.
On February 18, 2020, Zhou and colleagues reported the full-length human ACE2 Chryo EM structure with a full-length resolution of ۲۹ Angsterm in combination with the amino acid carrier B0AT1.
They found that the compound, which had open and closed formulations, was synthesized as a dimmer, and the ACE2-B0AT1 compound can bind two S proteins, which could be evidence for the identification of CoV and infection.
BA0AT1 can become a therapeutic target for drug screening to resuppose SARS-CoV-2 infection.
Origin and Middle Hosting
It turns out that SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV originates from both bats and were transported to humans through the sive and camel cats, respectively.
By comparing SARS-CoV-2 philognetics with other CoVs, it has been found that bats host native SARS-CoV-2 because the 96% virus is identical to two SARS-like CoVZX45 bats and SL-CoVZX21 bats.
However, the question of what the middle host has helped the virus to go through species to infeculate humans remains unanswered, and the path of transmission still requires more enlightenment.
Jay and co-workers believe that snakes, which contain the same combination within the S protein, have acted as a carrier of the wres of bats to humans.
According to one study, researchers in Guangzhou, China, based on a 99 per cent genetic similarity in CoV discovered in Pangolin and SARS-CoV-2, stated that perhaps eating pangolin – a kind of long-treated ants that is often used in traditional Chinese medicine – has acted as the potential mid-host of SARS-CoV-2.
However, the ۱ٱ٪ difference that has been extended in the two genomes is still a big difference, so we are still waiting for definitive results for solid evidence (Figure ۳۳).
SARS-CoV-2 chemical properties are still largely unknown.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV survive in dry environment for up to ۴۸ hours, at 20°C and humidity of 40 to 50 percent to five days.
SARS-CoV-2 may have similar properties.
SARS-CoV-2 has been reported to be sensitive to ultraviolet radiation and 56°C heat for 30 °C for 30 minutes, ethanol 75%, disinfectants containing chlorine, prastic acid, chloroform, and other fatty solvents other than chlorrhexidine, and these can effectively deactivate the virus.
The human population is generally unimmunited against SARS-CoV-2 and is therefore prone to the new virus.
There is currently not a detailed study on immunological response to SARS-CoV-2.
In this way, we can only refer to previous studies of other CoVs, especially SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV. (Figure ۴).۴).
In general, after a virus attack on the host cell, the recognition of the virus is primarily performed by the host's intrinsic immune system through pattern detection receptors (PRRs) including type lectin-like receptors, Toll-like receptors (TLR), NOD (NLR), and the RIG-I (RLR).
Through various pathways, the virus causes the emergence of inflammatory factors, the maturity of dendritic cells, and the synthesis synthesis (IFNs), which limits the spread of the virus and accelerates the macrophage of phagocytes in the antigen virus.
However, the protein N in SARS-CoV can help the virus escaping from the immune system.
The adaptive immune system soon joins the fight against the virus.
T lymphocytes, including CD4+ and CD8 + T cells, play an important role in defense.
CD4 + T cells stimulate B cells to produce specific antibodies of the virus, and CD8 + T cells directly kill cells infected with the virus.
T-helping cells produce pro-inflammatory cytokines to help defend cells.
However, CoV can instill the function of T cells by inducing T-cells apoptosis.
Humoral immunity includes supplements such as C3a and C5a, and antibodies are also essential in the fight against viral infection.
For example, the detached antibody from the healed patient causes MERS-CoV to be neutralized.
On the other hand, an overreaction of the immune system results in the production of a large number of free radicals locally, which can cause severe damage to the lungs and other organs, and in the worst-case scenario, result in multi-organ failure and even death.
SARS-CoV-2, which is characterized by cluster emergence, mostly influences elderly people with underlying diseases and pregnant women.
It’s a rumor that people who are exposed to a large amount of the virus or have poor immune function are more likely to be infected than others.
According to the study of the first 425 cases in Wuhan, the average of SARS-CoV-2 inquisition was determined to be 1-14 days, but in most cases it lasts from three to seven days.
However, a study of ۱،۰۹۹ cases shows that the incubation period is an average of three days and in the period from zero to ۲۴ days.
A newer study shows that, as described above, the incubation period is ۴۸ days (۳۰ to ۷۲ days), which is based on statistics of ۸۸۶۶
It is very important for the health authorities to determine an effective quarantine time based on the most accurate incubation, thereby preventing the transmission of the virus from infected people who do not have symptoms to others.
A common method is that people exposed or infected with the virus must usually be quarantined for 14 days.
Should quarantine time be extended up to ۲۴ days?
In most cases, fever is the main and primary symptom of COVID-19, which can be asymptomatic or with other symptoms such as dry cough, shortness of breath, muscular pain, dizziness, headache, sore throat, runny nose, chest pain, diarrhea, nausea and vomiting.
Some patients experienced shortness of breath or decreased oxygen in the tissues a week after the onset of the disease.
In severe cases, the disease is rapidly moving towards acute respiratory syndrome, septic shocks, metabolic acidification, and blood coagulation disorder.
Patients with fever or respiratory symptoms and severe fever, even without pulmonary anomalies in radiological images, should be screened for early detection of the virus.
A demographic study in late December in late December shows a percentage of symptoms: 98% fever, 76% dry cough, 55% shortness of breath and ۳ٳٳٳ٫ patients needed a ventilator.
Similar findings were reported in two recent studies of a family cluster and a cluster of transmission from an unmarked person.
In a population study conducted in 2012, it was found that in patients with MERS-CoV, fever (۹۸ percent), dry cough (۴۷ percent) and shortness of breath (55 percent) were seen as the main symptoms.
However, eighty percent needed a ventilator, which is much higher than COVID-19 patients, which indicates that MERS is more deadly than COVID-19.
Diarrhea (26%) and sore throat (21%) were also seen in MERS patients.
In patients with SARS, it has been shown that fever (۹۹۱۱۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰ٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٱ۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲٫۲۲۲۲۱۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۰۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲), and
By February ۱۴, and when confirmed cases globally reached 6,576 cases, the percentage of COVID-19 deaths was about 2%.
In comparison, SARS’s death rate had been confirmed by November 2002 about 10 per cent of ۸،۰ٹ۶.
According to a demographic study in June 2012, the percentage of deaths for MERS accounted for 37% of ۲,۴۴۹۴ confirmed cases.
A previous previous study found that the R0 goes from SARS-CoV-2 to 6.47, with a 95% reassurance distance (CI) around 5.71 to 7.23, while the R0 range for SARS-CoV is only from 2 to 4%.
SARS-CoV-2 comparison with MERS-CoV and SARA-CoV in terms of symptoms, mortality rate and R0 is presented in Table 1.1.
The above images show that SARS-CoV-2 is more capable in terms of prevalence than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, but SARS-COV-2 is lower than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV.
In this way, the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic is much harder to control than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV.
Cluster starts often take place in a family or from a gathering location or a vehicle, such as a cruise ship.
Patients often have had a history of traveling or staying in Wuhan or other infected areas or contact with infected people or patients two weeks before the onset of the disease.
However, it has been reported that people can carry the virus for more than two weeks without symptoms, and patients recovered and discharged from the hospital can also transmit the virus again, which is a warning to increase quarantine time.
In the early stages of the disease, the number of white blood cells (especially lymphocytes) in patients is normal or low.
For example, lipophania with a white blood cell count of less than 4×109/L, including lymphocyte numbers less than 1×109/L, and aminotrotransferease aspartate levels were increased, and the presence of the virus in the blood was found in 1,099 patients infected with COVID-19.
The levels of liver and muscular enzymes and myoglobin increased in the blood of some patients, and C-reactive protein and red blood cell sedimention increased in most patients’ blood.
In patients with severe cases, the levels of Dimmer D, which is the result of fibroin destruction in the blood, increased and the number of lymphocytes was gradually decreasing.
Observable abnormalities are seen in chest radiography images in most COVID-19 patients, and its properties are two-by-semit shades or opaque turbulence in the lungs.
In most patients, abnormal pneumonia, acute lung damage and acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).
When ARDS occurs, uncontrolled inflammation, fluid accumulation and advanced fibrosives severely jeopardize a person’s air exchange.
Type I and II dysfunction in pneumocytes reduces surfactant levels and increases surface elasticity, thereby reducing the lung's ability to expand and the risk of lung failure increases.
Therefore, the worsening chest radiography findings, the greater the extent of the disease.
On February 18, 2020, the first pathological analysis of COVID-19 showed that pneumocyte shelling, the formation of glass membranes and the penetration of interstitual lymphocytes, and multi-cosular sincial cells in the lungs of the disease, which has died due to the disease, are consistent with the pathology of viral and ARDS infection, similar to SARS and MERS patients.
SARS-CoV-2 RNA-recognition was used through reverse polymerase-refrincing (RT-PCR) chain reaction as the main criterion for detecting COVID-19.
However, due to the above-mounted false negative rate of this method, which may accelerate the epidemic, on February 13, 2020, the use of clinical symptoms (which no longer relied only on RT-PCR) began to diagnose the disease in China.
There was a similar situation for SARS diagnosis.
Therefore, a combination of disease history, clinical symptoms, laboratory tests, and radiological findings is necessary and necessary to have an effective diagnosis.
On February 14, 2020, the Feng Zhang Group proposed a protocol using CRISPR-based Sherlock methods to identify SARS-CoV-2, which detects synthetic RNA fragments related to SARS-CoV-2 at 20×10-18 mol/L to 200×10-18 mol/L (۱۰۰ copies per microliter).
There is hope that the new method can dramatically increase the accuracy and convenience of the test if approved in clinical samples.
Due to the lack of experience regarding this new type of CoV, physicians can mainly provide supportive care to COVID-19 patients, while various treatments used to improve other CoVs such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV and other viral diseases have been suggested (Table ۲).
These treatments include current and potential treatments with anti-virus drugs, immunosuppressive drugs, steroids, plasma received from improved patients, Chinese medicine and psychological support.
Even plasmas received from recovered patients were suggested for use in treatment.
Pharmaceutical companies are competing to produce antibodies and vaccines against the virus.
SARS-CoV-2 mainly attacks the lungs at the beginning and is likely to have weaker attacks on other organs that have ACE2, such as the digestive system and kidneys.
Accordingly, dysfunction and failure of the respiratory system are the most important risk in lurking patients and the main cause of death.
Therefore, respiratory support is vital to relieve the symptoms of the disease and save a person's life, which, depending on the severity of the disease, can include general oxygen therapy, high-current oxygen, non-invasive ventilators and invasive vantilators.
Patients who have severe respiratory symptoms should be supported by exorportal membrane oxygenation (ECMO), which is a modified pulmonary bypass technique that is used to treat heart or respiratory failure.
In addition, it is also necessary to maintain electrolyte balance, prevention and treatment of secondary infection and septic shock, and maintenance of vital organs in patients with SARS-CoV-2.
It has been found that cytokin storms are derived from an overresponse of the immune system in patients with SARS and MERS.
Cytocine is a systemic inflammatory response characterized by the release of a cytokine series including TNFα, IL-1β, IL-2, IL-6, IFNα, IFNβ, IFNγ and MCP-1.
This cytokin induces immune cells to release a large number of free radicals, the main cause of ARDS and multi-member insufficiency.
Suppression of the immune system is essential in the treatment of cytokine storms, especially in patients with dire conditions.
Corticosteroids and tocilizumab, an anti-IL6 monoclonal antibody, have been used to treat cytokine storm.
Other treatments to suppress the immune system to prevent cytokin storms include the modulation of the guided immune response with T-cells; the IFN-γ blockage, IL-1 and TNF; the JAK inhibition; belinatomob; the suppressider of the cytokine symptoms and HDAC inhibitors.
Steroids were widely used as immunosuppressive drugs in SARS treatment to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
However, the use of steroids in high doses of severe lung damage was not helpful in patients with SARS and COVID-19.
Rather, it may cause severe side effects, especially evascular osteocrosis, and have a significant negative impact on the recovery.
However, short periods of using corticosteroids at low to medium doses are advised to be discreetly used for patients with COVID-19 who are in a critical condition.
At the time of writing, no effective antiviral treatment for the disease has been confirmed.
However, it has been found that intravenous intervention with remdesivir, a nucleotid analog, has become effective in an American patient with COVID-19.
Remdesivir is a new anti-virus drug developed by Gilead initially to treat diseases caused by Ebola and Marlburg viruses.
The possible containment of other single-strength RNA viruses, including MERS and SARS viruses, was shown by remdesivir.
Accordingly, Gilead has sent the composition to China to conduct a series of experiments in people infected with SARS-CoV-2, and the results were very promising.
In addition, baricitinb, interferon-α, lopinavir/ritonavir and ribavirin have been suggested as a potential treatment for patients with acute respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhea, nausea, vomit vomiting, liver damage, and other side reactions can occur after combined treatment with lopinavir/ritonavir.
Interference of this treatment with other medications used in patients should be carefully monitored.
Plasma received from improved patients and antibody production
It has a long history of taking blood from patients who have recovered from a communicable to help treat other patients who suffer from a similar disease or to protect healthy people against the disease.
In fact, patients who have recovered often have a relatively large antibody in their blood to deal with the pathogen.
Antibodies are immunoglobulin (Ig), produced by B lymphocytes to fight pathogens and other external objects, detect unique molecules in pathogens and directly neutralize them.
Accordingly, plasma was taken from the blood of a group of patients who had recovered from COVID-19 and was injected into 10 ill patients.
Their symptoms improved within ۲۲۴ hours, which were accompanied by a decrease in inflammation and viral load and improved oxygen saturation in the blood.
However, before certain treatments are provided, the use of this large-scale procedure requires approval and clarification.
In addition, due to the therapeutic effects, some plasma-related disadvantages should be carefully considered.
For example, antibodies can overstimulate the immune response and cause cytokin release syndrome, which is potentially a life-threatening poison.
The concentration of antibodies in the blood is usually low and the demand for plasma to treat patients is very high.
It’s difficult to procure and quickly produce special antibodies that are enough to combat the global epidemic.
Therefore, the separation of B cells from recovered patients and the identification of genetic codes that encode antibodies or sieve antibodies are more important and more practical against the main proteins of the virus.
In this way, we can increase the production of antibodies quickly.
TCM has been used for thousands of years to treat a variety of diseases in China.
However, its effects are largely dependent on the composition of different components in the formula, which varies depending on TCM theories.
Most of its effective components are still unknown or ambiguous because it is difficult to extract and verify these components or their optimal composition.
Currently, due to the lack of effective and specific treatment for COVID-19, TCM has become one of the most important treatments for patients with mild symptoms or patients with moderate symptoms or those who have recovered from difficult stages.
For example, Shu Feng Jie Du and Lian Hua Qing Wen capsules were found to be effective in the treatment of COVID-19.
The highest rates of treatment in COVID-19 patients were observed in several Chinese provinces, in which they used TCM for 87% of patients, including in Gansu (63.7%), Ningxia (50%) and Hunan (50%). In Hubei Province, where only 30 percent of COVID-19 patients used TCM, the lowest treatment (۱ٳٳٳٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰٰ).
However, this comparison is not quite correct, as many factors, such as the number and severity of patients, should be included in the evaluation.
On February 18, 2020, Boli Zhang and his colleagues published a study to compromate Western (WM) therapy alone and the combined WM and TCM therapy.
They found that the time needed to restore body temperature, the disappearance of symptoms, and hospitalization in a group treated with WM+TCM was significantly shorter than a group that was only treated with WM.
Surprisingly, the rate of worsening symptoms (from mild to severe) in the WM+TCM group was lower (7.4% versus 46.2%) and the mortality rate in the WM+TCM group was lower (8.۸٪ versus 39%).
However, the efficiency and safety of TCM is still waiting for best-controlled tests on larger scales and more centers.
Also, determining the mechanism of action and specifying the effective components of the TCM treatment method or their compounds, if possible, is an issue that arouses the curiosity of scientists.
Suspicious patients or COVID-19-approved patients often have a lot of fear of this very contagious and even deadly disease, and quarantined people suffer from boredom, loneliness and anger.
In addition, the symptoms of this infection, such as fever, hypoxia (dehydration of oxygen in body tissues), coughing, and side effects of treatment, such as insomnia caused by corticosteroids, can lead to more anxiety and mental anxiety.
In the first stage of SARS outbreak, a variety of psychiatric complications including severe depression, anxiety, panic attacks, psychological-motival emotions, psychological symptoms, delirium, and even suicide have been reported.
Required and quarantined contact tracking, as part of the public health system’s method to deal with the spread of the COVID-19 virus, can make people anxious and guilty of contagion, quarantine, and fascination between their families and friends.
Therefore, psychotherapy care should be provided for COVID-19 patients, suspicious people, people in contact with them, as well as the general public in need.
Psychological support should include the creation of mental health teams from different disciplines, transparent communication with regular and detailed updates about the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak and treatment schemes and the use of professional electronic devices and applications to prevent close contact with each other.
Effective vaccines are used to cut the chain of transmission from livestock populations and humans infected with susceptible hosts and are often used as a complementary method for antiviral treatment in the control of contagious diseases caused by emerging viruses.
Efforts have been made to make S-based vaccine-based vaccines to produce strong and lasting anti-neutral antibodies or protective immunity against SARS-CoV.
Destructed live vaccines have been evaluated in animal models for SARS.
However, the effectiveness of these vaccines in the elderly and the deadly rigid models and their protection against common viral infections between humans and animals should be determined before starting a clinical study.
This is probably because SARS was eradicated seventeen years ago and no new cases have been reported since.
Conversely, the scattered cases and clusters of MERs in the Middle East continue to be seen and spread to other regions due to the existence of animal-to-human transmission sources in indigenous areas.
Vaccination strategies for MERS have been developed using inactive viruses, DNA plasmides, viral carriers, nanoparticles, virus-like particles and recombinant protein subunits, some of which have been evaluated in animal models.
Producing a safe and effective vaccine against SARS-CoV-2 for non-safe people is an essential and vital duty to control the epidemic.
However, it is difficult to overcome due to the long period that is necessary to produce the vaccine (an average of eighteen months) and the dynamic changes of different CoV variants.
As a new disease, COVID-19 has begun to mark a complete clinical course among thousands of newly established patients.
In most cases, patients recover gradually and without complications.
However, such as SARS and MERS, the COVID-19 virus is also linked to complications and high mortality in patients with acute cases.
Hence, the creation of a predictive disease flow model is essential for health and health organizations to prioritize their services, especially in areas with limited facilities.
According to clinical studies that have been reported so far, the following factors can affect the flow of COVID-19 patients (Table 33):
Age: Age was the most important predictor of the SARS flow, which is also true for COVID-19.
COVID-19 mainly occurs between the ages of 30 and 65; in a study of ۸،۸۶۶۶۷۴ٴ٪ patients over the age of ۴۷۴ٴ٪ over the age of ۵۰.
Patients in need of intensive care were more likely to have underlying complications and abnormalities, and they were older than those who didn't need intensive care (the average age of the first group was ۶۶ and the middle age of the second group was 51 years old).
Gender: SARS-CoV-2 infects most men so that women (0.31/100,000 versus 0.27/100,000), as explained above.
Previous Diseases and Complications: Patients with COVID-19 who need intensive care were more likely to have acute heart and arrhythmia injuries.
Heart events were also the main cause of the death of SARS patients.
SARS-CoV-2 can also be linked to ACE2-positive colangiocytes, leading to liver disorder in COVID-19 patients.
It should be noted that age and underlying diseases are strongly intertwined with each other and may interact with each other.
Abnormal laboratory findings: The level of the C-reactive protein in the blood indicates the severity of inflammation or damage in the tissue and has been suggested to be a potential factor for the disease, a response to treatment and final recovery.
Correlation of CRP levels with the severity and prediction of the flow of COVID-19 has also been suggested.
In addition, an increase in dehydrogenase lactate (LDH), aspartate aminotranosphere (AST), Alanine aminotranosphere (ALT) and creatine kinase (CK) can also help in predicting the results.
These enzymes are widely represented in several organs, especially in the heart and liver, and are released from tissue damage.
Therefore, they serve as traditional symptoms of heart or liver disorders.
The main clinical symptoms: To predict the results and complications of COVID-19, chest radiography and the time progression of clinical symptoms should be considered along with other issues.
Steroid use: As mentioned above, steroids are immunosuppressants, which are commonly used as a treatment for infectious diseases to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
Because high doses of corticosteroids were widely used in SARS patients, many people recover from vascular osteochromosis with lifelong disability and reduced quality of living.
Therefore, if needed, the steroid should be used at a low dose and for a short period of time in COVID-19 patients.
Psychological stress: As described above, many patients have been suffering from extraordinary stress since the COVID-19 outbreak, as they are often quarantined for a long period of time, with severe uncertainty and have witnessed the deaths of close family members and co-mroom patients.
It is necessary to provide psychological counseling and long-term support to help these patients improve and get rid of stress and return to a normal life.
According to-year-to-demographical studies so far, it seems that the epidemiological characteristics of COVID-19 are different from SARS.
SARS-CoV-2, in addition to proliferation in the lower respiratory system, can effectively multiply in the upper respiratory system and, in the first stage of infection, have mild or negligible symptoms, similar to other CoVs that cause common cold.
Therefore, infected patients in the first or incubation can produce many viruses during daily activities, which can make it difficult to control the epidemic.
However, SARS-CoV transmissions occurred when the patients were seriously ill and did not occur in the early stages of the disease.
Hence, the COVID-19 outbreak is much more severe than the SARS outbreak and is more difficult to control.
There are currently a lot of efforts underway in China, including a ban on leaving homes in the city of Wuhan and surrounding cities and the continuous quarantine of almost the entire population in the hope of cutting the SARS-CoV-2 transmission chain.
Although these measures have severely harmed the economy and other parts of the country, the number of new patients is decreasing, indicating a slowdown in the disease.
The most optimistic estimate is that the outbreak will end by March, and the downward trend of the virus will last for three to four months.
However, some other experts aren’t very optimistic.
Paul Hunter and colleagues have estimated that COVID-19, which appears to be fundamentally more contagious than SARS, won’t end in 2020.
Ira Longini and colleagues have established a model to predict the outcome of the epidemic, believing that SARS-CoV-2 could infect two-thirds of the world’s population.
A Canadian group reported that SARS-CoV-2 can be detected in the mid-nasal tentacle swab and throat swab of patients who had left the hospital two weeks earlier, indicating that the newly identified virus could turn into a cyclical disease similar to the flu.
However, there have been promising indications in China based on a reduction in the number of new cases, suggesting that current strategies may work.
Initially, it was predicted that Ebola would cause one-million-million people to die.
However, through quarantine and severe isolation, the disease was eventually controlled.
It’s possible that like SARS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 becomes pathogenesistically weaker and eventually disappears or becomes a virus with low pathogenesis that lives alongside humans.
The comparison of the COVID-19 pandemic with SARS and MERS is shown below (Figure 55).
SARS-CoV-2 is highly transmittable by cough or sneezing and is likely to be transmitted through direct contact with substances infected with the virus.
The virus is also found in the feces, which makes it possible to transfer from the stool to the mouth.
In a new study of 138 cases, it was reported that ۴۱ percent of cases occurred due to a hospital infection, which included 17 patients with previous diseases and ۴۰ medical staff.
Therefore, it is necessary to be very careful in the protection of humans, especially the health care staff, social rescuers, family members, co-workers and even pedestrians who are in contact with patients or people with the disease.
The first line of defense that can be used to reduce the risk of infection is to wear a mask, which can be both through surgical masks and N95 respiratory masks (1860 series), which helps control the spread of viruses.
The surgical mask prevents the passage of liquid droplets distributed by potential people through the air or sticking to the surface of the material where they can be transferred to others.
However, only N95 masks (1860 series) can protect a person from inhaling viral particles from 10 to ۸۰ nanometers, and only 5 percent of viral particles can go through completely; SARS-CoV-2 is similar to SARS-CoV, and both are approximately ۸۵ nanometers.
Since the particles can even go beyond the five surgical masks that are stacked together, the health care staff who are in direct contact with patients should wear the N95 mask, not a surgical mask.
In addition to the mask, the health care staff should wear to further reduce contact with viruses.
Viruses can also infect a person through the eyes.
On January 22, 2020, a doctor was infected with the SARS-CoV-2 virus even though the N95 mask was infected; the virus may have entered his body through his inflamed eyes.
Therefore, the healthcare staff should also wear transparent shields and glasses when working with patients.
For the general public in polluted or potentially contaminated areas, it is highly recommended that people wash their hands more than usual with disinfectant soaps and try to stay at home and limit communication with potential affected people.
A three-foot (one-meter) distance is considered a suitable distance for people to stay away from the patient.
These are effective ways to reduce the risk of infection and prevent the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-2 entered the human world as a new virus, its similarity with SARS-CoV, which was reported on January 7, 2020, had an important warning to China based on deep memories of the SARS outbreak in 2003.
However, by January 19, 2020, the director of the Wuhan Center for Disease Control, comforted the citizens by saying that there is a low contagion of the new virus and its transmission from human to human, and that there is no problem to prevent and contain the disease.
The message significantly eased risk awareness among the general public, especially when the entire country was preparing for the Spring Festival and the critical time to contain the disease on a minimal scale in Wuhan was lost.
The disease control agencies in China may have learned a difficult lesson and make fundamental improvements in the future.
For example, these organizations (۱۱) should be more careful, given that every word referred to by health organizations is important for citizens and can change people's attitudes and decisions; (۲) Instead of waiting for official reports by doctors and authorities, they should be much more sensitive to the unusual information that is received from clinics. (3) Instead of trying to consort the people, they should take more restrictive measures.
An outbreak of COVID-19 by the new SARS-CoV-2 virus began in late December 2019.
In less than two months and times of writing, the virus has spread throughout China and nearly 50 other countries worldwide.
Since the virus is very similar to SARS-CoV and there are similar symptoms between COVID-19 and SARS, the COVID-19 outbreak has created a sense of SARS return.
However, there are significant differences between COVID-19 and SARS that knowing them is essential to contain this epidemic and treat patients.
COVID-19 affects older people older than young people and more men than women, and the severity and mortality rate is also higher in older adults.
SARS fatality was higher than COVID-19 (۱۰۹۱ٱٴ۴ٴٴٴٴٴٴٴٴٴٴٴٴٴٴ٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫٫
Patients with COVID-19 transmit the virus even when they don’t have symptoms, while SARS patients transmitted the virus when they were seriously ill, which made it harder to contain COVID-19 than SARS.
This partly explains why SARS-CoV-2 became more and more widespread than SARS-CoV.
Normal RNA testing in SARS-CoV-2 may be negative in some COVID-19 patients.
On the other hand, treated patients can re-infect the virus and have a positive test result.
These findings dramatically increase the risk of the spread of the virus.
Due to such a rapid advance in COVID-19 investigations, several important issues remain unresolved, which are as follows:
Where did SARS-CoV-2 come from?
Although between SARS-CoV-2 and two SARS CoV, such as a bat, about ninety-six percent of the genetic synonym, we still can’t conclude that SARS-CoV-2 is a bat.
Which animal, as an intermediary, transmitted the virus to humans from the main hosts, such as bats?
Without knowing the answers to questions ۱ and ۲, we can’t effectively stop transmitting, and the spread of the disease may relapse at any time.
Although molecular modeling and biochemical measurements have determined that SARS-CoV-2 is connected to ACE2, how does the virus enter the respiratory cells and cause subsequent changes associated with the disease?
Is the virus attached to cells with ACE2 in other organs?
Without explicit answers to these questions, we cannot achieve a quick and accurate diagnosis and effective treatment.
How long does the outbreak continue?
How does the virus change genetically during the transfusion between humans?
Will the disease all over the world become an epidemic, such as SARS will gradually go away, or will it return periodically, such as the flu?
The answers to the above questions are necessary, but it may take some time to get the answers to the above and many other questions.
However, at any cost that is necessary, we have no choice but to stop this pandemic as soon as possible and bring our lives back to normal.
Animal Origin of Human Coronavirus
For thousands of years, compatibility and adaptation have led to the simultaneous evolution of the coronavirus (COVs) and their hosts, including humans.
Prior to 2003, two human CoV (HCoV) were known to be the cause of a mild illness, such as a cold.
The emergence of Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) and Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS), showed on the side of the coin how devastating and dangerous HCoV infection could be.
The emergence of SARS-CoV-2 in central China in late 2019 put CoVs back in the spotlight and surprised us with its high contagion, but the symptoms were lower compared to her sister SARS-CoV.
HCoV infection is a common type of disease between humans and animals, and recognizing the animal origin of HCoVs is a good help.
Most HCOVs are bats that do not cause disease in their bodies.
Hosts that act as the middle tank of some HCoVs are also known.
The identification of animal hosts has a direct impact on the prevention of disease in humans.
Investigating CoV host interactions in animals can also provide important insight into the pathogenesis of CoV in humans.
In this study, we provide an overview of the existing knowledge of the Seven HCoVs, with a focus on their history of discovery, as well as animal origin and interspective transmission.
The most important thing to look at is the comparison of different HCoVs from the perspective of virus evolution and genetic recombination.
The current coronavirus pandemic (COVID-19) will be discussed in this regard.
In addition, the need to successfully change the host and the evolution of the virus for the severity of the disease is explored in this article.
Corona viruses (COVs) belong to the Coronaviridae family, consisting of a group of luffed viruses, with positive polarity and single-strand RNA.
These viruses, which have the largest genome from ۲۶ to ۳۳۲ kilobis among RNA viruses, called "CoV", due to corn morphology, is under an electron microscope.
Structurally, CoVs have an integrated genome that has a similar structure.
Nearly two-thirds of the genome consists of the two largest screening (ORF1a and ORF1b), which are converted into pp1a and pp1ab replicase polyprotein.
Polyprotein is processed and produces 16 non-structural proteins, which are called nsp1 to 16.
The residual part of the genome consists of ORF, which is used for structural proteins such as Spike (S), Leaf (E), Membrane (M) and nucleopronine (N).
A number of side-by-by-side proteins are encoded by different ancestors of CoVs.
Based on the differences in protein sequences, CoVs are classified into four sexes (alpha-CoV, beta-CoV, gamma-Co-CoV and delta-CoV), among which the beta-CoV genus contains most HCoVs and divided into four races (A, B, C, and D).
Philogenetic evidence has shown that bats and rodents act as a source of alpha-CoVs and beta-CoV genes, while birds are the main reservoir of gamma-CoVs and delta-CoVs.
For thousands of years, CoVs have consistently crossed-by-of-the-counter barriers, and some have emerged as important pathogens to humans.
To this day, seven human CoV (HCoV) is known.
Among them are HCoV-229E and HCoV-NL63, alpha-CoV.
The other five beta-CoVs are HCoV-HKU1, HCoV-OC43, Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV), Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV) and SARS-CoV-2.
HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL6 usually cause mild symptoms such as a cold or diarrhea.
Conversely, MERS-CoV, SARS-CoV, and the new SARS-CoV-2 virus are highly pathogenic, which causes a lower respiratory tract infection in most patients and is more likely to cause acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) and the emergence of out-of-the-pulmonary symptoms.
HCoV-229E, B814, was separated from the nasal secretions of patients with a common cold in the mid-1960s.
From that time onwards, through extensive studies on HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, both of which cause self-restrictive symptoms, our knowledge has increased.
In fact, the concept was widely accepted that HCoVs is generally harmless until SARS spread.
The SARS outbreak occurred in 2003, which is one of the most devastating outbreaks at the present time, infected more than 8,000 people, and its raw fatalities were approximately 10 percent.
Ten years later, the Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS), caused an outbreak of an epidemic in the Arabian Peninsula, which had a sporadic spread to the rest of the world.
In 2019, the new HCoV (2019-nCoV), which was then renamed SARS-CoV-2, became the cause of the coronavirus pandemic in ۲۱۱۲۱۲۲۰۱۲۰۲۰۰۱۱۲۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۱۱۱۱۱۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۱۱۱۱۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۱۰۰۰۰۰۰�
The alarm is ringing and the world needs to prepare for the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic.
All seven HCOVs have an animal origin, which includes bats, mice, or pets.
There’s a lot of evidence that the evolutionary origin of all HCoVs is from bats, in which viruses are well-equipped and as symptom-discomminated, and there’s a lot of genetic diversity.
The COVID-19 pandemic has created enormous medical, scientific, social and ethical challenges for China and the world.
Tracking the animal origin of the disease provides a framework for understanding the natural history, stimulus, and restrictive factors of species mutations.
It is also possible to facilitate the search of the reservoir, the middle host, and animal-reinforminant hosts of SARS-CoV-2, and have important implications in preventing the upcoming pandemic.
In this study, we will have an overview of animal origin, interspecial transmission, and the pathogenophizogenesis of HCoVs.
Particularly, we will highlight and discuss the common issue in which HCoV parent viruses are not normally pathogenic at their natural habitat, but become pathogenic after interspecies are transmitted to the new host.
We will also investigate the evolution of HCoV, in which the increase in the spread of the virus is often associated with a reduction in pathogenesis.
The implications of SARS-CoV-2’s current outage have also been discussed.
Animal CoVs have been known to us since the late 1930s.
Prior to the initial separation of HCoV-229E strain B814, from the nasal secretions of patients with common colds, various CoVs were separated in a variety of animals, including turkeys, mice, cows, pigs, cats and dogs.
Over the past decades, seven HCoVs have been identified.
A brief summary of HCoV's discovery date (Table ۱۱) can be informative and contains good information.
In 1966, the first HCoV-229E censemosis was sampled from the respiratory system of patients with an upper respiratory tract infection, after which it was adapted for growth in WI-38 lung cell culture.
Patients with HCoV-229E had common cold symptoms, including headaches, sneezing, weakness and sore throat, and fever and cough were seen in 10 to 20 percent.
Later in 1967, HCoV-OC43 was separated from organ cultivation in the laboratory and gradually entered the brains of small mice.
It seems that the clinical characteristics of HCoV-OC43 infection appear to be similar to those developed by the HCoV-229 E, which cannot be detected in terms of symptoms from other respiratory infections, such as influenza A and rhinoviruses.
HCoV-229 and HCoV-OC43 are both distributed globally and are mainly transported in temperate climate during the winter season.
In general, the incubation time of these two viruses is less than a week, after which the disease lasts approximately two weeks.
According to studies of human volunteers, healthy people infected with HCoV-229E had a mild cold.
In just a few immunodeficiency patients, a severe lower respiratory tract infection was observed.
SARS, also known as ‘Peak Future Nature’, is the first pandemic in human history to cause SARS-CoV and the 3rd HCoV discovered.
The first SARS can be traced back to China’s Guangdong Province in late 2002.
The SARS outbreak caused ۸۰۹۹۶ ۷۷۴ deaths and spread in many countries and continents.
Apart except for the ultra-publiation states, it has been estimated that each case can infect approximately two other cases, the incubation period of four to seven days and the peak viral load appears on the tenth day of the disease.
Patients with SARS-CoV initially experienced muscle pain, headaches, fever, weakness and chills, followed by shortness of breath, cough, and respiratory diastres as subsequent symptoms.
Lynphopino, an increased liver function and increased kinaze seretin are common laboratory abnormalities of SARS.
Extensive destruction of alveoli, the proliferation of epithelial cells, and the increase in macrophages have also been observed in SARS patients.
Subsequently, about 20 to 30 percent of patients needed intensive care and mechanical ventilators.
In addition to the lower respiratory system, several organs, including gastrointestinal tract, liver, and kidneys, were infected in acute cases, which were usually accompanied by a cytokin storm, which could have fatal immunodeficiency patients.
The virus was first sampled during the open-pulmonary lung biopsy operation, a relative of a prominent patient who had traveled from Gwangzhou to Hong Kong.
Since then, there have been a lot of efforts to investigate HCoV.
HCoV - NL63 separated from a seven-month-old child from the Netherlands in late 2004.
Initially, it was found that the disease was common in young children, older patients and immunodeficiency people with respiratory diseases.
Zakam, conjunctivitis, fever and bronchitis are common in the disease caused by HCoV-NL63.
In another independent study, the separation of the same virus from an eight-month-old-month-old boy who suffers from pneumonia in the Netherlands is described.
Although the virus was detected in the Netherlands, it actually spread globally.
It is estimated that HCoV-NL63 is the cause of about 4.7 percent of chronic respiratory diseases, and its peak outbreak occurs in early summer, spring and winter.
HCoV-NL63 is associated with obstructive larynx inflammation, also known as Krupe.
In the same year, HCoV-HKU1 separated from a seventeen-year-old man who was hospitalized in Hong Kong by pneumonia and bronchitis.
In addition to pneumonia and bronchitis, it has been reported that HCoV-HKU1 is linked to the exacerbation of acute asthma.
Like HCoV-NL63, HCoV-229E and HCoV-229E HCoV-HKU1 are found around the world to cause mild respiratory diseases.
These four families transferred through the community have adapted well to humans and are generally less prone to severe diseases, although cases occurred for unknown reasons, for example in a rare case recently reported, the malignant HCoV-NL63 type has caused a lower respiratory tract infection in China.
In general, when these HCoVs have the ability to effectively transmit and maintain themselves continuously in humans, the severity of virolence or birth disease decreases.
MERS - CoV was separated from the lungs of a sixty-year-old patient with acute pneumonia and kidney failure in Saudi Arabia in 2012.
While most of the laboratory-approved cases of the virus originate from the Middle East, imported cases have been reported due to close contacts in different European and Tunisian countries.
Another second outbreak in South Korea was confirmed in 186.
The clinical emergence of MERS is similar to SARS, which is characterized by progressive acute pneumonia.
Unlike SARS, many patients with MERS also had acute kidney failure, which is specific to MERS among HCoV-related diseases.
More than thirty percent of patients had gastrointestinal symptoms such as diarrhea and vomiting.
By February ۱۴۲۰۲۲۰ , more than 2,500 laboratory-approved cases with high fatalities of 34.4 percent were reported, causing MERS-CoV to be one of the most-known viruses known for humans.
From mid-to-late December 2019, clusters of patients infected with SARS-CoV-2-related infectious diseases were identified in Wuhan, China's Hubi province.
The World Health Organization (WHO) announced the beginning of an outbreak of lower respiratory tract infection caused by SARS-CoV-2, a world-wide public health emergency, calling it COVID-19.
As of March 3, 2020, the number of confirmed cases worldwide reached 90, ۰۵۳, and the percentage of raw fatalities was 3.4 percent.
It’s worth noticing that the death toll in Hubi China was 4.2% while outside it was 1.2%.
SARS-CoV-2, such as SARS - CoV and MERS - CoV causes a severe respiratory infection that can be seen in the form of fever, cough and shortness of breath.
Diarrhea can also be seen in some patients.
pneumonia is one of the most severe symptoms and can quickly become acute respiratory distress syndrome.
Although SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 are very similar, due to 82% homogenization above nucleotide sequences, they are clustered into different branches in the phylogenetic tree.
SARS-CoV-2 appears to have less pathogenesis compared to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, but it is more able to transmit.
It has been reported that some people with SARS-CoV-2 did not have any symptoms, which could contribute to its rapid spread around the world.
Comproming SARS-CoV-2 with six HCoV reveals significant similarities and differences.
The first is that the incubation period and the duration of HCoV's disease period are very similar.
SARS-CoV-2 follows the overall trend of the other six HCoV.
Secondly, the severity of the symptoms of COVID-19 is the middle of SARS-CoV and four HCoV transferred from the community (i.e. HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63).
SARS-CoV-2 infection, on the one hand, indicates characteristics that are commonly seen in HCoV infections transmitted from the community, including non-special, mild, or even in some cases without symptoms.
