On the other hand, it should be confirmed whether SARS-CoV-2 transmission capabilities such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV will be reduced after a few transitions between humans.
Finally, like other HCOVs, SARS-CoV-2 can be detected in stool samples.
That SARS-CoV-2 transmission through feces or urinary, such as SARS-CoV, plays an important role in the case of transmission, at least in some circumstances, must be illuminated by future studies.
It’s also interesting to see if SARS-CoV-2 may be seasonal, like HCOVs transferred from society.
However, SARS-CoV-2 features, including transmission, pathogenesisity, and sustained post-transmissition from person to person, will affect the final fate of the current COVID-19 outbreak.
All four HCoVs transferred from the community that cause mild symptoms of the disease are well adapted to humans.
From another point of view, it may be true that humans are well adapted to these four HCoV.
In other words, they can be survivors of the ancient HCoV pandemic.
HCoVs that caused severe diseases in humans and people with severe HCoV diseases have now gone away.
For this purpose, HCoVs must be reproduced in humans enough to allow the accumulation of adaptive mutations that neutralize host limiting factors.
In this case, the longer the prevalence of SARS-CoV-2 continues and the more people are infected with the disease, there will be a better chance of fully adapting the virus to humans.
If the virus is well adapted, it will be difficult to prevent its transmission in humans by quarantine or other infection control measures.
For several years, four acquired CoVs have circulated in human societies, causing a common cold in people with a healthy immune system.
These viruses don’t need an animal source.
In contrast, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV are incompatible with humans, and their transmission among humans cannot be sustained.
These viruses need to be preserved and spread into their animal reservoirs and are looking for the opportunity to infect susceptible human targets, which is likely to be carried out through one or more intermediate and boosters.
SARS-CoV-2 features similar to SARS-CoV or MERS-CoV and the four HCoV obtained from the community.
The virus, like HCOVs transferred from the community, is highly transmittable, at least for now.
However, its pathogenesis is lower than HCoVs transferred from the community and greater than SARS - CoV or MERS - CoV.
We’ll have to wait to see if the virus is fully compatible with humans and is transmitted among humans without an intermediary or an animal host.
Before discussing the animal origin of HCOVs, it will be useful to examine the definitions and characteristics of evolutionary, natural, reservoirs, intermediary hosts and HCOV replicators.
If an animal bunker is a near-viral who has a high similation at the nucleit sequence level, that animal acts as the evolutionary host of HCoV.
Ancestral virus is usually well adapted in the host and is not pathogenic to it.
In the same way, a repository hoster shelters HCoV for a long time.
In both cases, the host is naturally infected and is the natural host of HCoV or parent viruses.
Conversely, if the HCoV has an intermediary pre-infection of humans, or almost the same time, doesn’t adapt well to the new host and is often pathogenic.
This intermediary host can act as the animal reservoir of human infection, and increase the level of infection in humans by providing the ability to temporarily reproduce the virus and then transmit it to humans.
HCoV can cause deadly infections if it fails to keep its transmission in the middle host stable.
Conversely, HCoVs can be compatible with the mid-host host and even long-term presence in the hosting group.
In this case, the middle host acts as a natural reservoir host.
Epidemiology data show that the first person with SARS was dealing with hunting animals.
Subsequent investigations of serum outbreaks show that levels of anti-SARS anti-virus immunoglobin are higher in animal product sellers more than ordinary people.
For the for the first time, a number of masked pagoma larvata and a raccoon dog were diagnosed on the live animal market carrying SARS-CoV-like viruses, which were roughly the same as SARS-CoV.
