On the other hand, studies in the Middle East have shown that the camels of a mountain are positive in terms of MERS-CoV-refutable antibodies, which also applies to Middle Eastern camels living in an African country.
Live MERS-CoV, similar to the virus found in humans, was sampled from the camel's nose and showed that camels host the actual MERS-CoV reservoir.
It should be noted to note that the generally mild symptoms and the widespread excretion of the virus have been observed in camels that were experimentally infected with MERS-CoV.
It’s worth noting that infected camels spread the virus not only through the respiratory system, but also through a fecal-oral, which is also the main method of excreting the virus in bats.
However, one of the questions that has remained unanswered since then is that in many confirmed cases MERS there has been a history of contact with camels before symptoms occur, which is probably attributed to human-to-human transmission pathways that include unknown animal species.
The SARS-CoV-2 virus has about 96.2 percent similar to the neocleutide sequence with the CoV RaTG13 virus separated from the bat.
Like SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV cases, sequential divergence between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is great to determine the relationship of parents.
In other words, the bat cannot host an uninterrupted reservoir of SARS-CoV-2 virus unless the bat coVs are found almost the same in the future.
Probably, the animal hosts of the SARS-CoV-2 virus, in which many of the early cases of COVID-19 have been seen, must be among the wild animals sold and killed on the Hunane seafood market, indicating the possible transmission of the virus from the animal to humans.
Recent metagenomic sequence studies have shown that a group of small, endangered mammals known named Pangolin (Manis javanica), can also be an ancestral Beta-CoV shelter in conjunction with SARS-CoV-2.
This new genetic variant of the CoV Pangolin virus is about 85 to 92 percent similar to the neucleutide sequence with the SARS-CoV-2 virus.
However, these viruses are associated with about 90 percent similarity of nucleotide sequences to RaTG13.
