In contrast, SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 RBDs are even more different, albeit at a higher degree of sequential similarity throughout the genome.
In a previous study conducted on pangolins, it is reported that from lung samples, fragments have been separated from viral DNA linked to SARS-CoV-2.
In this study, various methods of sequencing and manual modification were used to create a part of the genome sequence that constituted about 86.3% of the length of the virus genome.
