Therefore, the probability cannot be excluded that alpha-CoVs associated with HCoV-229E have been received from humans.
In fact, bats are a direct source of pathogenic viruses in humans, including rabies, Ebola virus, Nipa virus, and the Hendera virus.
So it’s not surprising that bats directly transmit HCoV-229E to humans.
On the other hand, while bat alpha-CoVs act as HCoV-229E gene treasury, alpacas and camels of a mountain may act as intermediary hosts that transmit viruses to humans, just as seen in the case of MERS-CoV.
MERS-CoV is an excellent example of the transition between a species of bats to the camel and from camel to humans.
MERS-CoV's evolutionary origin from the bat was evident from the early detection of the virus, and subsequent findings reinforced this theory.
It is clear that bats provide a tank rich in virus strains to exexchange the species of genetic fragments and interspective transmission.
Long-living, crowded colonies, close social interaction, and a strong ability to fly, are all perfect conditions for the bat, making him an ideal virus diffuser.
On the other hand, MERS-CoV has been entering a mountain camel for decades.
The virus is well adapted to these camels, and this camel has been transformed from an intermediate host into a fixed and natural host.
MERS-CoV causes a very mild illness, and in these animals, they have a relatively low rate of mutation.
Occasional transmission of this virus to humans is a random occurrence, and humans host the MERS-CoV stalemate because its transmission cannot be sustained.
Compared to the role of camels in MERS-CoV transmission, the role of Pangoline, of course, if it plays a role in this case, differs in SARS-CoV-2 transmission.
In particular, pangolin beta-CoV is very pathogenic in Pangolin.
