There are a number of possibilities for the inter-type transmission of SARS-CoV-2 from animals to humans that must be accepted or rejected in future studies.
First of all, the bat can host the SARS-CoV-2 virus-related virus, almost identical to SARS-CoV-2.
Humans may share an ecological place with the bat through butchery or coal mining.
Second, Pangolin can be one of the mid-middle boosters in which a virus associated with SARS-CoV-2 has recently arrived.
Humans are infected with this virus through butchers and the consumption of hunting meat.
It’s possible that many mammals, including pets, are at risk for SARS-CoV-2.
It is necessary to examine domestic and wild animals in terms of antibodies.
Thirdly, as mentioned above, SARS-CoV-2 compatibility may have occurred in a third species that has been in contact with both bats and pangulins.
SARS-CoV-2 animal origin search is still ongoing.
Apart from different types of animal hosts, three other factors from the virus are also important to facilitate the passage of CoVs from existing barriers.
First of all, the relatively high rate of mutation in their RNA replication.
The estimated mutation rate in the CoV virus, with an average replacement rate of about 10-4 substitutions per year, depending on the CoV compatibility stage with the new host, can be considered "average" to "high" compared to other single-strand RNA viruses.
CoVs have an error-corrected exoribonoclase that can lead to a very high mutation, miracy, or even inability to survive.
It is interesting to note that remdesivir of nucleotidia by harnessing these exorbulance and RNA-dependent polymerases prevents coV replication.
Remdesivir is one of the promising anti-SARS-CoV-2 factors that need to be tested in clinical trials.
However, CoV’s bounce rate is about a million times higher than their hosts.
In addition, the bounce rate is high when CoVs aren’t well-compated with the host.
Compared to SARS-CoV with a high bounce rate, the SARS-CoV-2 jump rate appears to be lower, indicating a higher level of compatibility with humans.
The virus may now be adapted to another host that is close to humans.
In addition to SARS-CoV-2, this applies to MERS-CoV, which is well-compliant with the camel of a mountain.
Theoretically, it’s unlikely that genetic change can quickly inhaof vaccines and anti-virrhores against SARS-CoV-2.
Second, the large RNA genome in CoVs provides additional flexibility in genome modification for genetic and recombinatory mutations, thereby increasing the likelihood of mutual transformation between species, which contributes to the emergence of new CoVs under appropriate circumstances.
This is supported by a large amount of unique open reading frameworks and protein functions that are encoded towards the end of the 3′′ genome.
Thirdly, CoVs randomly and repeatedly change templates at the time of RNA replication through the unique "copy-choice" mechanism.
In the hosting that acts as a mixing tank, string switching occurs frequently at the timing of the RNA CoV transcription.
Subgeneral and fully identical long-lasting RNAs can recombine together to produce new CoVs.
In both HCoV - HKU1 and HCoV - OC43, as well as animal coVs such as bat - CoV and batCoV - HKU9, phylogenetic evidence has been found for natural recombination.
Virus Interaction - Host in Relation to Transition
In addition to the three viral factors mentioned above, the interaction of the virus with the host receptor is another key factor influencing interspecies transmission.
Here, SARS-CoV recombinance is considered to be a typical example, which demonstrates evidence of positive selection during an interspecial transmission event.
Based on a comparative analysis between human detached and SARS-CoVs side, SARS-CoV is thought to have a fast-paced match in different hosts, especially with a mutation in the RBD Prothin S.
In general, RBD interacts with the cell receptor in the protein S virus CoV and triggers the host’s intense antibody reaction.
In the SARS-CoV virus, it is located in Amino Acid ۳۱۱۱۸ to ۵۱۱۰ and on Fergument S1, which binds to human ACE2 and common virus input receptors.
RBD SARS-CoV is able to detect ACE2 receptors of various animals, including bats, sives, mice and raccoon dogs, which make it possible to transmit interspecies for the virus.
In fact, it was observed that only the remainder of six amino acids are different from the viral samples taken from humans and syveties in RBD, with four of them in the mutifier-recipient to interact with the ACE2 receptor.
SARS - CoV Civette has a K479N and S487T mutation in its RBD, which may increase the desire to interact between the chilad protein with a human ACE2 receptor.
In other words, these two amino-acid substitutes may be critical to viral compatibility with humans.
It should be noted that SARS-CoV-2 has a cell receptor similar to SARS-CoV.
The existence of a 30% difference between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV-CoV in the S1 Prothin S unit indicates that the desire to connect protein S with human ACE2 may have changed.
In fact, a cryo-EM study shows that there is a 10 to 20-fold tendency for this link between human ACE2 and SARS-CoV S protein.
It will also be interesting to determine whether another common receiver is required for SARS-CoV-2 transmission.
Interestingly, the HCoV-NL63 also connects to ACE2, but with another part of the S.
There are many HCoV receptors, such as aminopepptidase N for HCoV-229E, and 9-O-acetylated sialic acid for HCoV-OC43.
These receptors may also explain the successful compatibility of these CoVs in humans after the transition between a species of animal hosts.
In addition to cell receptors, the result of inter-type transmission of HCoVs is also controlled by other host and restrictive factors.
The divergence of host proteins between humans and natural HCoV hosts such as bats, single camels and rodents may create an obstacle to interspecial transmission.
For an interdisciplinary transition, HCoV must control host-dependent agents and eliminate host limiting factors.
In this regard, molecular determinants in this important area of virus-host interaction should be identified and identified.
Completely unbiased genome screening of host dependencies and restrictive factors for SARS-CoV-2 using advanced CRISPR technology may be fruitful.
New HCoVs: From the Beginning
The diversity of Bat CoVs has provided many opportunities for the emergence of new HCoVs.
In this case, Bat CoVs act as an HCoV gene repository.
In addition, rapid and recombinant genetic mutations also advance the evolution of HCoV and act as two important steps in the process.
For example, obtaining or losing new protein coding genes has the potential to drastically alter the phenotypes of the virus.
Among SARS-CoV lateral proteins, it is thought that the ORF8 protein plays an important role in human compatibility, because after separating the SARS-CoV-related bat virus, it was found that the virus encodes different ORF8 proteins.
SARS-CoV nucliotide removal feature has been observed in isolated strains at the beginning of the human epidemic.
This deletion divides ORF8 into ORF8a and ORF8b and is thought to be an adaptive mutation that will help change the host.
In addition, SARS-CoV has a recompromissible recombinable history with the ancestors of alpha and gamma CoVs, in which a large number of smaller recombinant areas were identified in RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.
Recombinant locations were identified in nsp9, much of nsp10 and parts of nsp14.
It has also been shown that the MERS-CoV virus, the cause of the pandemic, has experienced recombinations between different ancestors that took place in Saudi Arabia’s single-eating camel.
In addition to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, there has also been a recombinable event in other HCOVs where HCoVs are combined with other animal CoVs in their non-structurical genes.
It should also be cautioned that synthetic choice can contribute to unintended changes in the viral genome, which is more likely to be caused by the host’s immune system.
An example of these effects is the loss of full-length ORF4 in the HCoV-229E prototype breed due to the removal of two nucleotides.
Even thas untouched and complete ORF4 can be seen in viruses associated with HCoV-229 E bats and camels, Alpha-CoV Alpaca with an additional monoliothide will lead to a change in the framework.
The last point, of course, is that the evolution of new HCOVs is also guided by the selective pressure in their tank hosts.
