In addition, high levels of reactive oxygen (ROS), caused by high bat metabolic activity, can both suppress CoV reproducation and re-reading the effect of exorbonoccalase, thus providing selective pressure to produce highly pathogenic virus breeds when it enters a new host.
CoV breeds, which are mostly pathogenic, may lead to recombinance with recombinance, resulting in the acquisition of new proteins or protein properties for host adaptability.
Therefore, it’s no coincidence that three new HCoVs have emerged over the past two decades.
CoVs aren't pathogenic or cause mild symptoms in reservoir hosts such as bats and camels.
These viruses replicate vigorously without facing the host’s strong immune response.
Here there are secrets of why asymptomatic carriers are seen and what causes severe human conditions.
Severe symptoms are mainly due to the intense activity of the immune system and cytokine storm, in which the stronger the immune system’s response, the more severe the damage to the lung will be.
Conversely, in asymptomatic sickness carriers, the immune response is separated from CoV replication.
