The bat, pangulin, or any other mammal is expected to be the SARS-CoV-2 or its parents viruses that are nearly the same in their natural hosts in the future.
Continuous research in this area will clarify the evolution of SARS-CoV-2 in animals and will have important implications for the prevention and control of COVID-19 in humans.
Updating COVID-19 diagnostic criterion is required on "suspicious cases" and "approved cases"
On February 6, 2020, our team published a quick guide to detecting and treating coronavirus in 2019 (2019-nCoV), this guide has been a provider of our experience and is a good reference for fighting this epidemic around the world.
However, 2019 (Covid-19) is a new disease, our awareness and knowledge is gradually increasing based on ongoing research findings and clinical experience; therefore, diagnostic and therapeutic strategies are constantly updated.
In this article, we presented a view on our instructions and provided the latest diagnostic criteria for “suspicious cases” and ‘approved cases’ based on the latest diagnostic and treatment guidelines for COVID-19 (the seventh edition) issued by the National Health Committee of the People’s Republic of China.
In December ۲۰۱۹ ۲۰۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۹۱۱۹۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱۱�
On March 11, 2020, the World Health Organization introduced COVID-19 as a pandemic.
To combat SARS-CoV-2, our team created a quick guide and published online in the Journal of Military Medical Research on February ۲۰۲۲۲۰.
This guide has attracted a lot of attention since its release.
Note that COVID-19 is a new disease, our awareness and knowledge is gradually enhanced based on ongoing research findings and clinical experience; therefore, diagnostic and therapeutic strategies are constantly updated.
For example, the Diagnostic and Treatment Guidelines for COVID-19 issued by the National Health Committee of the People's Republic of China (http://www.nhc.gov.cn/), between January ۱۲۰۲۲۰۲۲۰۲۰۲۲۲۲۲۰۲۲۲۲۲۰۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۱۲۲۲۱۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۲۰۰۲۲۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۰�
Zhou and colleagues have now provided a perspective on our guide, which includes simple scoring proposals based on their clinical experience.
Their paper added new evidence to our guide and also raised a valuable reference for this pandemic around the world.
We acknowledge their outstanding work and express our gratitude.
However, their paper should be updated in accordance with the latest diagnostic and treatment guidelines for COVID-19 (the seventh version) and recent studies.
According to the seventh edition (March ۲۰۲۲۲۰), in order to confirm suspicious cases, one of the characteristics of the history of epidemic diseases must be combined with two of the obvious clinical symptoms to provide a comprehensive analysis, or if it lacks a history of epidemic diseases, it must meet three of the obvious clinical symptoms:
History of epidemiology: (۱) The history of traveling or living in the city of Wuhan and the surrounding areas, or other communities in which cases of COVID-19 have been reported within ۱۴ days prior to the occurrence of symptoms; (۲), a history of communication with patients with SARS-CoV-2 (with a negative-positive acid test); (۳۳).
Clinical symptoms: (۱) fever or respiratory symptoms; (۲) Image characteristics derived from COVID-19 infection; (۳) The total number of white blood cells, which indicates a normal, increased number of reduced lymphocytes in the early stage of initiation of the disease.
Diagnosing confirmed cases should be based on suspected cases along with one of the most pathogenic cases or serium evidence as follows: ۱ ۱) PCR's immediate test to diagnose SARS-CoV-2; (۲) The genome sequence of the entire virus that shows high homogeneity with the new coronavirus (۳) The positiveness of the specific IgM and IgG antibodies of the SARS-CoV-2; or the change of the positive IgG antibody.
We can see that the immediate PCR test in terms of nucleic acid in the respiratory tract or blood samples has been added to the second edition (January 18, 2020) and 3rd (January ۲۲۲۲۲۰).
Pathogenic diagnosis was added to the 4th edition (January 27, 2020) and the 5th (February ۲۲۲۰); and then the brief evidence was added to the seventh edition.
These changes have been based on the continuous work of researchers who have been looking for an optimum nucleic acid detection kit for rapid detection, as well as based on the sampling of the respiratory system, which resulted in greater access to various samples and has supported the positiveness of certain antibodies in the approved criteria.
In addition, there's more evidence to warn us to be discreet with unusual symptoms and patients who lack symptoms.
Hence, the chart of Zhou and colleagues should be updated, as a person who does not have clinical symptoms is categorized as a “low risk.”
The score system must also be approved in practice and with further clinical studies.
To conclude, we hope that more direct evidence will be presented and we ask readers to present their opinions.
To diagnose "suspicious cases" and "approved items", we suggest that physicians follow and complies with the latest guidelines of their countries.
Our team will also regularly guide you to provide up-to-date assistance.
Bangladesh reports five new deaths due to COVID-19, the highest daily.
Yesterday, Bangladesh confirmed five new deaths from COVID-19 on this day.
This was the highest number of daily fatalities due to the virus.
The Bangladesh Institute for Epidemiology, Disease Control and Research (IEDCCR) reported that recorded cases included 114 active cases and 33 recovering cases that would rest at home.
A total of 17 deaths have been recorded.
Dr Meerjady Sabrina Flora, director of IEDCR, said at an online news briefing that the death toll was four men and one was a woman.
According to Dr. Meerjady, two over the age of 60 were between the ages of 50 and 60, and one between the ages of 41 and ‘50.
He also added that two of the victims were from Dhaka.
On March 11, the World Health Organization (WHO) announced the COVID-19 pandemic.
A hospital employee told Anadolu news agency a local news agency that one of the deceased, Jalal Sifur Rahman, a director of the Bengal Anti-Corruption Commission, had been under surveillance at Kuwait Maitree.
On Saturday, Bangladesh’s Minister of Roads and Bridges Obaidul Quader said in an online video statement that public transportation would be closed until next Saturday, more than originally planned.
The public transport shutdown first began on March 26 and was scheduled to end on Saturday, April 4.
The transportation of basic goods – medical goods, fuel and food – continued.
The first case of COVID-19 recorded in Bangladesh on March 8 was two passengers who had returned from Italy, as well as the wife of one of them.
On March 19, the three recovered.
SARS-CoV-2 has infected more than a million people around the world
Data from John’s Hopkins University shows that on Thursday, the total cases of SARS-CoV-CoV-2 have exceeded one million worldwide.
At least 52,000 deaths are attributed to COVID-19, the disease caused by the coronavirus.
With the confirmation of the first case of the coronavirus in Malawi and the first coronavirus-related death in Zambia, this day became a turning point.
On Thursday, North Korea claimed that the country is one of the few coronavirus-devoring countries.
The World Health Organization (WHO) reported 1,051,635 confirmed cases until yesterday that 79,332 of which corresponded to twenty-four hours before the morning of Central European Time (8000 UTC) on April 4.
In the U.S., more than 244,000 cases have been recorded, resulting in at least 5,900 deaths.
CBS NEWS, according to data from Johns Hopkins University, reported that more than 1,000 deaths were recorded in the U.S
Different countries around the world have taken tougher measures to prevent the spread.
Sergegi Sobyanin, Moscow’s mayor, extended the city’s closure until May 1.
President Vladimir Putin announced that across the country, the salary of the Russian people will be paid by April 30 without the need to go to work.
Portugal's parliament voted to extend the national emergency for another 15 days, with 215 votes in favor, ten abstinence and an opposition vote.
Saudi Arabia generalized restrictions in the holy cities of Mecca and Medina to all hours of the day; previously, traffic restrictions were only enforced between 3 p.m. and 6 a.m.
Thailand plans to impose restrictions between 10 p.m. to 4 a.m.
Ohio Governor Mike DeWine has announced that the state has extended home quarantine regulations until May 1.
Australian Stores Reduce the Quota of Buying Toilet Papers Every Shopping
Woolworths and Coles chain stores in Australia, on Saturday and Sunday evening, reduced the restrictions on the purchase of toilet paper in all stores across the country to two and one packages for each purchase, respectively.
The ALDI store on Monday also announced a limit on the purchase of a package.
These restrictions are in the form of messages on inboxes and on the Facebook page of these chain stores.
According to reports, shoppers are reportedly buying and shoving for the need for personal quarantine due to the COVID-19 outbreak.
Also on Wednesday, Woolworths restricted the purchase of toilet paper for home delivery to one package per order.
These changes continued to limit the previous four packages per purchase, which was announced by Woolworths and Coles, respectively, on March 4 and March 5.
In a press release on March 8, Cole's store said that despite a four-pack purchase limit, "the inventory of many stores will end within an eun hour" and called the demand "unprecedented", the ALDI store on its Facebook page on Tuesday described it as "unaspected."
According to Woolworth's spokesperson, sales experienced a "severe increase" last week.
The Costco store in Canberra also narrowed the allowable purchase to two packages last week.
To reduce this shortfall, Coles has ordered larger packages to suppliers and has increased the frequency of delivery of goods to the stores, Woolworths has ordered more inventory. ALDI has made the inventory of one of Wednesday's special sales plans available earlier.
Russell Zimmerman, executive director of the Australian Retailers Association, said retailers are trying to increase their inventory, but the local council’s restrictions on the timing of delivery trucks have made it difficult.
He said he expects production costs to rise as suppliers are trying to respond to demand and special sales to be slow.
On Tuesday, the ALDI store announced that some stores were unable to hold Wednesday's special sale due to early storage.
Dr. Gary Mortimer, a retail expert from Queensland University of Technology, said in a report at News.com.au that stores fill their warehouse inventory every night.
He added that the bulkiness of toilet paper leads to that the inventory of the stores in terms of a small number, and when the product is finished, it drains a lot of space on the shelves and instills the feeling of scarcity.
Russell Zimmerman told ABC News that Coles and Woolworths believe that if there are a lot of products on the shelf, if products such as toilet paper and disinfectants are available in high numbers and can be purchased, your fear will probably be minimized.
Who Gives a Crap, a recycled toile toothpaper, announced last Wednesday that their inventory was over.
According to News.com.au, Kimberly-Clark, which manufactures Kleenex toilet paper, and Solaris Paper, which manufactures Sorbent toilet paper, emphasized that they operate 24/7 to meet demand.
Domain.com, one active real estate sites, reported that when fewer auctions were held due to shoppers on a long weekend working day holiday, some real estate vendors donated free toilet paper to the first bidders of auctions in Melbourne.
Thursday's NT-News version, one of Darwin's newspapers, had an eight-leaf specialty to be cut and used as toilet paper.
According to ABC Australia on March 3, the stores were originally reluctant to impose restrictions and announced that they had no plans to impose restrictions on purchases.
Russell Zimmerman added that there is a high demand for other goods, including masks, disinfectants, dry goods, lucid and toilet soap and flour.
Outside of Australia similarly, Ocado's online online supermarket, Sunday evening saw restrictions on the purchase of Andres toilet paper with the amount of two packs of 12 rollies.
The World Health Organization (WHO) announces COVID-19 pandemic
On Wednesday, the World Health Organization (WHO) announced the outbreak of COVID-19, caused by the SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus.
Although the word ‘pandemia’ refers only to the extent of the spread of a disease, not how dangerous it is, the WHO stressed the need for governments to take countermeasures:
All countries can still change the duration of this pandemic.
WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus said that if countries diagnose, tested, treated, quarantined and tracked and prepared their people.
We are deeply concerned about both the alarming level of the outbreak and its severity, as well as the alarming level of appropriate action.
According to Dr. Tom Frieden, a former head of the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the pandemic is “unprecedented.”
In a statement released by CNN in February, he said: ‘Expite the flu, no other respiratory virus has been tracked from the emergence to a sustained global outbreak.
Ghebreyesus said in a similar statement: “We’ve never seen a pandemic caused by a coronavirus before.”
"And we've never seen a pandemic that's at the same time manageable."
The new situation as the pandemic came after the WHO's decision in January to announce the spread of the disease as a public health emergency with international concern.
Dr Anthony Fauci, director of the U.S. National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, said: "The front-line situation is deteriorating."
The Associated Press reported that as of Thursday, there were at least 126,000 cases of COVID-19 around the world, resulting in more than 4,600 deaths.
The 20-2019 coronavirus pandemic is a progressive pandemic of 2019 (Covid-19), which is caused by Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-COV-2).
The outbreak was detected in December 2019 in Wuhan, China, on January 30, 20, 2020, it was declared a public health emergency with international concern and was recognized as a pandemic on March 11, 2020.
As of April 10, 2020, about 1.61 million cases of COVID-19 have been reported in 210 countries and territories, resulting in about 97,000 deaths.
About 364,000 people have recovered.
The mortality rate in China is estimated to be about 4%, while globally ranging from 0.08% in New Zealand to 13.04% in Algeria.
Common symptoms include fever, cough, and shortness of breath.
Complications can include pneumonia and acute respiratory distress syndrome.
The time interval from the arrival of the virus to the initiation of symptoms is usually about five days, but it can vary between two and fourteen days.
There is no no known vaccine or any special treatment.
Initial treatment is the treatment of symptoms and supportive therapy. Recommended preventive measures include washing your hands, covering the mouth during a cough, getting away from other people, and personal monitoring and quarantining for people who are suspected of developing.
Officials from different countries of the world have reacted to the virus by imposing travel restrictions, quarantine, military rule, workplace risk control and closure of various places.
This pandemic has led to severe socio-economic disruptions, the postponement or abolition of sporting, religious, political and cultural events, and a widespread lack of resources that exacerbated with nervous shopping.
Schools and universities have been closed in 193 countries either at the national level or locally, affecting 99.4 percent of the world’s students.
Misinformation about the virus has spread online, and there have been cases of xenophobia and discrimination against the people of Chinese and other people who have the race and appearance of the East and Southeast Asia, as well as people from other areas with the spread of the virus.
Due to travel restrictions and the closure of heavy industries, air pollution and carbon emissions have been reduced.
Health officials in Wuhan, China (Capital of Hubei Province) reported a group of undisclosed-orientated pneumonia cases on December 31, 2019, and in early January 2020, an investigation was launched.
These mainly had links to Huana's wholesale seafood market, which is why it is thought that the virus has an animal origin.
The virus that caused the outbreak is known as SARS-CoV-2, a virus that has recently been discovered and is closely related to bat coronavirus, Pangolin and SARS-CoV coronavirus. It was later revealed that the first known person with symptoms was sick on December 1, 2019, and this person did not have a visible connection to the next group related to the seafood market.
It turned out that two-thirds of them had anything to do with this market, among the initials reported in December 2019.
On March 13, 2020, an unconfirmed report from the South China Morning Post claimed that a case had been tracked on November 17, 2019, a 55-year-old man from Hubei province who could be the first. On February 26, 2020, WHO reported that, despite the decline in cases in China, according to reports, there has been a sudden increase in cases in Italy, Iran and South Korea, and for the first time the number of new cases outside of China has surpassed the number of new cases inside China.
The number of cases may be reported significantly lower than in reality, especially among people with milder symptoms.
As of February 26, a relatively small number of cases were reported among young people, with 19-year-old and less of only 2.4% of cases around the world around the world.
Items mean the number of people who have been tested for COVID-19 and their testing is based on official positive protocols.
By March 23, no country has tested more than 3% of its population, and many countries, such as Italy, the Netherlands, Spain, and Switzerland, have adopted an official policy not to test people with only mild symptoms.
A study published on March 16 found that up to 86% of cases of COVID-19 have not been identified until January 23, and these unregistered cases were a source of 79%.
A statistical analysis published on March 30 estimated that the number of cases in Italy was significantly higher than reported.
Initial estimates of base contagion number (R0) for COVID-19 were between 1.4 and 2.4.
A study published by the U.S. Centers for Disease Prevention and Control has concluded that the number could be 5.7.
Most people with COVID-19 improve.
For those who don’t recover, the time interval between symptoms and death was between 6 and 41 days, and the most common duration has been 14 days.
By April 10, 2020, approximately 97,000 deaths have been attributed to COVID-19.
In China, as of February 5, there have been about an 80% of deaths in people over the age of 60, and 70% of them have had underlying diseases, including cardiovascular disease and diabetes.
The actual number of COVID-19-related deaths can be much higher, as these statistics may not include people who have died without testing—for example at home, in nursing centers, etc.
Incomplete data from Italy showed that the number of untested deaths during the pandemic is about 4-4 times higher than the official death toll.
A spokesman for the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) stated, "We know that [stated death rates] are lower than the actual rate," a spokeswoman for the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) said. "Such actions often take place in pandemics, including in the H1N1 swine flu pandemic in 2009.
The first death toll occurred outside of mainland China on February 1 in the Philippine and the first case of death outside of Asia on February 14 in France.
By February 28, more than a dozen deaths were recorded in Iran, South Korea and Italy.
As of March 13, more than forty countries and government territories on all continents except Antarctica were reported deaths. Various measurement scales are commonly used to measure the mortality rate.
These numbers vary according to the region and over time and are influenced by the amount of tests, the quality of the health system, the treatment options, the time spent on the initial outbreak, and demographic characteristics such as age, gender, and the overall state of health. The mortality rate is achieved by dividing the number of deaths over the total cases detected over a period of time.
According to Johns Hopkins University statistics, the global mortality rate is 6.0% (97,039/1,617,204).
This number is different in each region.
In China, the estimated death rate fell from 17.3% (for those whose symptoms started from January 10, 2020) to 0.7% (for those whose symptoms began after February 1, 2020).
These statistics don’t have a time limit, and a specific population is following from the infection to solving the case.
A number of academics have tried to calculate these numbers for specific populations.
The University of Oxford's Evidence-Based Medical Center estimates that the rate of fatalities for the pandemic is generally between 0.1% and 0.39%.
Estimating the maximum of this range is consistent with the results obtained from the first randomized COVID-19 experiment in Germany, as well as a statistical study that analyzes the impact of the test on the CFR estimate.
The WHO emphasizes that the pandemic is controllable.
In the case of the peak and the final duration of this outbreak, it cannot be definitively commented and may vary based on location.
Maciej Boni of Penn State University stated: “Masuring infectious diseases usually stabilize if they do not take an attempt to control them and begin to decline when the disease exits available hosts.
But for now, it’s impossible to make any reasonable predictions about when it’s going to happen.
Zhong Nanshan, China’s government’s chief medical adviser, argued that if all countries are mobilized to take the World Health Organization’s recommendations to tackle the spread of the virus, ‘the disease could end by June.’
Adam Kucharski of London’s School of Tropical Health and Medicine announced on March 17 that SARS-CoV-2 “probably will travel for one or two years.”
According to research conducted by Neil Ferguson at the Royal College, physical distancing and other measures “until a vaccine (probably it lasts 18 months or more) will be necessary.”
William Schaffner of Vanderbilt University said, “I think it’s less likely that the coronavirus will disappear completely—because it’s easily transmissible” and “it may turn into a seasonal disease that returns every year.”
The intensity of this return will depend on collective impunity and the intensity of the mutation.
The symptoms of COVID-19 can be relatively non-exclusive, and affected people may be symptom-free.
Two of the most common symptoms are fever (88%) and dry cough (68%).
Symptoms that are less prevalent include fatigue, breast phlegm production, loss of olfactory sense, shortness of breath, muscle and joint pain, sore throat, headache, chills, vomiting, blood, diarrhea and cyanosis.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) has published a list of emergency symptoms: difficulty breathing, persistent pain or pressure in the chest, sudden distress, difficulty waking up, bruising of the face and lips; If these symptoms occur, it is recommended to take immediate medical attention.
Some people may lack symptoms, do not have any clinical symptoms, but the results of the test will confirm their infection, so researchers have recommended that people who have had close contact with the affected people should be carefully monitored and tested to prevent them from being infected.
Estimates made by China varied from the rates of people without symptoms ranging from very small amounts to 44%.
The typical incubation period (time between a person's infection and the appearance of symptoms) varies from one to 14 days; in the most common case, this period is five days. As an example of uncertainty, the stats of a part of people with COVID-19 who lose their sense of smal was initially 30% but later reduced to 15%.
Some details about how the disease spreads are still being determined.
It is thought that the disease mainly spreads through a close connection and by small particles produced by cough, sneezing, or talking; and close communication means a distance between 1 and 2 meters (3 to 6 feet).
Studies have shown that non-coated coughing can spread small particles between 4.5 meters (15 feet) to 8.2 meters (27 feet).
Some studies have suggested that the virus can also be transmitted by small particles produced by speaking and staying in the air for a longer time. Although the virus is not generally airborne, fine respiratory particles can be produced by breathing, including speech.
These tiny particles can land in the mouth or nose of nearby people or be transported to their lungs through breathing.
Some medical procedures, such as respiratory intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), can result in spraying respiratory secretions in the air and as a result of an airborne outbreak.
It is also transmitted when a person touches an infected surface, including the skin and then touches his eyes, nose, or mouth.
While there are concerns about the possibility of transmitting the disease through feces, it’s thought to be a low risk.
The Chinese government has ruled out the possibility of SARS-CoV-2 fecal transfusion. Although it is also possible to transmit the disease prior to the appearance of the symptoms and subsequent stages of the disease, the highest rate of contagion of the virus in the first three days is the appearance of symptoms.
People who have tested positive for the disease up to three days before the appearance of symptoms indicate that it is possible to transmit the disease prior to noticeable symptoms.
There are few reports of unmarked cases that have been approved by the laboratory, but symptom-free transmission has been detected by some countries during communication tracking investigations.
The European Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC) stated that while the rapid spread of the disease is not fully known, usually an infected person can infect two or three other people.
In particular, it has been determined that the virus will survive for up to three days on plastic surfaces (polypropylene) and stainless steel for up to three days on the cardboard for up to four hours.
However, these amounts can change depending on the humidity and temperature. So far, there have been cases of the positive test results of pets and other animals for COVID-19.
There is no evidence that the animals can transmit the virus to humans, yet British authorities recommend that people wash their hands after contact with the animals, such as if they come into contact with other surfaces that the infected people may have touched.
Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is a new virus that was first detected from three people with pneumonia who were in contact with a group of people with acute respiratory disease in Wuhan.
All the characteristics of the new SARS-CoV-2 virus, occurs in the related coronavirus in nature. Outside of the human body, the virus is killed by homemade soap that destroys its protective cover. SARS-CoV-2 has a close relationship with the original SARS-CoV.
It is believed that the virus has an animal origin.
A genetic analysis has shown that the coronavirus is genetically derived from beta-corona virus, a subspecies of sarbeco virus (generation B), along with two genes derived from bats.
At the overall genome level, the virus corresponded to 96 with other BatCov RaTG13.
In February 2020, Chinese researchers found that there was only one aminoidoids in certain parts of their genome sequence.
Compromising the full genome to date has concluded that up to 92% of the genetic material is shared between Pangolin and SARS-CoV-2, which is not enough to prove that the pangulins are intermediary.
Infected by the virus can be temporarily detected through symptoms, however the final verification is done through the serial reaction of reverse transcription polymerase (rRT-PCR) of infected secretions or CT imaging.
A study that compares PCR to CT in Wuhan has suggested that CT is significantly more sensitive than PCR and at the same time less proprietary because many features of CT imaging overlap with other inflammatory pulmonary diseases and disease processes.
As of March 2020, the U.S. College of Radiology recommended that "CT should not be used as a screening or initial test to diagnose COVID-19."
The World Health Organization (WHO) has released several RNA testing protocols for SARS-CoV-2, the first of which was released on January 17.
This experiment uses the reaction of the reverse transcription polymerase (rRT-PCR).
This test can be done on respiratory and blood samples.
The results of this test are usually determined between a few hours and a few days.
Usually, this test is done with a nasopharnex swab, however, swab throat can also be used. A number of laboratories and companies are working on serological tests that detect antibodies.
As of April 6, 2020, none of these methods have been accurate enough to be approved for widespread use.
In the United States, a serological experiment developed by Cellex for emergency use has only been approved by some accredited laboratories.
Characteristic characteristics of imaging in radiography and computer tomography (CT) of people who have symptoms include asymmetric peripheral grandglas views and the lack of peripheral evaluation.
The Italian Radiological Society is compilating an international online database of imaging findings for confirmed cases with the disease.
Non-approved imaging by PCR, due to overlapping with other infections, such as adenovirus, cannot be used specifically to detect COVID-19.
A large study in China compared chest CT results to PCR and showed that although imaging is less specifically usable to diagnose the disease, it is faster and more sensitive and suggested that it be used as a screening tool in areas with the epidemic.
Artificial intelligence-based twist neural networks have been developed to identify the imaging features of the virus both by radiography and by CT.
Some ways to prevent the transmission of this disease include proper personal hygiene, washing your hands, not touching your eyes, nose or mouth sitting and holding a tissue while coughing or sneezing and throwing a handkerchief directly in the trash.
Those who already have this disease are advised to wear surgical masks in public settings.
Observance of physical distancing measures is also recommended to prevent the transmission of the disease. Many governments have banned or recommended non-essential travel to countries or areas where the disease is common.
However, the virus has reached an outbreak in many parts of the world.
This means that the virus is spreading within communities, and some people don't know where or how they've been infected. People who provide health care for potential patients are advised to take standard precautions, call-to-casceptive precautions, and use eye guards.
The use of mobile location information by governments for this purpose has raised privacy concerns, and Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations have issued a statement to limit this type of surveillance.
A variety of apps have been created or proposed to be used voluntarily, and by April 7, 2020, more than a dozen expert groups are working on solutions that do not violate privacy, such as the use of Bluetooth to record the user’s proximity to other mobile phones.
Then, users will receive a message if they are in close contact with a person with COVID-19. Some misconceptions about how to prevent the disease are spreading; for example, washing the nose or grumbling with a mouthwash are not effective ways.
Although many organizations are struggling to develop the vaccine, there is still no vaccine for COVID-19.
In order to prevent the spread of the disease, hand washing is recommended.
The CDC recommends that people frequently wash their hands with soap and water for at least twenty seconds, especially after the toilet or when the hands are clearly dirty before eating; after emptying the nose, coughing or sneezing.
It’s because the virus is killed outside the human boy, which removes its protective bubble.
The CDC also recommends using alcohol-based sanitizers with a minimum volume of 60% alcohol when soap and water are not readily available.
The World Health Organization advises people to refrain from touching their eyes, noses, and mouths with sitting hands.
Contamination is possible in different ways (one-minute exposure of disinfectants for stainless steel surface), including 71-62% ethanol, 100-50% ispropanol, 0.1% sodium hypochlorite, 0.5% hydrogen peroxide and 7.5-0.2.2% betadine.
Other methods, including benzalconium chloride and chlerhexidine gluconate, are less effective.
The CDC recommends that if there issuspected or approved case of COVID in a building such as an office or daily care center, all departments such as offices, toilets, shared areas, shared electronic devices such as tablets, touch screens, keyboards, remote controls, and ATMs used by sick people should be disinfected.
Hygienic organizations have recommended that when people cough or sneeze, cover their mouth and nose with their elbows or napkins and throw away the handkerchief immediately.
Surgical masks are recommended for people who are likely to be infected because wearing a mask can reduce the volume and spacing of particles that are dispersed when talking, sneezing, or coughing.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has issued guidelines on when and how to wear a mask.
According to Stephen Griffin, virologist at the University of Leeds, "wearing a mask can reduce people's desire to touch their faces, which is one of the important sources of infection if they don't maintain proper hand hygiene."
The World Health Organization recommends wearing masks by healthy people only if they are at a high risk, such as those who take care of people with COVID-19, however, they also confirm that wearing masks will help people avoid touching their faces.
Several countries have started to applaude people wearing face masks.
In the United States, the CDC recommends using non-medical masks that are made of fabric. China has specifically recommended the use of disposable medical masks by healthy people in the community, especially when they have a close relationship with other people (3 feet) or less).
Hong Kong has recommended that surgical masks be used when using public transportation or stopping in crowded places.
Thailand’s health authorities encourage people to make masks at a home and wash them on a daily basis.
The Czech-Slovak Republic has banned people from going out and gathering without wearing masks or covering the mouth and nose.
On March 16, Vietnam called on all people to wear masks when they went to public places in order to protect themselves and others.
The Australian government has made it mandatory to wear masks for those who go to grocery stores.
Israel has called on all its residents to wear masks when they are in the community.
Since mid-March, which has been producing ten million masks every day since mid-March, Taiwan has required inter-urban trains and buses to wear masks since April 1.
Panama has made it mandatory to wear a mask when they go out and has advised those who can’t afford to wear a homemade mask.
Masks are also widely used in Japan, South Korea, Malaysia and Singapore.
Social distancing (also known as physical distancing) involves pollution-control measures aimed at slowing the outbreak by minimizing a close relationship between people.
These include quarantine; travel restrictions; closing schools and workplaces, stadiums, theaters and shopping malls.
By staying at home, restricting your trip, refusing to attend crowded areas, greetings without contact and physical distance from others can implement these methods.
Currently, many governments in areas where the disease is spreading have made social distancing mandatory or recommended it.
The maximum recommended number of people gathered by U.S. government institutions and health agencies dropped quickly from 250 people (if there is no COVID-19 outbreak in the region) to 50 and later to 10.
On March 22, 2020, Germany banned the accumulation of more than two people. Elderly people and those with underlying diseases such as diabetes, heart disease, respiratory disease, blood pressure and immunodeficiency face a higher risk of developing and severe complications, and the CDC has advised these individuals to stay at home as much as possible in the areas where the disease has spread in the community.
The use of the word "social distancing", instead of encouraging people to maintain communication using alternative methods, implies that people should enter complete social isolation. Some officials issued guidelines on sexual health for use during this pandemic.
These guidelines included recommendations about having sex only with the person you live with, a person who is not infected with the virus, or does not have symptoms of the virus.
For people who have been diagnosed with COVID-19 and those who are suspected to be infected, personal quarantine is recommended at home.
Health organizations have identified minor guidelines for proper personal quarantine. Many governments have forcibled or recommended personal quarantine for all people living in areas affected by the virus.
The strictest personal quarantine guidelines have been issued to those in high-risk groups.
To those who may have been in contact with people infected with COVID-19 and those who have recently traveled to the country or region where the disease has spread widely, it has been recommended to observe personal quarantine for 14 days from the date of their last contact.
Prevalence control strategies of a disease include inhibition, suppression and reduction.
Inhibition is carried in the early stages of the outbreak with the aim of tracking and separating affected people, as well as the introduction of other measures to control the disease and vaccination to stop the spread of the disease to the rest of the population.
‘When it’s no longer possible to inhibit the spread of the disease, efforts are shifted to a reduction strategy: measures are taken to reduce the prevalence and reduce its effects on the health system and society.
A combination of containment and reduction measures can be implemented simultaneously.
Suppression requires more severe measures to reverse the pandemic by reducing the base contagion number to less than 1. The part of managing the outbreak of an infectious disease is an attempt to reduce the peak of the epidemic, known as the smoothing of the pandemic curve.
This reduces the risk of excessive pressure on health services and provides more time to develop vaccines and treatments.
Non-pharmaceous interventions that can help manage the spread of the disease include personal preventive measures, including hand hygiene, wearing masks, personal quarantine; social measures aimed at physical distancing, including school closures and cancellation of events in which there is a large number of people; social participation to encourage admission and adherence to these interventions; as well as environmental measures such as cleaning levels.
Other countries also implemented a series of measures aimed at restricting the spread of the virus.
Soute South Korea carried out group screening and local quarantine and issued warnings about the displacement of infected people.
Singaported financial support for people who quarantined themselves and imposed heavy fines for those who didn’t.
Taiwan increased the production of masks and imposed penalties for hoarding of medical supplies. Simulations carried out for the United Kingdom and the U.S. show that the strategy of slowing down the spread of the pandemic and not to stop it and the repression (revoking the growth of the pandemic) are facing great challenges.
Optimal reduction policies can reduce peak health demand to 2/3 and the death toll by halve, but will still kill hundreds of thousands of people and put too much pressure on the health system.
Suppression can be a better strategy, but as long as the virus circulates in the human population (or until a vaccine is made, if this happens sooner), it must be maintained, because if the measures are released, the contagious rate will quickly return to the previous amount.
Long-term intervention to resupprove this pandemic creates social and economic costs.
There is no confirmed anti-virus drug for COVID-19, although efforts are underway to make it, including existing drug testing.
The use of medications without the need for a cold prescription, drinking fluids and rest can help reduce symptoms.
Depending on the severity, oxygen therapy may be needed, intravenous injections of fluids, and respiratory assistance devices.
The use of steroids can make things worse.
Several compounds that have already been approved for the treatment of other viral diseases are being investigated to be used if they are useful for the treatment of COVID-19.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has also announced “multite traditional and home remedies” that can help relieve SARS-CoV-19 symptoms.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has described the increasing capacity and adaptation of the health system to the needs of COVID-19 patients as one of the basic measures to deal with the outbreak.
ECDC and the World Health Organization's European regional office have issued guidelines for hospitals and primary health care services to transfer resources to various levels, including the focus of laboratory services on COVID-19 testing, cancellation of non-necessary surgeries if possible, separating and isolated patients with COVID-19, and increasing intensive care capacity by training personnel and increasing the number of ventilators and beds available.
There are different opinions about where the origin of the first (so-called zero-emission) was.
The first case of the new coronavirus may go back to December 1, 2019 in Wuhan, Hubei, China.
Over the course of a month, the number of cases infected with the coronavirus in Hubei gradually increased.
Most of these people were associated with Huana's seafood wholesale market, which also sold living animals, and one of the theories is that the origin of this virus was one of these animals; i.e., in other words, the virus has an animal origin. A group of people with pneumonia were seen for an unknown cause on December 26 and were treated by Dr. Zhang Jixian at Hubei Provincial Hospital, which on December 27 informed Wu Jianghanhan CDC.
On December 30, a group of doctors at Wuhan Central Hospital warned their co-workers about a ‘SARS-like corona virus.’
The police advised eight of these doctors, including Li Wenliang, to refrain from spreading false rumors, and another doctor, Ai Fen, was punished by his superiors for providing the warning.
Later, the Wuhan City Health Commission issued a public announcement on December 31, informing the World Health Organization.
Many cases of pneumonia were reported to Wuhan health authorities with an unknown cause, prompting them to launch an investigation in early January. In the first stages of the outbreak, the number of cases doubled almost every seven-and-a-a-a-a-half days.
In early and mid-January 2020, the virus was transported to other Chinese provinces with the help of Chinese New Year-of-year trips, given that Wuhan is one of the main transport hubs and rail exchange hubs.
On January 20, 2020, China reported about 140 new cases, including one in Beijing and one in Shenzhen, within a day.
Subsequent official data shows that 6,174 people had symptoms until January 20, 2020. As of March 26, the United States surpassed China and Italy with the highest number of confirmed cases in the world. As of April 9, 2020, more than 1.31 million cases were reported worldwide; more than 97,000 people died and 36,4,000 recovered.
About 200 countries and state territory have had at least one case.
Due to this pandemic in Europe, many countries in the Schengen area have restricted free displacement and started border controls.
National reactions included measures to contain the outbreak, including quarantine (known as orders to stay at home, order to take shelter or home quarantine) and martial law. As of April, about 300 million in the U.S., about $300 million, or about 90% of the population were subjected to some kind of home quarantine, in the Philippines, more than 50 millions, in South Korea about 59 million, and in India 1.3 billion were placed in home quarantine.
On March 26, 1.7 billion people around the world fell into quarantine, up to 2.6 billion, about one-third of the world’s population.
The first confirmed case of COVID-19 goes back to December 1 in Wuhan; an unconfirmed report says the first case was on November 17.
On December 26, Dr. Zhang Jixian observed a group of people with pneumonia for an unknown cause, according to December 27, the hospital notified Wuhan Jianghan CDC.
Initial genetic testing on patient samples on December 27, 2019 showed the presence of the SARS-like coronavirus.
On December 31, a public announcement was published by the Wuhan City Health Commission.
The World Health Organization (WHO) was notified on the same day.
With the announcement, police warned Wuhan doctors about a "rumour spread" about the spread of the disease.
China’s National Health Commission initially asserted that there was no “cleright evidence” of human-to-human transmission.
In late January, the Chinese government launched a fundamental campaign titled “People’s War” to contain the spread of the virus, which was later described by Xi Jinping, Secretary-General of the Chinese Communist Party.
In what is referred to as the “largest quarantine in human history,” on January 23, a hygienic belt was determined to stop entering and exiting Wuhan, extending a total to 15 cities from Hubei province, affecting a total of about 57 million.
The use of a private vehicle in the city was prohibited.
Chinese New Year celebrations (January 25) were canceled in many places.
Officials also announced that a provisional hospital will be built in the name of Huoshenshan Hospital, which was completed within 10 days.
Subsequently, another hospital called Leishenshan Hospital was built to handle more patients.
In addition to the newly built hospitals, China turned 14 other buildings in Wuhan, including convention centers and stadiums, into temporary hospitals. On January 26, the government launched other measures to contain the COVID-19 outbreak, including health announcements for travelers and an increase in spring-festival holidays.
Universities and schools have also been closed across the country.
Hong Kong and Macau have begun measures, especially in schools and universities.
Measures of teleworking began in several regions of China.
Travel restrictions have been imposed to enter and get out of Hubei.
Public transport was reformed and museums across China were temporarily closed.
Public traffic control was imposed in many cities and it is estimated that about 760 million people (more than halves of the population) faced some kind of restriction to go out. In March, after the outbreak entered the global stage, Chinese authorities took stringent measures to prevent the "entry" of the virus from other countries.
For example, Beijing imposed a mandatory 14-day quarantine for all international travellers entering the city. On March 23, only one in mainland China was transferred into the country within five days earlier, which happened by a traveler returning from Istanbul to Guangzhou.
On March 24, 2020, Chinese Prime Minister Li Keqiang reported that the spread of the disease within the country was taken substantially and the spread of the disease has been controlled in China.
On the same day, two months after the quarantine, travel restrictions in Hubei were reduced, except for the city of Wuhan. The Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced on March 26, 2020, that entry into the country would be suspended for visa holders or residence permits from March 28 onwards and did not provide any details about when the implementation of this policy would end.
Those who want to enter China will have to apply for a visa at Chinese embassies or consulates.
On March 30, the Chinese government urged businesses and factories to reopen and presented monetary stimulus packages for companies. The State Council announced that on April 4, which coincides with the Day of Pilgrimage of Graves, it would be public mourning and will begin with three minutes of silence across the country at 10:00.
On January 20, 2020, it was confirmed that COVID-19 had been transferred to South Korea through China.
On February 20, the country’s health department announced a significant increase in confirmed cases, largely linked to a gathering in Daegu that belonged to a new religious movement called the Church of Christ.
Shincheonji's disciples, who came to Daegu from Wuhan, were believed to be the source of this outbreak.
As of February 22, of the 9336 followers of this church, the presence of symptoms in 1261 people, about 13%, was reported. South Korea announced the highest level of warning on February 23.
On February 28, more than 2000 confirmed cases were reported in Korea, up to 3,1515 on February 29.
After the three soldiers tested positive, all South Korean military bases quarantined.
The airline's program was also affected and thus changed. South Korea introduced a program to screen the population and isolate people, the world's largest and most organized program to screen the population and isolate all infected people, as well as tracking and quarantinarization of people who have been in contact with.
Screening methods include mandatory reporting of symptoms by the person themselves for international newcomers through the mobile application, test centers that can be entered by car, and the results will be determined on the next day, and an increase in testing capacity to 20,000 people per day.
South Korea's program, despite not quarantining the entire city, is considered one of the successful programs in controlling the outbreak. South Korean society was initially polarized against President Moon Jae-in's reaction to the crisis.
Many Koreans signed lawsuits to impeach the Moon for what they called the bad governance of the government, while others applauded his reaction.
On March 23, South Korea was reported to have the lowest one-da-day infection in the past four weeks.
It was reported on March 29 that from April 1, all travellers arriving from abroad will be quarantined for two weeks.
According to media reports on April 1, South Korea received calls for help in testing the virus on behalf of 121 different countries.
Iran reported the first confirmed case of SARS-CoV-2 on February 19 in Qom, where two people later died on the same day, according to the Minister of Health and Medical Education.
Initial measures announced by the government included the abolition of concerts and other cultural, sporting events, Friday prayers and the closure of universities, higher education institutions and schools.
Iran allocated five trillion rials to the fight against the virus.
President Hassan Rouhani said on February 26, 2020, that there is no plan to quarantine areas affected by the outbreak and that only people will be quarantined.
In March, plans were announced to limit travel between cities, but heavy traffic between cities continued on the eve of the Nowruz Iranian New Year celebration.
Shia shrines in Qom remained open to pilgrims until March 16, 2020. In February, Iran became the epicenter of the virus after China.
Among the claims of concealing the actual prevalence in Iran by February 28, the trace of cases in more than 10 countries showed that their origins were related to Iran, indicating that the actual rate of the outbreak was probably more than 388 cases that the Iranian government had reported to that date.
The Iranian parliament was closed with a positive test of 23 of the 290 MPs on March 3, the Iranian parliament was closed.
On March 12, the Human Rights Watch appealed to the Iranian Prisons Administration to temporarily release human rights defenders who had been detained for peaceful dissent, as well as all eligible prisoners.
The watch said there was a higher risk of the spread of the virus at closed sites, such as detention centers that lack adequate medical care.
On March 15, the Iranian government reported 100 deaths in just a single day, the country’s highest since the outbreak began.
By March 17, at least 12 current or former Iranian politicians or government officials had died from the disease.
As of March 23, Iran experienced 50 new cases every hour and every 10 minutes of new death due to the coronavirus.
According to a World Health Organization official, cases in Iran are likely to be five times the statistics reported.
It is also thought that U.S. sanctions against Iran may have negatively impacted the country’s financial ability to deal with the viral outbreak.
The United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights has called for economic sanctions to be eased for the countries most affected by the pandemic, including Iran.
On January 31st, when the results of two Chinese tourists tested positive in Roma for SARS-CoV-2, the outbreak in Italy was confirmed.
The number of cases began a sharp increase, prompting the Italian government to suspend all flights of China’s origin or destination and declared a state of emergency.
On February 22, the Board of Ministers announced a new legal order to contain the spread of the disease, including quarantine of more than 50,000 people from 11 different cities in northern Italy. On February 22, the Council of Ministers announced a new legal order to contain the outbreak, which included quarantining more than 50,000 people from 11 different cities in northern Italy.
Deputy Prime Minister Giuseppe Conte said: "In areas where the disease is common, it will not be possible to enter and exit.
"On March 4, when the death toll reaches 100 people, the Italian government ordered all schools and universities across the country to be completely closed.
All major sporting events including Serie A football matches should have been held behind closed doors until April, but on March 9, all sporting events were completely suspended for at least one month.
On March 11, Prime Minister Conté ordered a halt to almost all commercial activities except supermarkets and pharmacies. On March 6, the Faculty of Anesthesia, Anesthesia, Resuscitation, and Intensive Intensive Care (SIAARTI) published recommendations on medical ethics on triage protocols that may be used.
On March 19, after a 3,405 deaths due to the pandemic, Italy surpassed China as the world's highest number of coronavirus-related deaths.
On March 22, it was reported that Russia had sent nine military aircraft containing medical equipment to Italy.
As of April 5, 128,948 items were approved, 15,887 were killed and 21,815 recovered in Italy, most of which were in Lombardy.
A report from CNN found that a combination of two factors in a large population of older adults and the inability to test all those infected with the virus to this day can be effective at a high mortality rate.
Britain’s response to the virus was one of the simplest reactions among countries affected by the disease, and by March 18, 2020, the British government did not impose any social distancing or mass quarantine measures for its citizens.
As a result, the U.S. government was faced with criticism about the lack of proper speed and intensity in response to concerns that society faced. On March 16, Prime Minister Boris Johnson advised in a statement to revoke all unnecessary social trips and communications, and advised people to work from home if possible and to refrain from attending places such as pubs, restaurants and theaters.
On March 20, the government announced that all entertainment centers, such as pubs and gyms, should be closed as soon as possible and pledged to pay up to £2,2,500 a month to prevent unemployment during the crisis. On March 23, the prime minister announced tougher measures on social distancing, prohibiting gatherings of more than two people, taking restrictions on travel and outdoor activities.
Contrary to previous actions, these restrictions were enforceable by the police by issuing fines and dispersing gatherings.
Most businesses were ordered to be shut down, with the exception of businesses deemed “necessary” including supermarkets, pharmacies, banks, hardware stores, gas stations and repair shops.
On January 20, the first well-known case of COVID-19 was confirmed in the north-west Pacific district of Washington state, a man who had returned from Wuhan on January 15.
The White House's Coronavirus Task Force was formed on January 29.
On January 31, the Trump administration declared a public health emergency and imposed restrictions on travelers arriving from China.
On January 28, 2020, the Centers for Disease Control – the U.S. government’s main public health institution – announced that it had built its own test kit.
Despite this, the U.S. had a slow down in people’s experiments, which obscured the actual rate of the outbreak at the time.
Defective kits produced by the federal government, disapproval of non-governmental test kits (produced by universities, companies and hospitals) by the federal government until the end of February and restrictive criteria for qualifying for testing until early March (so requiring a doctor’s prescription), caused a disruption to people’s testing process in February.
The Washington Post reported that by February 27, less than 4,000 tests have been conducted in the United States.
The Atlantic reported that less than 14,000 tests had been carried out by March 13.
On March 22, Associated Press reported: "A lot of people who have had a doctor's prescription have been waiting hours or days to get tested. "After on February 29, the first death in the United States was reported in Washington, Governor Jay Inslee declared a state of emergency, a move that also happened very soon in other states.
On March 3, schools in the Seattle region canceled their classrooms, and schools across the country were closed until mid-March. On March 6, 2020, a group ofemiologists from Imperial College of London made predictions about the impact of the new coronavirus on the United States.
On the same day, President Trump signed a cover-apprucised and anti-corona-requisition law, which provided $8.3 billion in emergency funding for federal agencies to deal with the outbreak.
The companies imposed restrictions on employee travel, canceled conferences and urged employees to work from home.
On March 11, Trump imposed travel restrictions for most European countries except the United Kingdom for 30 days, which would take effect on March 13.
The next day, he generalized these restrictions to England and Ireland.
On March 13, he declared a national state of emergency, prompting federal funds to be available to deal with the crisis.
Since March 15, many businesses across the U.S. have been shutting down in an effort to reduce the spread of the virus, or have eased their working hours.
By March 17, the pandemic was confirmed in all 50 states of the country and the District of Columbia. On March 23, it was reported that New York City had 10,700 cases of coronavirus, which alone exceeded everything in South Korea.
On March 25, the governor said social distancing appeared to have become useful because the rate of infection was slowed, and the estimated timing of cases doubled from 2.0 days to 4.7 days.
As of March 28, there were 32,308 confirmed cases in New York City, and 672 people died from the virus. On March 26, it was reported that the U.S. has more cases of coronavirus than any other country in the world, including China and Italy.
According to media reports on March 30, U.S. President Donald Trump decided to extend social distancing guidelines until April 30.
On the same day, USNS Comfort, a hospital ship with about 1000 beds, docked in New York.
On April 3, the U.S. recorded 8,84 deaths due to the coronavirus within a 24-hour period.
On April 3, in New York State, the number of cases exceeded $100,000. The White House was criticised by ordering health officials and scientists to coordinate statements and public announcements about the vice with Vice President Mike Pence's office.
Trump’s overall crisis management endorsement had support and dissident among supporters.
Some U.S. officials and analysts criticized the U.S. reliance on the import of sensitive goods, including essential medical equipment from China.
In order to draw a map and anticipation of disease prevalence patterns, an analysis of air travel patterns was used and published in mid-January 2020 in the Journal of Travel Medicine.
According to data from 2018, the International Association of Air Transport, Bangkok, Hong Kong, Tokyo and Taipei had the largest number of passengers from Wuhan.
Dubai, Sydney and Melbourne have also been reported as popular destinations for travelers traveling from Wuhan.
Among the 20 most popular destination cities, Bali was regarded as a city with the lowest preparations and the cities of Australia were considered as more prepared cities. Australia announced an emergency anti-coronavirus (Covid-19) plan on February 7.
The plan said there should still be a lot of information about COVID-19, and Australia would emphasize border control and communication in its confrontation with the pandemic.
On March 21, the human environmental emergency was announced in Australia.
Due to the effective quarantine of public transportation in Wuhan and Hubei, several countries decided to evacuate their citizens and diplomats from the area, mainly through the exclusive flights of the country of origin and the approval of the Chinese authorities.
Canada, the U.S., the United States, Japan, India, Sri Lanka, Australia, France, Argentina, Germany and Thailand were the first countries to plan to vacate their citizens.
Pakistan has said it has no plans to evacuate its citizens from China.
On February 7, Brazil evacuated 34 Brazilians or family members, plus four Poles, a Chinese person and an Indian citizen.
Citizens of Poland, China and India in Poland, where the Brazilian plane was halted before it continued to reach Brazil, dropped off the plane.
Citizens of Brazil who went to Wuhan were quarantined at a military base near Brazília.
On the same day, 215 Canadian nationals (176 people on the first plane and 39 people on a second plane rented by the U.S. government) were discharged from Wuhan and transferred to the CFB Trenton to quarantine for two weeks.
On February 11, another plane transported 185 Canadians from Wuhan to the CFB Trenton.
The Australian authorities evacuated 2,77 of their citizens on February 3 and 4, and moved to the detention center of the Christmas Island, which had been converted to a place for quarantine, to stay there for 14 days.
The flight from New Zealand to evacuate landed in Auckland on February 5; its passengers (including people from Australia and Oceania) were quarantined at a naval base in Whangaparoa, north of Auckland.
On February 15, the U.S. announced that the Americans would unwave the Diamond Princess cruise ship.
On February 21, an aircraft carrying 129 Canadian passengers evacuated from the Diamond Princess ship landed in Trenton, Ontario.
In early March, the Indian government began evacuating its citizens from Iran. On March 14, a South African Airlines aircraft chartered by the South African government returned 112 South African citizens to the country.
In order to reduce the risk, medical screening was carried out before the movement, and four South African nationals with coronavirus symptoms were not allowed to board the plane.
The only South African nationals whose test results were negative were returned to the country.
The test results showed that all South African nationals, including flight attendants, pilots, hotel staff, police and soldiers involved in this clean humanitarian mission, however, as a precaution, they were all quarantined and supervised for 14 days in The Ranch Resort.
On March 20, the U.S. began to partially withdraw its troops from Iraq because of the pandemic.
On February 5, China’s Foreign Ministry announced that 21 countries (including Belarus, Pakistan, Trinidad and Tobago, Egypt and Iran) had sent aid to China.
Some Chinese students at U.S. universities joined each other to send aid to China's virus-stricken wards and formed a joint group in the Greter District of Chicago, who reportedly sent 50,000 N95 masks to hospitals in Hubei Province on January 30. By January 30, the Humanitarian Assistance Direct Relief, in coordination with FedEx, sent 200,000 masks, along with other personal protective equipment, including gloves and guns, to the Union Hospital.
On February 5, Bill and Melinda Gates donated a $100-million donation to the World Health Organization to research vaccines and medical efforts and protect “at-risk groups in Africa and South Asia.”
On February 6, Interaksyon reported that the Chinese government had donated 200,000 masks to the Philippines after Senator Richard Gordon sent 3.16 million masks.
On February 19, the Singapore Red Cross announced that it would send $2.26 million aid to China.
Japan donated one million masks to Wuhan, Turkey shipped medical equipment, Russia sent more than 13 tons of medical supplies to Wuhan, Malaysia announced that it would donate 18 million medical gloves to China, Germany sent various medical equipment, including 10,10,000 hazardous material clothing, and the United States donated 17.8 tons of medical equipment to China and promised to provide $100 million in financial support for countries affected by the virus.
In March, China, Cuba and Russia sent medical equipment and experts to Italy to help the country fight the spread of the coronavirus.
Jack Ma, a well-known businessman, sent 1.1 million test kits, 6 million masks and 60,000 protective clothing to Addis Ababa, Ethiopia to be distributed by the African Union.
He later sent 5,000 test kits, 100,000 masks and 5 ventilators to Panama.
Ma also donated medical equipment to Canada. The Netherlands, Spain, Turkey, Georgia and the Czech Republic expressed their concerns about masks and test kits made by China.
For example, Spania returned 58,000 Chinese-made coronavirus test kits whose accuracy rate was only 30%, and the Netherlands returned 600,000 Chinese masks that were defective.
Belgium returned 100,000 unusable masks that were believed to have come from China but actually came from Colombia.
On the other hand, China's assistance was well-accepted in parts of Latin America and Africa. On April 2, the World Bank launched emergency support operations for developing countries.
The World Health Organization (WHO) praised the efforts of the Chinese authorities to manage and contain the pandemic.
The World Health Organization pointed to the contradiction between the SARS outbreak in 2004-2002 and the current crisis, at which time Chinese authorities were accused of secrecy that hindered the success of efforts to prevent and contain the disease, but in the current crisis, the central government “previded new news regularly so that there would not be a panic on the eve of the Lunar New Year holiday.”
On January 23, World Health Organization Representative Gauden Galea, in response to the decision of central authorities to ban transportation in Wuhan, stated: "While the decision "sure certainly not from the World Health Organization's recommendations", "a very very important sign of the commitment to contain the epidemic in a place where the disease is centered most" and called it an "unprecedented decision in the history of public health."
WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom said the PHEIC was declared due to "the risk of a global disease outbreak, especially in low- and middle-income countries that lack a powerful health system.
In response to travel restrictions, Tedros said “there’s ‘no reasons for actions that are unnecessaryly interfering in international travel and trade,” and “the World Health Organization does not recommend restricting trade and displacement.”
Referring to the need to support low-income countries that “do not have systems to detect people with the virus, even if its symptoms emerge,” the World Health Organization asked the international community €675 million to spend on strategic preparedness in these countries.
Tedros also said in a statement that "we are only as strong as our weakest member" and called on the international community to "invest today or spend more later." On February 11, the World Health Organization (WHO) at a press conference called the disease COVID-19.
On the same day, Tedros announced that U.N. Secretary-General Antonio Guterres had agreed to “dedicate the power of the entire U.N. system to deal with the disease.”
As a result, a crisis management team at the U.N. was activated to coordinating all U.N. coordinating the U.N., the World Health Organization said the team would “contemplatively focus on coordinating the disease’s health, while other organizations use their expertise to deal with broader social, economic and development implications of the outbreak.”
On February 14, a joint operational team led by the World Health Organization (WHO) in cooperation with China was activated to help internal management and evaluate the "intensification and contagion of the disease" by hosting workshops and holding meetings with key institutions at the national level.
On February 28, World Health Organization (WHO) announced a global coronavirus threat assessment from "extreme" to "extreme" to "extremely high".
Mike Ryan, Executive Director of the World Health Organization’s Health Emergency Program, warned in a statement: “It’s a real test for all governments on the planet: Wake up.
The virus may be on the way, and you need to be prepared,” stressing that proper countermeasures can prevent “worrest of it” occurring in the world.
Ryan also stated that the current data did not yet persuade public health authorities to declare the global pandemic, emphasizing that declaring the situation means that "we have basically accepted that every human being on the planet is at risk of contracting the virus."
On March 11, the World Health Organization declared the coronavirus outbreak a pandemic.
"The World Health Organization is deeply concerned about both the alarming level of the outbreak and its severity and the alarming level of the lack of appropriate action." The World Health Organization received a lot of criticism due to the improper management of the pandemic, including the late announcement of a public health emergency and the classification of the virus as a pandemic.
These negative reactions included the signing of a petition for the resignation of the Director General of the World Health Organization Tedros Adhanom, signed by 73,3,000 by 733,000.
On March 26, 2020, dozens of UN human rights experts reaffirmed their respect for the rights of all individuals during the COVID-19 pandemic.
This group of experts stated that everyone deserves interventions to save their lives, and this is the responsibility of the government.
The group of experts stressed that the lack of resources or health insurance should not be used as a justification for discrimination against a specific group of individuals.
The experts stressed that each person, including persons with disabilities, minority groups, the elderly, forced domestic immigrants, homeless people, those who live in very bad conditions, people in custody, as well as refugees and other unspecified groups that require government protection, are entitled to health.
International government agencies are addressing the economic and social impacts of the COVID-19 crisis.
The Organization for Economic Collaboration and Development has launched a platform to provide comprehensive and timely information on coping policies in countries around the world, as well as providing perspectives and recommendations.
The digital center offers policies to strengthen the global health system and the global economy to deal with the negative effects of shutdowns and travel restrictions, and includes a section to track the policies of countries aimed at helping countries learn from each other and facilitate a global coordinating confrontation with the coronavirus challenge.
The Chinese government has been criticised by the U.S., Michael Gove, the secretary of the British Cabinet, and Eduardo Bolsonaro, the son of Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro.
A number of the Chinese Communist (CPC) provincial-level managers were dismissed due to quarantine mismanagement in China's central section, a sign of dissatisfaction with the political institution's handling of the outbreak in these areas.
Some analysts believe the move is aimed at protecting Xi Jinping, the secretary-general of the Chinese Communist Party for public outrage due to the spread of the coronavirus.
Some Chinese officials, including Zhao Lijian, rejected their previous confirmation that Wuhan's coronavirus outbreak would begin and raised a conspiracy theoretically that COVID-19 originated from the United States or Italy.
President Donald Trump's administration referred to the coronavirus as "Chinese virus" or "Wuhan virus" and announced that "China's censor has exacerbated the virus and turned it into a global pandemic."
The Daily Beast obtained some U.S. government information that described a media trike that clearly originated by the National Security Council, calling the strategy “It’s all related to China.
We were told to try to convey the message using any possible tools, including a press conference and presence on TV.”Media, including Politico, Foreign Policy and Bloomberg, have claimed that China’s efforts to send aid to the countries where the virus has spread, are part of the propaganda pressure for global influence.
Josep Borrell, the European Union’s foreign policy chief, warned that “there’s a geopolitical component that involves trying to make an influencing through a news ter turn and a policy of generosity.”
Borrell also said, “China wants to send the message that, unlike the United States, is a responsible and reliable partner.”
China also called on the U.S. to lift its sanctions against Syria, Venezuela and Iran, while reportedly sending aid to Venezuela and Iran.
On April 3, U.S. Sanctions prevented 100,000 Jack Ma masks from arriving in Cuba.
U.S. officials have also been accused of transporting aid sent to other countries to their home country.
Disputes were also reported on masks between different countries such as Germany, Austria and Switzerland and the Czech Republic and Italy.
In addition, Turkey confiscated hundreds of ventilators that were sailing to Spain.
In early March, the Italian government criticized the EU's lack of solidarity with Italy, which had suffered a lot of negative effects on the coronavirus.
Maurizio Massari, Italy’s ambassador to the European Union, said: ‘Only China responded.
“It’s certainly not a good sign of European solidarity.”
On March 22, Russian President Vladimir Putin, after a phone call with Italian Prime Minister Giuseppe Conté ordered the Russian army to send military medicines, special disinfection vehicles and other medical equipment to Italy.
Italian newspaper La Stampa quoted an unidentified ��‘high-level ‘policial source’ that 80 percent of Russia’s contributions were “unused or low-usable for Italy.”
The source accused Russia of starting a “geopletic and diplomatic” appearance.
Lombardy President Attilio Fontana and Italian Foreign Minister Luigi Di Maio rejected media reports and expressed their appreciation for the aid.
Russia also sent a cargo plane containing medical assistance to the United States.
The Kremlin spokeswoman Dmitry Peskov said: “Putin believes when sending aid to U.S. partners that when U.S. producers are reinforced and moved, they will be able to retaliate if needed.”
"Defender 2020" will be held on a more limited scale in Germany, Poland and the Baltic countries, which is NATO's largest military exercise since the end of the Cold War.
