Many Lunar New Year’s events and attractions, including the Forbidden City in Beijing and traditional temple exhibitions, were closed to prevent mass gatherings.
In 24 of China’s 31 provinces, municipalities and regions, authorities extended the New Year’s holiday until February 10 and ordered most workplaces not to reopen that date.
These areas accounted for 80% of the country’s GDP and 90% of the country’s exports.
Hong Kong increased the level of anti-infectience to the highest level, declaring a state of emergency, closing schools until March, canceling New Year's Eve celebrations.
