Tests for the 2019 Coronavirus Respiratory (Covid-19) and related SARS-CoV-2 virus include ways to detect the presence of the virus and antibodies produced in response to this infection.
The presence of the virus in samples is confirmed by RT-PCR, which detects corona virus RNA.
This test is proprietary and detects only the SARS-CoV-2 RNA virus.
This test is used to confirm very new or active infections.
The detection of antibodies (serology) can be used both for diagnosis and to monitor the population.
An antibody test shows how many people have had the disease, taking into account people who have had very mild symptoms and did not report, or had symptoms altogether.
Using the results of this test, the exact mortality rate of the disease and the level of collective immunity is detectable in the population.
Due to limited trials, as of March 2020, no country has had reliable data on the extent of the outbreak in its population.
As of March 23, no country has tested more than 3% of its population and there is a huge difference in the number of tests conducted between countries.
This difference is likely to significantly affect the reported death rate, which is likely to overestimated in some countries.
This experiment, which uses instant reverse transcription polymerase (rRT-PCR), can be performed on respiratory samples taken in a variety of ways, including nasopharnex or phlegm sample.
The results of this test are usually determined between a few hours and 2 days.
The RT-PCR test, which is done with throat swab, provides reliable results only in the first week of the disease.
After that, the virus can disappear from the throat, while in the lungs are multiplying.
For infected people who are tested in the second week, alternative samples can be taken from the depths of the respiratory tract by suction catheter, or it is also possible to use coughing substances (phlegm).
One of the first PCR test samples in January 2020 at Charité in Berlin was invented instantaneous using reverse-translination polymerase (rRT-PCR) chain reaction, forming the basis of 250,000 test kits distributed by the World Health Organization (WHO).
The United Kingdom also developed a testing method until January 23, 2020. Kogenebiotech of South Korea on January 28, 2020, developed a PCRS-CoV-2 clinical diagnostic kit (PowerChek Coronavirus).
In China, the BGI group was one of the first companies to receive the confirmation of the emergency use of the PCRS-CoV-2 virus-based diagnostic kit from the Chinese National Medical Products Organization. In the United States, the Centers for Disease and Prevention (CDC) prevention and disease control (CDC).
One of the three genetic tests in the previous versions of the test kit would lead to indeterminatable results due to defective reagents and testing restrictions at the CDC in Atlanta, which caused an average of less than 100 samples per day to be successfully processed throughout February.
As of February 28, 2020, the experiment was not relifitable using two components, and it was after state and local labs were allowed to be tested.
The test was approved by the Food and Drug Administration, under the authorization of emergency use. U.S. commercial laboratories began conducting the test in early March 2020.
As of March 5, 2020, LabCorp announced that RT-PCR-based COVID-19 testing is available nationwide.
Quest Diagnostics similarly made the COVID-19 test available nationwide from March 9, 2020.
There is no limit to the number of tests; the examination and processing of the sample must be carried out according to the requirements of the CDC.
In Russia, the production and development of the COVID-19 testing kit was carried out by the National Center for Virology and Biotechnology Research VECTOR.
On February 11, 2020, the test was recorded by the Federal Center for Health Supervisory Services. On March 12, 2020, it was reported that the Mayo Clinic developed a test for COVID-19 disease. On March 13, 2020, Roche Diagnostics received FDA approval for a test that could be carried out within 3.5 hours in large volumes, allowing a device to conduct about 4,128 tests over a 24-hour period.
On March 19, 2020, the FDA granted Emergency Use (EUA) for the m2000 system to Abbott Laboratories; the FDA had previously issued similar licenses for Hologic, LabCorp and Thermo Fisher Scientific.
On March 21, 2020, Cepheid similarly obtained an EUA license from the FDA for a 45-minute trial.
The FDA has approved an experiment that uses nucleic isotermal acid reinforcement instead of PCR.
Because it doesn’t require an alternating temperature cycle, this procedure can provide a positive result in just five minutes and a negative result within 13 minutes.
There are about 18,000 of these devices currently available in the United States, and Abbott expects to be able to deliver 50,000 tests a day by increasing production. An experiment using monoclonal antideton specifically attached to the new coronavirus nucleoxide (protein) protein is developing in Taiwan, and it is hoped to be able to deliver results within 15 to 20 minutes, just like a fast flu test.
By reviewing research conducted in March 2020, it can be concluded: “Radio photos of the chest have little diagnostic value in the early stages, while CT [computer tomography] findings can be determined even before symptoms appear.”
The common features in CT include multi-lobule matte opaque turbulence with side, symmetrical and posterior distribution.
As the disease progresses, subcommunity, defective coating and stiffening occur.
A study that compares PCR with CT in Wuhan, the origin of the current pandemic, has suggested that CT is significantly more sensitive than PCR, at the same time less proprietary because many features of CT imaging overlap with other inflammatory pulmonary diseases and disease processes.
As of March 2020, the U.S. College of Radiology has recommended that "CT should not be used as a screening or initial test to detect COVID-19."Since March 2020, the CDC proposes the PCR method for initial screening.
Part of the immune system’s response to this infection is the production of antibodies, including IgM and IgG.
These antibodies can be used to detect infections in people after 7 days or more of the appearance of symptoms, the detection of immunity and population monitoring. This test can be done in central laboratories (CLTs) or through a patient’s libin (PoCT).
High-capacity automated systems in many clinical laboratories will be able to perform these tests, but their availability will depend on the amount of production of each system.
For CLT, an environmental blood sample is commonly used, although it is possible to use several samples to track the immune system’s response.
For PoCT, a sample of the blood is usually taken by piercing the skin.
Contrary to PCR methods, it is not necessary to extract pre-test. On March 26, 2020, the FDA published a list of 29 entities that had been notified to the organization, as requested, and were now able to distribute their antibody tests.
As of April 7, 2020, only one trial was approved by the FDA under an emergency use license. In late March 2020, the Euroimmun Medical Detection Laboratory and Epitope Diagnostics gained European approval for their testing kits. These kits could identify IgG and IgA antibodies that were secreted against the virus that were contained in the blood sample.
The testing capacity of several hundred samples is in a matter of hours, so it’s much faster than PCR testing to detect the virus’s RNA.
These antibodies are usually recognizable 14 days after the onset of the disease. In early April, the United Kingdom found that none of the antibody test kits it purchased were adequately used adequately.
Hong Kong implemented a plan according to which suspicious patients can stay at home, "the emergency department will give the patient a sampling tube", the patient pours his saliva into it and pays it back and receives the test result some time later. The British NHS has announced that he is conducting a probatorial plan to get a test of suspected cases at home, which is the risk of being infected if the patient goes to the hospital, as well as if the ambulance is used.
Automotive testing centers have enabled South Korea to operate one of the fastest and most extensive testing methods compared to other countries. In Germany, the National Association of Forced Health Insurance Physicians announced on March 2 that it had a capacity of 12,000 tests per day on a mobile basis and was tested from 10,700 people in the previous week.
