Various test recipes that target different parts of the coronavirus genetic profile were prepared in China, France, Germany, Hong Kong, Japan and the United States.
The World Health Organization (WHO) used German directives to prepare and send kits to low-income countries that do not have the resources to produce domestic kits.
Guidelines for German test kits were published on January 17, 2020. Because the protocol developed by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control wasn't available until January 28, it caused a delay in the start of experiments in the U.S. and the U.S. had a problem with the reliability of test kits early in the outburst, and these countries, along with Australia, failed to provide enough kits to meet the demands and items recommended by health professionals for testing.
Conversely, experts say the widespread availability of tests in South Korea has helped reduce the spread of the new coronavirus.
Testing capacity, mainly in private-section laboratories, has been built by the South Korean government for several years.
On March 16, the World Health Organization called for strengthening testing programs as the best way to slow the progression of the COVID-19 pandemic. The high demand for testing due to the widespread spread of the virus caused hundreds of thousands of tests to be postponed in private laboratories in the United States, with scarce swab inventories.
In March 2020, China reported problems with the accuracy of its test kits.
In the United States, test kits produced by the CDC had "flaws". The government then removed bureaucratic barriers from conducting private experiments. Spain bought test kits from the Chinese company Shenzhen Bioeasy Biotechnology Co Ltd, but found that test results were not correct.
