The test, followed by quarantining people whose test results were positive and tracking those associated with people with a positive SARS-COV-2 virus result, have yielded positive results.
Investigators working in Vò Italy, where the first COVID-19 death took place in Italy, carried out two rounds of experiments on a total population of about 3,400.
About half of the people who tested positive did not have any symptoms, and all positive cases were quarantined.
By restricting travel to public centers, new infections were completely eliminated.
Serious tracking of people who have been in contact, restrictions, testing and quarantine of incoming travellers, the 2020 coronavirus pandemic in Singapore was much slower than in other developed countries, without severe restrictions such as the forced closure of restaurants and retail centers.
Many events have been cancelled, and Singapore advised residents to stay at home on March 28, but schools were reopened on March 23, on March 23.
Several other countries, including Iceland and South Korea, also managed the pandemic by seriously tracking those who had contact, restrictions, testing and quarantine of incoming passengers, with fewer closures.
A statistical study has shown that countries that have conducted more tests than the number of deaths have a much lower mortality rate, possibly because these countries are better able to recognize those who only have mild symptoms or have no symptoms.
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends that countries that do not have testing capacity and have limited national laboratories in the field of COVID-19, send the first five positive prototypes and their top ten negative COVID-19 tests to one of the 16 World Health Organization's reference laboratories to be confirmed.
Out of these 16 reference labs, 7 in Asia, 5 in Europe, 2 in Africa, 1 in North America and 1 in Australia.
