Hand washing, also referred to as hand hygienity, is the act of cleaning a person's hand in order to remove dirt, fat, microorganisms, or other unwanted substances.
Hand-washing with soap constantly at "sensitive occasions" throughout the day prevents the spread of many diseases, for example diarrhea and cholera that are transmitted through feces-mouth.
For example, if people don’t touch their eyes, nose, or mouth (i.e. mucous membranes), they may have respiratory illnesses such as the flu or cold.
The five most important situations throughout the day when washing your hands with soap are important: before and after going to the bathroom, after cleaning the baby or changing his diapers, before feeding the baby, before eating and before and after preparing food or touching raw meat, fish or chicken.
If soap and water are not available, hands can can be cleaned with ash. The World Health Organization recommends hand washing in the following cases:
Before, during and after meals.
Before and after taking care of the patient.
After changing diapers or cleaning the baby after using the toilet.
After sniffing, coughing or sneezing.
After touching the animals, dropping animals or feeding the animals.
From a medical point of view, health refers to health measures related to medical procedures.
Washing your hands before taking medication or medical care can prevent or minimize its transmission.
The main purpose of handwashing medically is to clean the hands of pathogens (bacteriums, viruses or other microorganisms of the disease) and chemicals that can pose a risk or disease.
It’s especially important for people who deal with food or work in medical-related occupations, but it’s also important to the general public.
Hand washing has many health benefits, including minimizing the spread of the flu, coronavirus, and other infectious diseases; prevention of infectious agents of diarrhea; reducing respiratory infections;
and reduced infant mortality rates during childbirth at home.
A 2013 study found that improvements in hand washing practices may lead to a slight improvement in the height growth of children under the age of five.
In developing countries, the mortality rate from respiratory and diarrhea can be reduced by promoting simple behavioral changes, such as hand-washing with soap.
This simple action can reduce the mortality rate from these diseases by approximately 50%.
Measures that promote hand washing can reduce cases of diarrhea to about one-third, and this can be compared to providing clean water in low-income areas.
A 48% reduction in cases of diarrhea can be associated with handwashing with soap. Washing hands with soap as an automatic behavior that has become a habit in homes, schools, and communities around the world is the only, most effective and cheapest way to prevent diarrhea and acute respiratory infections (ARI).
pneumonia, one of the most important acute respiratory infections, is one of the causes of death for children under the age of 5, which kills about 1.8 million children annually.
Diarrhea and pneumonia together kill approximately $3.5 million a year.
According to UNICEF, transforming hand-washing with soap before meals and after using the toilet into a firm habit can save more lives than any vaccine or medical intervention, roughly halve mortality from diarrhea, and reduce one-four-four deaths from acute respiratory infections.
Hand washing is usually integrated with other hygienic interventions as part of water, sanitation and cleanliness (WASH).
Hand washing also protects against yellow-beam infection that is transmitted through direct physical contact.
One of the harmful but small effects of hand washing is that frequent hand washing can lead to drying of the skin, resulting in damage to the skin.
A 2012 Danish study found that over-washing your hands can lead to skin itching, also referred to as eczema or hand dermatitis, which is particularly common among health care workers.
Excessive hand washing is also one of the symptoms of obsessive-compulsive-compulsive (OCD).
The five most important times during the day when washing your hands with soap is important to reduce fecal-oral transmission of the disease are after going to the bathroom (in secret, stools), after cleaning the child (changing diapers), before feeding the baby, before eating, before and after preparing food or touching meat, fish, or raw chicken.
Other items in which the correct method of hand washing should be observed in order to prevent the transmission of the disease are before and after treatment of cuts or wounds, after sneezing, coughing, or sniffing; after touching animal droppings or touching the animals; and after contact with the garbage.
In many countries, the amount of hand washing with soap is very low.
In 2015, a study on hand washing in 54 countries found that an average of 38.7 percent of households, hand-waishing with soap has become a habit. A 2014 study found that Saudi Arabia had the highest rate of hand-washing with soap, 97 percent; the U.S. is in the middle with 77 per cent, and China is at the lowest with 23 per cent.
The Basic Health Care Program, implemented by the Department of Education in the Philippines, is an example of extensive measures to promote health and education in children.
Twice-paracialization a year, along with day-to-day hand washing with soap, brushing your teeth on a daily basis with fluoride is the main focus of this national program.
In Indonesia, the program has also been successfully implemented.
The addition of soap or detergent to the water is effective in removing microorganisms from the skin.
The main function of soaps and detergents is to reduce the barriers to solubility and increase solubility.
Water alone is an inefficient cleanser of the skin because fats and proteins that are part of organic soil don’t easily dissolve in water.
However, a sufficient amount of water flow helps in cleaning.
Due to the reusability nature, solid soap may retain bacteria from previous uses.
A small number of studies have concluded that the transmission of bacteria from contaminated solid soap has concluded that the transmission of bacteria in this way seems unlikely because the bacteria are washed with foam.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention still believes that “liquid supposium is better accessible without direct contact.”
Antibacterial soaps have been highly advertised for people who care about hygiene.
To date, there is no evidence that disinfectants or disinfectants are effective in eliminating antibiotic-resistant organisms in nature.
However, antibacterial soaps contain common antibacterial substances such as triclosane, with a wide variety of organisms resistant.
Therefore, even if antibiotic-reliant species weren’t meant to be eliminated with antibacterial soaps, these soaps may not be as effective as they are advertised on the market.
In addition to surfactant and skin protection, advanced formulations may contain acid (acetic acid, ascorbic acid, lactic acid) as a pH regulator, anti-microbial-active benzoic acid and other skin conditioners (aloe vera, vitamins, mentoles, plant extracts).
Warm water that is used for hand washing is not hot enough to eliminate bacteria.
Bacterabics grow much faster at body temperature (37°C).
However, hot water combined with soap is more effective compared to cold water along with soap in removing natural oils that contain dirt and bacteria.
However, contrary to popular belief, scientific studies have shown that the use of hot water does not have an effect on reducing the microbial load on the hands.
Hand sanitizer is a non-aqual hygienic.
In the late 1990’s and early 21st, water-based alcoholic hygienic (also known as alcohol-based disinfectant or hand sanitizer) gained popularity.
Most of these are made of isopropyl alcohol or ethanol, along with a thickener, such as carbomer (acrylic plimer) and gel-shaped, or use a dilutor like glycerin to make it easy to use and reduce the effect of the alcohol dryener and are made in the form of liquid or foam.
The addition of diluted hydrogen peroxide increases further antimicrobial activities. Antiseptizers containing at least 60 to 95 percent alcohol are effective micro-killers.
Alcohol-containing disinfectants cause the loss of bacteria, multi-druge-resistant bacteria (MRSA and VRE), tuberculosis and some viruses (including HIV, herpes, RSV virus, rhinovirus, cowpox, flu and hepatitis), and fungi.
Saintifiers that contain 70% alcohol, 30 seconds after use, destroy 99.97% (reducing 3.5 logs equivalent to a decrease of 35 dB) of hand bacteria and 1 minute after use, resulting in the loss of 99.99% to 99.999% (4 to 5 logs). Hand disinfectants are more effective against bacteria but are less effective against some viruses.
Alcohol-based hand sanitifiers are almost ineffective against neuroviral (or Norwalk), which is the most common cause of contagious gastrointestinal disease. An adequate amount of hand sanitizer or alcohol-containing gel should be used so that both hands are completely wet or covered.
The back and on both hands and between and the ends of all fingers should be applied for approximately 30 seconds until the liquid, foam, or gel is dried.
Fingertips should also be washed well by rubbing to the palms of both hands. The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends hand-washing rather than using hand sanitifiers, especially when the hands are completely dirty.
The ever-increasing use of these materials is due to the ease of their use and their ability to eliminate microorganisms quickly; however, they should not be used as an alternative to proper hand washing unless soap and water are unavailable.
Frequent use of alcohol-based hand sanitifiers can cause dry skin unless softeners or moisturizers are added to their formulation.
Adding glycerin or other softeners to their recipe formula can reduce or eliminate the alcohol drying effect.
In clinical trials, hand sanitifiers, which contained alcohol and softening substances, caused much less burning and dry skin than soaps or antimicrobial detergents.
Allergic contact dermatitis, contact hive syndrome or alcohol sensitivity, or additives found in alcohol-containing hand sanitifiers rarely occur.
A lower probability of contact dermatitis compared to hand washing with soap and water made them attractive.
Despite their efficacy, non-water-based materials do not clean the hands of organic matter, but disinfect them.
That’s why hand sanitifiers don’t be as effective as soap and hydrated to prevent the spread of many diseases, as pathogens still remain on their hands.
The effect of alcohol-free hand sanitifiers relies heavily on their ingredients and formulations and has historically had a lower effect than alcohol-containing sanitizers.
Recently, it has been shown that formulas that use benzealconium chloride have more cumulative and persistent antimicrobial activity after use, unlike alcohol, which is likely to decrease due to negative skin reactions after repeated use.
Many people in low-income communities can’t make soap and use ash or soil instead.
Ash or soil may be more effective than water alone, but it may have a less effectiveness compared to soap.
One of the concerns is that if soil or ash is contaminated with microorganisms, it may increase the spread of the disease, on the contrary.
Ash, like soap, is a disinfectant because it forms an alkaline solution in contact with water.
In the absence of soap, the World Health Organization recommends ash or sand as a substitute for soap.
The correct method of hand washing recommended by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control to Prevent Disease Transition includes the following steps:
Soak your hands with warm or cold running water.
Current water is recommended because stagnant waters may be polluted. However, the water temperature does not appear to make any changes.
Put your hands in a sufficient amount of soap and rub it together, including the back of your hands, between your fingers, and under your nails.
Soap destroys germs on the skin, and studies show that people tend to wash their hands more carefully when they use soap when they only use water.
Put your hands together for at least 20 seconds.
Rubbing the hands creates friction and helps to remove germs from the skin, and rubbing the hands for a longer long-time eliminates more germs.
Wash your hands with good running water.
Washing in stagnant water can re-infect the hand.
Dry with a clean towel or let it dry in the air.
Wet and wet hands can easily become infected. Most of the parts that remain sitting are the thumb, the wrist, the areas between the fingers and under the nails.
Synthetic nails and nail varnishes may be the hideout of microorganisms.
The moisturizing cream is often recommended to prevent the hands from drying out. Dry skin leads to skin damage and can increase the risk of transmitting the infection.
There are a variety of low-cost options to facilitate handwashing in developing countries when tap water or soap is not available. For example, pouring water from a gallon or perforated container hanging or if necessary to use ash. In situations where water resources are limited (such as schools or rural areas in developing countries), there are solutions to save water, such as "Tippy taps or low-cost taps" and other low-cost options.
Type Tap is a simple technology that is made from a container that is hung by a rope and a controllable lever by the foot to shed a small amount of water on the hands and a soap mold.
Effective hand drying is an important part of the hand hygiene process, but there’s a debate about the most effective form of hand drying in public bathrooms.
An increasing volume of research suggests that paper towels are much more hygienic than electric dryers that exist in many bathrooms.
In 2008, a study supported by the paper towel industry was held by the University of West Minster in London to compare the hygienic amount of paper towels, hot air dryers, and more modern air jet dryers.
After washing and drying the hands with a hot air dryer, the total number of bacteria in the fingertips increased by an average of 194%, and the bacteria in the palms increased by 254%.
The drying of the hands with an air jet dryer led to an average increase in the total number of bacteria on the fingertips by 42% and 15% in the palms of the hands.
After washing and drying the hands with a paper towel, the total number of bacteria was reduced by an average of 76% at the fingertips and 77% in the palms of their hands.
An air-jet dryer, which claims to blow the air out at a speed of 180 m/s (650 km or 400 miles/h), is capable of blowing microorganisms out of the hands and devices, and could potentially pollute other people who use the bathroom, as well as the restroom up to a distance of 2 meters.
The use of a hot air drying machine can dissibrate microorganisms up to 0.25 meters from the dryer.
Paper towels did not cause the significant release of microorganisms. In 2005, in a study conducted by TÜV Produkt und Umwelt, various methods of hand drying were investigated.
The following changes were observed in a number of bacteriaces after drying their hands:
There are a large number of hand drying machine manufacturers, and hand drying machines are compared with hand drying with paper towels.
In the absence of soap and water when traveling, hand washing with hand wipes can be used as an alternative method.
Alcohol-based hand sanitizer must contain at least 60% alcohol.
A long time after the Hungarian doctor, Ignaz Semmelweis, diagnosed the effectiveness of hand washing (in 1846) in the prevention of disease in a hospital environment, hand medical washing became mandatory.
There are electronic devices that can be reminded if hospital staff forgot to wash their hands.
One study found that the rate of infection had been reduced with their use.
The time it takes to wash a medical hand for at least 15 seconds, which is done using a sufficient amount of soap and water or gels and rubbing all parts of the hand.
Hands must be rubbed together and the fingers are intertwined.
If there is pus under the nails, a brush can be used to remove it.
Since germs may remain in the water on your hands, it’s very important that your hands wail well and dry with a clean towel.
After drying the hand, the paper towel should be used to close the faucet (and if necessary to open the outlet door).
This will prevent re-pollution of the hands by contact with those surfaces.
The purpose of hand-washing in medical centers is to eliminate pathogenic microorganisms (“michrubes”) and to prevent their transmission.
Non-hand washing is an unacceptable level in most hospital settings, with a large number of doctors and nurses usually forget to wash their hands before they come into contact with patients, resulting in the transmission of microorganisms.
One study found that proper hand washing and other simple procedures can reduce up to 66 percent of the amount of blood flow infections that the catheter is involved. The World Health Organization (WHO) has published a paper that demonstrates the standard method of hand-washing and rubbing hands together in health care units.
The hand health manual developed by this organization is also available to the public on its website.
A related investigation was carried out by Whitby and colleagues.
In the need to prove compliance, commercial devices can measure and verify hand hygiene.
The World Health Organization (WHO) outlines “five times” to wash hands:
After exacerbation of the blood/body's fluids
Before doing work related to disinfection, and
After the patient's care. The addition of disinfectant chemicals to the soap (derfugal or "anti-microbial" soap) strengthens the fatal properties of the hand washing agent.
Such actions may be considered before surgery or in an environment where antibiotic-resistant organisms are very common. It is necessary to have the following to wash your hands before surgery: the faucet that can be opened and closed without direct contact, some chlorrhexidine or iodine detergent, sterilized towels for drying their hands after washing them and sterilized brushes and other sterilized appliances to clean under the nails.
All the jewels need to be taken out.
In this procedure, it is necessary to wash the hand and the forearm to the elbow for usually 2-6 minutes.
It’s not necessary to wash for a long (10-minute).
When drawing water, prevent the water from returning to the forearm to the hands.
After hand washing, the hands are dried with sterile fabric and surgical clothing is worn.
It is better to wash your hands or use a hand sanitizer before and after taking care of the patient.
To control staphylococic infections in hospitals, it has been found that the greatest benefit of cleaning hands comes from the first 20% of the washing, and has very few additional benefits when the period of hand cleaning reaches more than 35%.
Hand-washing with regular soap compared to antibacterial soap is three times the transmission of bacterial infectious diseases to food. Comparing hand-washing with alcohol-based solution by hand washing with antibacterial soap for 30 seconds showed that hand-washing with alcohol reduces bacterial contamination of 26% compared to antibacterial soap.
But the use of soap and water is more effective than hand-washing with alcohol-based substances in reducing the H1N1 Type A flu virus and celestridium diphiles from the hands. The necessary measures to improve hand hygiene in health centers can include hand-washing, increasing access to alcohol-based detergents, and written and oral reminders to employees.
Further research is needed to determine which of these measures is more effective in different health care environments.
In developing countries, hand washing with soap is referred to as a cost-effective and essential tool to achieve good health and even proper nutrition.
However, the absence of reliable water, soap, or handwashing devices at home, schools, and the workplace challenges achieving the behaviors necessary to hand-wash globally.
For example, in most rural areas in Africa, faucets are scarce for hand-washing near personal or public toilets, although there are cheaper options to build hand washing stations.
However, the low levels of hand washing, not mostly due to the absence of soap or water, can be the result of our bad habits.
Promoting hand-washing with soap can affect selective policies, raise awareness about the benefits of hand washing, and lead to a long-term behavioral change at the community level.
Observation and evaluation is required to ensure that this work becomes effective.
A systematic review of 70 studies found that community-based approaches are effective in increasing hand washing rates in low- and middle-income countries, while social advertising campaigns are less efficient. One example to promote hand-washing in schools is UNICEF's three-star approach, which encourages schools to take simple and inexpensive steps to ensure that students wash their hands with soap by complying with other health requirements.
By observing the minimum standards, schools can eventually be upgraded from one star to three.
The construction of hand washing stations can be part of the promotional measures taken to reduce disease and mortality in children.
The International Hand Washing Day is another example of awareness programs that aims to create a change in people's behavior. As a result of the 20-2019 coronavirus pandemic, UNICEF promoted the use of hand-washing emojis.
A small number of studies, in general, rejected the cost-effectiveness of hand washing in developing countries in relation to the number of years lost due to inability.
However, one study found that promoting hand-washing with soap is significantly more economical compared to other measures.
For the first time, the importance of hand-washing for human health—particularly for those who are in vulnerable conditions, such as mothers who had just given birth or soldiers injured at the hospital – in the middle of the 19th century was determined by two hand health pioneers: the Hungarian physician, Ignaz Semmelweis, who worked in Vienna, Austria, and Florence Nightingale English, who was the “founder of modern nursing.”
At that time, most people believed that infections were caused by an unwelcomant smell called miyasma.
In the 1980’s, outbreaks of food-related diseases and health care-related infections caused the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention in the U.S. broader to promote hand hygienicity as an important way to prevent the spread of infection.
An outbreak of swine flu in 2009 and the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020 led to an increased awareness in many countries about the importance of handwashing with soap to protect themselves from infectious diseases.
For example, posters titled “The Right Hand Washing Methods” were installed next to hand bathroom sinks in public toilets and toilets of German office buildings and airports.
‘Wasing someone’s hand from something means declaring a person’s unwillingness to take responsibility for a matter or sharing it.
The term is borrowed from Matthew's Bible, where Pontius Pilates stopped deciding to crucify the Messiah, but the phrase is now used in some English communities with a much broader use.
In Macbeth Shakespeare, Ms. Macbeth is forced to wash her hands in an attempt to remove a nimatical stamin, showing her guilty conscience about the crimes he had committed and urged her husband to commit.
It has also been found that after reminding or reflecting on unethical actions, people tend to wash their hands more than others and are more inclined to hand-washing equipment.
In addition, people who are allowed to wash their hands after such contemplation are less likely to participate in other “purification” compensatory measures, such as voluntary work.
Religions recommend hand-washing for hygienic and symbolic purposes. Symbolic handwashing and the use of water and not soap to wash hands are part of hand-washing rituals that have been exhibited in many religions, including Bahá'í, Hinduism, twiles, twiles, vulnification in Christianity and ablution in Islam.
Hinduism, Judaism and Islam are ordering to wash their hands after using the toilet.
And Hinduism, Buddhism, Sikism, Judaism and Islam recommend washing their hands before and after every meal.
Contrations of COVID-19-related hazards at the workplace.
Controlling risks related to COVID-19 in the workplace means the use of safe-hygienic methods to control hazards in order to prevent COVID-19 (Covid-19).
Appropriate hazard control in the workplace depends on the workplace and the type of job and should be based on the assessment of risk sources, the severity of the disease in the community, and risk factors for employees who may be at risk of vulnerability to COVID-19.
According to the U.S. Department of Occupational Safety and Health (OSHA), jobs that are at-risk, have less job contact with ordinary people and other co-workers, and to take basic measures to prevent infections, including hand washing, encouraging workers to stay at home if they are sick, to observe breathing etiquette, and maintain day-to-day cleanliness and disinfect the workplace.
Moderate-at-middle-risk occupations include jobs in which employees require frequent contact or close contact with people who are not infected with COVID-19, but may be infected due to the constant spread of the virus in the community or due to international travel.
This includes those who come into contact with the general public, for example in schools, in workplaces with a large population density, and in some retailers with a high volume.
In addition to basic infection prevention measures, including air conditioning through high-efficiency air filters, sneezing protectors and having personal protective equipment in the event of exposure to a person with COVID-19.
The U.S. Occupational Safety and Occupational Health (OSHA) Office considers health care workers who are exposed to contact with people affected or suspected of contracting COVID-19, including those who are at a high risk. If employees do things that result in the production of particulate in the air, they are at a very high dat risk if they collect, move, or handle samples from people who are suspected of COVID-19.
The appropriate way to control the risk for these people includes engineering controls such as the creation of negative pressure ventilation chambers and the use of personal protective equipment in accordance with the job.
The prevalence of COVID-19 can have different effects on the working environment.
Employees may not come to work due to getting sick, the need to take care of others, or the fear of a possible exposure.
Business patterns may change, both in terms of what goods are needed and in terms of the method of achieving these goods (such as non-clusive shopping or buying through home delivery services or buying by car).
Finally, the transportation of items from geographical areas that are severely affected by COVID-19 can be halted. Necessary preparation and programs that can be used to respond to infectious diseases to guide protective measures.
These programs address the level of risk associated with different workplaces and occupational tasks, including sources of exposure to the disease, risk factors caused by the home and community environment, and individual risk factors for employees such as aging or chronic illness.
It also describes the necessary control measures to resolve those risks, as well as possible plans to deal with situations that may arise as a result of the outbreak.
Appreparation and programs prepared to respond to infectious diseases may be subject to national or local recommendations.
Response targets include reducing disease transmission between employees, protecting people who are more prone to health side effects, maintaining business activities and minimizing the negative effects on other entities in their supply chain.
The severity of the disease in areas where business activities take place affects the decisions taken.
Risk control hierarchy consists of a framework widely used in occupational safety and occupational health in order to categorize hazard controls proportionate to their effectiveness.
In cases where it is not possible to eliminate the risks of COVID-19, the most effective methods of control, engineering controls, followed by administrative controls, and at the end of personal protective equipment.
The engineering control method requires keeping employees away from the risks of work without relying on the behavior of employees and can be the most cost-effective solution applicable.
Administrative control methods include changes in the policy or procedure implemented by the employee or employer.
Personal protective equipment (PPE) is less efficient than engineering and office control methods, but can partially prevent exposure to the virus.
All types of PEPEs should be selected based on the risk to the employees, installed properly (e.g., the breathing machine), be worn continuously and correctly, inspected, stored and replaced if necessary, properly removed, cleaned and properly maintained or discloded to prevent contamination.
According to the U.S. Department of Occupational Safety and Health (OSHA), jobs that are at-risk have less job contact with ordinary people and other co-workers.
The basic measures that are recommended to prevent infections in all workplaces include hand washing, encouraging workers to stay at home if they are sick, observing respiratory etiquettes, including covering the mouth and nose when coughing and sneezing, preparing napkins and trash, preparing for remote work or in intermittent shifts if needed, encouraging employees not to use the tools and equipment of others and cleaning and disinfecting the workplace regularly.
The rapid identification and disassembliation of people who are potentially infected is an important step in protecting employees, customers, visitors, and others in the workplace.
U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that employees who have symptoms of acute respiratory disease should stay at home for as long to stay at home without the use of a fever-reducing fever or other symptom-changing medications, fever symptoms, and other symptoms, for at least 24 hours, and that leave policies need to be flexible, allowing employees to stay at home to take care of family members.
According to the Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA), jobs at moderate level include occupations where employees require frequent contact or close contact at a distance of less than six feet (1.8 meters) with people who are not infected with COVID-19 or are not suspected of being infected, but may be infected due to the continuous spread of the virus at the community level or due to international travel in areas where COVID-19 has been widely published.
It includes employees who are in contact with the public, for example in places such as schools, high-density-density working environments, and some of the most crowded checkups. An engineering control method for these and high-risk groups can include the installation of high-power air filters, increasing the amount of ventilation, the installation of physical barriers such as a plastic protector to sneez, and the installation of car service windows.
Employees in this risk group rarely need to use the respiratory system.
If a person becomes sick inside the plane, the appropriate ways to protect employees and other passengers can include separating the patient from others at a distance of 6 feet, allocating only one of the crew to serve the patient and provide a face mask to the patient, or to ask the patient to cover his or mouth and nose while coughing or sneezing.
The aircraft's crew must wear disposable medical gloves when taking care of the patient's passenger or during contact with bodily fluids or contaminated surfaces, and if the patient has a fever, persistent cough, or difficulty in breathing.
Gloves and other disposable items should be thrown into a bio-risk bag, and infected surfaces must then be cleaned and disinfected. For commercial transportation, including cruise ships and other passenger ships, controlling the risks includes delaying travel during illness, individual quarantine, and in case of fever or other symptoms when traveling, it is to immediately notify the medical center inside the ship.
Ideally, medical follow-ups should be carried out in a quarantined person's cabin. For schools and child care centers, if the infected person has entered the school building regardless of the spread of the disease in the community, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) will recommend the closure of the premises for a short time in order to clean or disinfect.
If there is the transmission of the disease in the community at a low or moderate level, the following measures can be taken in line with social distancing: the abolition of scientific excursions, gatherings and other large gatherings, such as physical or deaf training classes or eating in cafeteria, increasing the gap between desks, sparing the distance between arrivals and expulsions, restricting unnecessary visitors and separate use of health facilities for children with flu symptoms.
When there is a significant possibility of disease transmission in the community, in addition to social distancing methods, the long-term closure of schools should be considered. For law enforcement personnel who carry out their day-to-day activities, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) underestimates their immediate health risk.
Law enforcers who need to be in touch with infected or suspicious people with COVID-19 are advised to follow the guidelines that medical emergency technicians follow, including the use of appropriate personal protective equipment.
If you have close contact when people are arrested, employees should clean and disinfect their belts and their belongings before re-use using a household cleaning spray or napkins, while at the same time follow the standard methods to use and dispose of personal protective equipment (PPE) and wash clothes.
The U.S. Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) recognizes health care workers among those at a high risk.
High-level occupations include health care, support, laboratory, and medical transportation employees who have been exposed to people who are infected or suspected to have COVID-19.
Employees whose work results in the production of particulate matter in the air ore collected, relocated, or handle, or handle samples of people infected with COVID-19 are at a very high risk.
Works that lead to the production of particulate matter in the air include intubation, induction of coughing, bronchoscopy and some dental operations and examinations or invasive sampling of the patient.
Occupations that are at very high risk include employees preparing the bodies of people who were infected or suspected of contracting COVID-19 when they died. In case of an autopsy, the level of risk is very high. Additional engineering control methods for this group include the creation of separate rooms for patients with or suspected of developing COVID-19, including when doing things that lead to the production of particulate matter in the air.
Negative pressure ventilation may be suitable for some health centers.
Patient samples should be taken into account Level 3 biometric precautions.
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends being kept in a specified and separate waiting room if the newcomers are suspected to have COVID-19. In addition to the use of other personal protective devices, the Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) recommends the use of the respiratory system for those who work within a range of 6 feet or suspected of COVID-19, as well as for those who do things that lead to the production of particulate in the air.
In the U.S., the N95 breathing filtering device approved by NIOSH should be used according to the provisions of a comprehensive and written respiratory protection program that includes appropriate tests, training and medical examinations.
Other types of respiratory tracts can have more protection and make it easier for employees to use. The World Health Organization does not recommend the use of full-body coverage because COVID-19 is a respiratory disease and is not transmitted through body fluids.
The World Health Organization only recommends the use of surgical masks to screen people at entry points.
The World Health Organization recommends the use of surgical masks, glasses, or protective of COVID-19 patients or carries them, and does not do anything that results in the production of particulate matter in the air, recommends the use of surgical masks, eyeglasses, or facial protectors, guns and gloves.
In the case of what is done, particulate matter is produced in the air, the surgical mask will be substituted with the N95 or FFP2 respiratory tract.
Due to the fact that the global supply of personal protective devices is not enough, the World Health Organization recommends the following to minimize the need for personal protective equipment: performing medical examinations in person, creating physical barriers such as installing transparent windows, allowing only people who are directly involved in the care of the COVID-19 patient, the use of personal protective devices only to perform certain tasks, the use of a breathing mask when taking care of multiple patients.
From: Katherine Maher, CEO of the Wikimedia Foundation
To: All Employees of the Wikimedia Foundation
Topic: [Covid-19] Lightning the load and preparing for the future
Post date/time: 14 March 2020, 00:24 UTC
Applicant: CC0: Rights are not reserved
We’ll see ourselves this month in a remarkable situation.
The COVID-19 pandemic is something that clearly illuminates the universal continuity of human beings and our responsibilities towards each other.
Its challenges are unprecedented for us, but we know that our best response depends on empathy, cooperation, and community-building that is at the heart of this organization.
Accompaniment and the level of care we’ve seen among our colleagues via e-mail, calls, and chat shows the incredible people we’ve had the opportunity to work alongside.
I am very appreciative and prehensible for having all of you on my side as a co-worker.
Last week, someone appreciated what we do.
They reminded me how meaningful the world can go to Wikipedia right now, and what a powerful indication that this important resource can be available online.
Your work makes it possible, whether it’s you keep sites up-to-date or our co-workers are getting paid or our communities are secure.
The universe, now more than ever, needs the information that Wikipedia provides.
It’s a moment in which not only what we are doing, but also the way we work will have a significant impact on the world.
Due to the importance of this mission and your role in it, we will make significant adjustments from the beginning of the next week.
Adjustments related to our work and programs
As Robin mentioned earlier, Team-C met last night to discuss our approach and plan for the coming days and months.
During that conversation, we made a decision that we thought was the right answer to what we were dealing with and is the best way to keep the organization stable during this time.
We wanted to relieve stress and support our long-term mission.
If you need to decrease, it’s okay.
To all employees, contractors and contract employees:
Until further notice, we’ll expect 4 hours to work a day or 20 working hours a week.
“We won’t announce a shutdown – if you can work more normal hours, you’ll be useful for this mission.
However, the world is currently unpredictable, and it doesn’t matter if you’re taking care of your loved ones, you’re buying food, or going to a doctor, your health is a priority.
We don’t track your time.
If you’re sick, don’t work.
It has to go without saying anything, but we say that.
You don’t need to take sick leave or personal leave – you just need to tell your manager and help your group review calendars and schedules and make sure that someone’s covered key tasks.
(If your COVID-19 test result has been diagnosed positive, please notify Bryan from the T&C section so that T&C can help you and ensure that your situation is managed correctly.)
The salaries of employees who work at an hour are fully paid.
As we have already said, we adhere to our obligations to contractors and colleagues who work on an hourly basis.
Everyone's wages are paid based on usual working hours under normal circumstances.
This includes you, even if you’re sick and unable to work.
If you want to work, we will support you.
A lot of people use working as a way to drain their stress into the world around us.
What we are doing, especially at such times, can be very valuable.
Let’s just say, it’s about your self-care.
Our request is that you communicate with your manager so that we know what to expect and be able to plan accordingly.
Some things are considered necessary.
There are things we need to do.
SRE, HR Ops, trust and safety and fundraising (and some others) do basic and important tasks that may require more support.
“We’re going to start a process with other departments to evaluate our current goals and shift our focus toward supporting the essentials for our mission.
There’s a lot of work for all of us, we’ll all focus on the most basic projects.
If we slow down now, we won’t get hurt later.
We don't have a plan to work twice as soon as the pandemic is over.
You’re not expected to work on extra hours to realize deadlines that are now impossible.
“We accept that the situation has changed and we will strive to set new goals and timelines if necessary.
How does the APP (annually planning) become?
To match the new facts and expectations of the daily working hours, we are going to adjust the delivery schedule of your 2021-20220 annual schedule.
Our goal is to propose an extension of the 2020-2019 program, which will give us more time to budget and allow employees to prioritize vital tasks, self-care and take care of their loved ones, while also allowing those who in need to or want to work fewer hours over the next few weeks.
This scheduling extension will greatly reduce the pressure of current work schedules throughout the organization.
“We will present our proposal to the board next week, and as soon as it is approved, we will inform the delegates and the teams in the next steps.
Thanks to the annual planning team to lead this work.
Office Status, Exposure to Illness and Cleaning.
Last We learned last week that a SF-based colleague may have been exposed to the COVID-19 virus.
Nevertheless, due to greater caution, we hired an anti-virus cleaning team to disinfect all surfaces in San Francisco's office.
They used a first-class hospital anti-virus solution to disinfect all surfaces, as well as the side of the lobby and an elevator that leads to our floor.
The building uses its care protocol and uses products that are beneficial to the health of its residents.
“We’re confident that when we make a decision to go back, the office will be well-prepared.
Our Washington office is located in a WeWork and they shared their COVID-19 protocol with us and all employees stationed in D.C.
According to San Francisco State Guidelines, since last week, our Washington D.C. office has become a completely remote suite.
As some of our New York-based colleagues know, we’ve also talked about renting a place in Brooklyn.
These discussions will continue, but it may be delayed.
Some of our colleagues are working remotely for the first time.
Old co-workers who work with us remotely know that this can be a compliance with the situation and want to give you some advice:
Limit the duration of the sessions to a maximum of one or two hours.
If you’ll need longer sessions, check out how they can be held within a few days.
Specify the meeting in a clear way, have an agenda, and submit the necessary content for the study in advance.
Set video with tools like Google Docs and Zoom by default so that you can facilitate collaboration and live communication.
Make someone in charge of the leadership of the meeting, appoint someone responsible for monitoring the chat to follow up the questions and the list of speakers and someone to take notes (or take a joint notation).
If you need a comfortable headphone, send an email to the technical support group.
