b‘henzyostansin 2 (ACE2) is an enzyme that attaches to the outer surface (cell membrane) of lung cells, arteries, hearts, kidneys and intestines.
ACE2 impairs the activity of angiotensin-transducer (ACA) by reducing the angiotensin-II angiotensin-II value and an increase (Ang(1-7), which makes it a promising medicinal purpose for the treatment of cardiovascular disease. ACE2 also acts as a cell entry point for some coronaviruses.
The human version of this enzyme is often called hACE2.
Angiothansin-transducer is a type of zinc-containing metalluthansium that is located on the surface of endothelial cells and other cells.
ACE2 protein contains an N-terminal pedepteptalysis range of M2 and an amino-carrying range of C-thermal colectrine.
ACE2 is a single-phasy I membrane protein whose enzymatic active range is located adjacent to the surface of the lung cells and other tissues.
ACE2’s extracellular amplitude, along with another an enzyme called Shadez, is separated from the turbulage range, and the resulting soluble protein is finally excreted with urination after entering the bloodstream.
ACE2 exists in most organs: ACE2 adheres to the cellular membranes of commonly pulmonary type II bubble cells, small intestine introcytes, endothelial cells of the arteries and veins, and the smooth muscle cells of most of the organs.
ACE2 mRNA symptoms can be seen in the cortex of the brain, lizard body, hypothalamus, and brain stem.
The main function ACE2 is the activity as ACE neutralizer.
ACE converts the hormone angiotensin I into angiotensin of vein II.
ACE2, in turn, separates carboxyl-therminal phenylanine amino acid from angiotensine (Asp-Arg-Val-Ill-Ile-He-Pro-Phe) and hydrolyzes it into angiotansin (1-7), (H-Asp-Arg-Val-Tyr-Ile-Ile-Pro-Pro-EH).
ACE2 can break a number of other peptides, such as [des-Arg9]-Bradi Kane, Aplin, Neurotensine, Dinorphin A, and Gherlin.
ACEO2 also regulates the membrane flow of the neutral amino acid carrier SLC6A19 and plays a role in the development of Hart Nap disease.
ACE2, as an uptractive protein, plays the role of the main entry point for some coronaviruses such as HCoV-NL63; SARS-CoV (SARS-CEV); and SARS-CoV-2 (covid-19-based virus).
More importantly, the adherence of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV2 proteins to the enzymatic AACE2 level on the cell’s surface leads to the intrustination and displacement of the virus and enzymes in the endosomes inside the cells.
This inclusion process entails the preparation of the S protein by TMPRSS2, the host serine protease, whose deterrence is under investigation in the current research process as a possible treatment. It has been hypothesized that a reduction in ACE2 levels in cells may help fight against infection.
However, numerous professional communities and regulatory bodies have proposed that treatment continue with standard ACE deterrent and ARB.
The systematic and meta-analysis published on July 11, 2012, determined that “the use of ACE deterrents is linked to a 34% reduction in the likelihood of pneumonia compared to controls.”
In addition, “the possibility of pneumonia was reduced in patients treated with ACE inhibitors who were more likely to develop pneumonia, especially those with stroke and heart failure.
“The use of ACE inhibitors was linked to a reduction in fatalities caused by pneumonia, although the results were poorer compared to the general results.”
It is thought that reuniversive ACE (rhACE2) is considered a new treatment for acute pulmonary injury, and it appears to have improved respiratory hemodynamics and oxygen saturation in pigs with acute respiratory distress syndrome caused by lipopolic saccharide.
The half-life of rhACE2 in humans is about 10 hours, and the start of the operation is 30 minutes plus the effect (duration period) of 24-hour.
Some findings suggest that rhACE2 may be a promising drug for people with systemic and classical retrenin-angiostancin inhibitors (RSA inhibitors) or diseases that promote the circulation of angiotensin I II. The effect of rhACE2 injections has been evaluated in clinical trials for the treatment of acute respiratory distress syndrome.
b‘Covid-19 apps are mobile software programs that are used to intercept calls during the 20-2019 coronavirus pandemic, i.e. the process of identifying people (communications) who may have been in contact with an infected person.
Numerous programs have been developed or have been proposed to have the support of government officials in certain territories and regions.
Several frameworks have been developed to prepare contact tracking programs.
Problems with privacy have been raised, especially in systems based on the relevance of the geographical location of app users.
Less-interventional solutions include the use of Bluetooth signals to record the user’s proximity to other mobile phones.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple jointly announced that they would incorporate Bluetooth-based support capabilities directly on Android and iOS operating systems.
In China, the Chinese government, in cooperation with Alipay, has developed a program that allows citizens to find out if they were in contact with people with COVID-19.
The program is used in more than 200 Chinese cities. In Singapore, a program called TraceTogether is used.
The program has been developed by the local IT community, released as an open source and will be handed over to the government. Northern Macedonia has released a Bluetooth-centric program called "StopKorona!" to track possible contact with infected people and provide immediate response to medical authorities.
The program has been developed by the Ministry of Communications and Technology and the Ministry of Health.
As of April 14, 2020, the app has been waiting for Google's Play Store and Apple's App Store.
On April 12, the government announced that the call tracking program was in an advanced stage of development and will be ready to be deployed within a few weeks. A similar program ("StopCovid") will be implemented in Ireland and France.
Both Australia and New Zealand have programs based on TraceTogether's TraceTogether and the BlueTrace protocol. Russia plans to designate a geolocation program for patients with COVID-19 and in Moscow to prevent these people from leaving home.
Ross Anderson, a professor of security engineering at the University of Cambridge, pointed out a number of applied problems related to program-based systems, including false positive results and possible inefficiencies if the program is used in small population samples.
By reviewing problems related to the spread of “coronavirus” misleading or harmful apps, Apple has imposed restrictions on the type of organizations that are allowed to add coronavirus programs to the App Store, and only allows “official” or reputable organizations to do so.
Google and Amazon have imposed similar restrictions.
Privacy advocates have expressed concern about the consequences of public surveillance using coronavirus programs and have in particular stated that it is unclear whether the regulatory infrastructure for the coronavirus pandemic will be dismantled after the threat has been resolved.
Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations have issued a statement calling for the restriction of this type of surveillance.
These organizations have listed eight conditions for government initiatives:
Supervision must be “legal, necessary and proportionate”;
Monitoring and monitoring additions must have clauses with the possibility of automatic cancellation;
The application of the data must be limited to cases related to COVID-19;
The security of the data and anonymity must be ensured and protected on the basis of evidence;
Digital surveillance should not pave the way for exacerbation of discrimination and isolation;
The transfer of the data to third parties must be within the framework of the law;
Guarantees shall be considered for the prevention of abuse and the citizen’s right to complain of abuse;
Targeted participation of all "related beneficiaries", including public health experts and fringe groups, is essential. The Chaos Computer (CCC) Club of Germany and Reporters Without Borders (RSF) have also published lists.
Google/Apple's proposed program aims to address the problem of continuous monitoring through the removal of their device's operating systems tracking mechanism after the necessity is resolved.
In some countries, instead of the program, the network-based location process has been used, and as a result, the necessity of downloading the program and the ability to evade intercepted has been eliminated.
In Israel, network-based tracking has been approved.
Network-based solutions that have access to raw spatial data may violate privacy.
However, it’s not that all central server systems require access to personal location data; a number of privacy-keeping systems use central servers only for built-in communications (see section below).
In South Korea, a non-planned system was used to intercept calls.
Instead of using a specialized program, the system collected tracking information from a variety of sources, including mobile device tracking data and card transaction data, and was used to communicate SMS to likely infected people.
In addition to use this information to send messages to potential audiences, the government makes location information available to the public. This type of information disclosure has been allowed due to widespread changes in information protection laws due to the prevalence of MERS in this country.
This information is available to the public through a number of applications and websites. Some countries, such as Germany, have considered the use of centralized and privacy-protecting systems.
By April 6, 2020, these details have not yet been disclosed.
Privactive-to-permanent contact tracking is an accepted concept whose documented research history dates back to at least 2013. By April 7, 2020, dozens of expert groups were working on privacy-protective solutions, such as the use ‘low Bluetooth ‘BLE’ to record the user’s proximity to other mobile phones.
However, PEPP-PT is a harmonious effort that encompasses centralized and decentralized approaches and is not an independent protocol. Decentralized protocols include "close-decentralized tracking with privacy" (DP-PPT/DDP-3T), "Temporary Contact Numbers" (TCN, known as Contact Event Numbers, CEN), "Specity-Speensitive Protocols" and "Spire Call Tracking (PACT).
In these protocols, personal identity data will never be taken out of the device, and all cases of conformity occur inside the device.
The “Privacy Group” at the MIT Media Laboratory has been developing SafePaths, a platform to use privacy-protective practices in the process of collecting and consuming location and routing data to track the COVID-19 outbreak.
The platform is based on the results of the research in the official report "Vulgarity of Programs: Privacy during the pandemic", which was published in March 2020. Another similar initiative is the Enigma MPC's SafeTrace platform. The company explores the development of privacy technologies that have been formed in the "MIT Media Laboratory."
SafeTrace with secure hardware technologies allows users to provide sensitive spatial and hygienic data to other users and authorities without violating data privacy.
On April 5, 2020, the TCN Global Coalition was formed by a number of groups. These groups were centered based on what was essentially similar and largely overlapping protocols aimed at reducing dispersion and providing a platform for global co-operation in the field of interception and warning programs.
On April 9, 2020, the Singaporean government announced that it had publicized the BlueTrace protocol, which had been applied to its government’s official program.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple, the controllers of mobile Android and iOS platforms, unveiled a call-to-imperation initiative that they claim to protect privacy. The initiative is based on the combination of "low Bluetooth energy" technology and privacy encryption.
They have also published the specifications of the main technologies used in this system.
According to Apple and Google, the system is going to be launched in three stages:
Introducing Government Empowerment Tools to Create Coronavirus Interception Programs with Privacy
Direct integration of this feature on iOS and Android. Google and Apple intend to fix the problems of continuous acceptance and monitoring by distributing this system by updating the operating system and then removing it in the same way after the threat is resolved.
b‘Appropriation of the drug (known as a change in application, index changes, task changes, or displacement of the treatment of the drug) refers to a change in the use of the approved drug to treat the disease or a physical problem different from what the drug originally developed.
It’s a trajectory of scientific research to develop safe and effective COVID-19 therapies.
Other research objectives include the development of the COVID-19 vaccine and the transmission of the recovered person's plasma. SARS-CoV-2 has about 66 drug proteins, each of which has multiple ligand binding sites.
Analyzing these binding sites provides a reasonable plan for the development of an effective antiviral drug against COVID-19 proteins.
One of the most important target proteins SARS-CoV-2 is referred to polypacine protease, RNA-related RNA polymerase, halikase, S protein, and ADP ribophase.
Hussain A. and colleagues explored several of the proposed drugs at the skeletal basis at the skeletal with the most approved drugs to accelerate the development of a powerful anti-SARS-CoV-2 in the pre-clinical study that was to be proposed in the design of the clinical study.
Klorokin is an anti-malaria drug that is also used to treat some autoimmune diseases.
On March 18, the WHO announced that chloroquine and related hydroxychloroquine were among the four drugs that were studied in the “unity” clinical trial.
New York Governor Andrew Cuomo announced that New York State tests on chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine will begin on March 24. On March 28, the FDA authorized the use of hydroxychloroquine sulfates and chloroquine phosphate (Emergency Consumption Permits).
This treatment has not been approved in the FDA's clinical trial process, and its use is only permitted for trial treatment and emergency use in hospitalized patients who are unable to receive drugs in a clinical trial.
The CDC has stated that “necessity of consumption, dosage or duration of hydroxychloroquine for the prevention or treatment of SARS-CoV-2 infection has not yet been determined.
Physicians have announced that they will take these medications if there is “non-other option.”
A research group in Istanbul, Turkey, is implementing a small research project on the use of zinc, vitamin A, vitamin C and vitamin D.
Extensive studies are underway at Duke University and Oxford University.
The Langon NYU School of Medicine is conducting a trial on the safety and efficacy efficacy of hydroxychloroquine's preventive consumption.
Clinical trials in Wuhan and Shen Zhen, China, claimed that favipiravir was “completely effective.”
The test result was negative in 35 patients in an average of 4 days, while the duration of the disease in the 45 diseases they did not receive was 11 days.
In a study conducted in Wuhan on 240 patients with pneumonia, half of the patients were given favipiravir to the other and the other half umifenovir.
The Italian Pharmacy Agency reminded the public that the available evidence is inadequated and introducial.
On April ۲, Germany announced that it would buy the drug from Japan for its storage and will get help from the military to deliver medicine to training hospitals, where it will use the drug to treat patients with COVID-19.
According to the South China Morning Post, Shinzo Abe has offered to the Trump administration. The drug may not be so effective to treat acute cases of the disease where the virus has replicated.
It may not be safe for pregnant women or people planning to get pregnant.
A review of lopinavir/ritonavir (Kaletra), the combination of lopinavir and ritonovivir, determined that “there was unanimated.”
These drugs were designed to prevent the replication of HIV through adherence to protease.
A group of researchers from the University of Colorado is trying to modify these drugs to find a combination that can stick to the SARS-CoV-2 protease.
The World Health Organization (WHO (WHO) has included volinavir/ritonavir in an international "unity" trial.
Gilead Sciences had developed Remdesivir as a treatment for ibula viral disease and marburg viral infections. Gilead Sciences later determined that Remdesivir was active in a laboratory environment against phyloe, pneomo, paramixo and corona viruses.
An anti-viral treatment problem is the formation of mutation-by-mutation resistance, which may lead to more severe and contagious diseases.
Some Pre-Terminary Pre-Termination studies suggest that Remdesivir may be an effective genetic barrier to resistance formation. Several clinical trials, involving two cases set by the Cleveland Education Hospital. One involves people with a mild illness and the one people with severe illness.
There are three clinical trials about the effect of vitamin C intravenous injections on hospitalized and severe COVID-19 disease. Two of them are controlled by placebo (China and Canada) and one is uncontrolled (Italia).
The state of New York began trials of antibiotic azithromycin on March 24, 2020.
Japan's National Center for Global Health and Medicine (NCGGM) is planning to conduct a clinical trial for Alvesco Tigen (cyclsonide), a type of inhaled asthma corticosteroide, to treat patients without symptoms and infected with the new coronavirus.
A type of Phase II trial about the enzyme 2 angiotensin transducers consists of 200 ongoing patients. 200 hospitalized and sick patients from Denmark, Germany and Austria are included in the trial to determine the effectiveness of this treatment.
Researchers at the Montreal Heart Institute in Canada are investigating the role of chloroquine in reducing inflammation and respiratory consequences in patients with mild symptoms of COVID-19.
In this study, "Colcorona, 6,000 patients who have been diagnosed with COVID-19 and have mild and unnecessarily hospitalized symptoms are given."
Pregnant or breast-feeding women or women who use effective contraceptive procedures are not eligible to participate in the study.
Several blood coagulation drugs are under investigation in Italy.
Heparin with low molecular weight is widely used to treat patients, which is why the Italian Medical Agency is required to release its use guidelines.
The multi-central study, including 300 patients, to investigate the effectiveness of neoxaparine sodium on the dose of prevention and treatment, was announced in Italy on April 14.
Since SARS-CoV-2 is a type of virus, significant scientific attention has focused on the use of antiviral drugs developed to treat previous contagious diseases such as Mers, SARS and the Western Nile virus.
Ribavirin: ribavirin was proposed according to China's guidelines for the treatment of COVID-19
Omi Fenovir: Omi Fenovier, according to China's 7 edition guidelines for the treatment of COVID-19
Antibiotics that have been proposed as a possible alternative to COVID-19 treatments include:
Tokizumab (Anti-IL-6 receiver): Approved in China.
