b'Covid-19 vaccine is a hypothetical vaccine against coronavirus 2019 (Covid-19).
Although no vaccine has completed clinical trials, several attempts are underway to develop the vaccine.
In late February 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) said it did not expect the SARS-CoV-2 vaccine to become available sooner than the next 18 months.
The proposed five vaccines in April were under Phase I safety studies.
COVID-19 was identified in December 2019.
The widespread global outbreak occurred in 2020, leading to significant investment and research activity to develop vaccines.
Many organizations use released genomes to develop a possible vaccine against SARS-CoV-2.
It was announced in April that the CEPI initiative's requirements for the development of the vaccine include speed, production capacity, on-scale deployment and nationwide access.
In April, CEP scientists announced that 10 different technology platforms are under investigation and development in early 2020 to develop an effective vaccine against COVID-19.
The goals of the main platform that have entered phase I safety studies are:
Nucleic Acid (DNA and RNA) (Phase I Developer and Proposed Vaccine: Moderna, mRNA-1273)
Viravial conveyor (Phase I Developer and Appreciated Vaccine: CanSino Biologics, Adenovirus Type 5)
According to CEPI scientists in April, a total of 115 vaccine proposals are in the early stages of development, 88 have been approved as an active project (79, according to the Milken Institute) and 37 others have been made public, but much information is not publicly available (supposined that they are in the planning or design phase).
The I-II phase trial examines safety and immunization, usually randomized and controlled with control, is carried out at different centers and determines more accurate and effective doses.
Phase III trials usually have more participants, including a control group, and while reviewing the effectiveness of the vaccine for disease prevention, they monitor adverse side effects at the optimal dose.
Of the 79 vaccines offered in active development (approved by early April 2020), 74 cases have not yet been tested on humans (they are “pre-clinical” in the research phase).
On January 24, 2020 in Australia, the University of Quinnsland announced that it was researching the molecular vaccine’s ability to genetically modify viral proteins and simulate an immune response.
On January 24, 2020 in Canada, the International Vaccine Center (VIDO-InterVac) at the University of Saskatchewan announced the beginning of a vaccine that is set to begin human trials in 2021.
Vaccine development plans were introduced at the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention on January 26, 2020, at the University of Hong Kong on January 28.
On January 29, 2020, the pharmaceutical companies Janssen, led by Hanneke Schuitemaker, announced that it had begun the vaccine development process.
Janssen, in collaboration with its biotechnology partner, Vaxart, is developing an oral vaccine.
On March 18, 2020, Emergent BioSolutions announced a manufacturing partnership with Vaxart for vaccine development.
On February 8, 2020, the OncoGen Laboratory in Romania published an article announcing the design of a vaccine similar to the vaccine used to treat neo-antigen cancer.
On March 25, the head of the research institute announced that they had completed the development of the vaccine and were at the beginning of the testing phase.
On February 27, 2020, NuGenerex Immuno-Oncology, one of Generex’s subsidiaries, announced that it had launched a plan to develop the li-Key peptide vaccine against COVID-19.
They planned to develop a vaccine that could be tested “within ‘90 days.”
On March 5, 2020, the University of Washington in St. Louise announced its plans to develop the vaccine.
On March 5, 2020, the U.S. Army's "Medical Research and Facilities Headquarters at Fort Detrick, and Walter Reed's Institute of Military Research in Silver Spring, both in West Maryland, announced that they were developing a vaccine.
About March 10, 2020, Emergent Biosolutions from its partnership with Novavax
Vaccine development and production announced.
These partners later announced that they will conduct pre-clinical testing and phase I clinical trials by July 2020.
On March 12, 2020, India’s Ministry of Health announced that they were testing 11 people in quarantine, and the development of the vaccine would take at least one-and-and-a-a-a-a-half to two years in the shortest.
On March 12, 2020, Medicago, an active biotechnology company in the city of Queb, Quebec, announced the development of a micro-budgeted coronavirus-spooked particle of the Canadian Health Research Institute.
The proposed vaccine is at the stage of laboratory research, and its human trials are scheduled to take place in July or August 2020.
Earlier this week, the Guardian reported that U.S. President Donald Trump had offered CureVac “to provide exclusive access to the COVID-19 vaccine in exchange for a ‘mouse amount of money.”
On March 17, 2020 American pharmaceutical company Pfizer announced a partnership with the German company BioNTech for the joint development of the mRNA-based vaccine.
The proposed mRNA-based vaccine, BNT162, is currently in pre-clinical testing, and its clinical trials are scheduled to begin in April 2020.
In Italy, on March 17, 2020, Takis Biotech, one of Italy’s biotechnology-active companies, announced that their pre-clinical test results would be ready in April 2020, and their proposed final vaccine may be tested on humans before the fall.
In France, on March 19, 2020, the Coalition of Innovations Against the Epidemic (CEPI), announced an investment of $ 4.9 million in a COVID-19 vaccine research consortium, including the Pasteur Institute, Themis Bioscience (Vienna, Austria) and the University of Pittsburgh.
Other CEPI investment partners in the development of the COVID-19 vaccine include Moderna, Curevac, Inovio, Novavax, the University of Hong Kong, the University of Oxford and the University of Queensland.
On March 20, 2020, Russian health officials announced that scientists had begun testing six different vaccines on animals.
On March 20, 2020, researchers from the Royal College of London announced that they were developing a self-strengling RNA vaccine to deal with COVID-19.
The proposed vaccine was developed within 14 days of receiving sequence information from China.
In late March, the Canadian government announced that it intended $275 million to fund $96 research projects in the field of COVID-19, including several proposed vaccines at Canadian companies and universities, such as Medicago and Saskatchewan.
Around the same time, the Canadian government announced that Canada’s $192 millimillion was specifically intended for the development of the COVID-19 vaccine, and plans to create a national “vaccine bank” consisting of several new, usable-applicable vaccines.
On April 2, 2020, researchers at the University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine announced the PittCoVac, the proposed COVID-19 vaccine on the mice, and announced that “SARS-CoV-2 S1 sub-unit vaccines with MNA destroyed strong anti-genin reactions [in mice] that had been revealed 2 weeks after immunization.”
In Canada, on April 16, 2020, the Faculty of Pharmacy of the University of Waterloo announced the design of the proposed DNA-based vaccine, possibly in the form of a nasal.
By using bacterium-eating agents, this DNA will be designed to multiply inside the human bacterium and produce harmless virus-like particles. This process stimulates the immune system to produce antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 virus.
In March 2020, the government, the industry sector, and three U.S. universities merged their resources to access IBM’s supercomputers, as well as Hewlett Packard’s cloud computing resources.
Some vaccines have heterogeneous effects or non-special effects.
That is to say, they may have benefits beyond the prevention of the disease.
Another random trial in Australia is scheduled to enroll in 4,170 healthcare activists.
Vaccines may not be safe or effective in the production phase.
Initial research to assess the effectiveness of the vaccine using COVID-19-specific animal patterns, such as ACE2 transfusion mice, other laboratory and non-human primates, demonstrates the need to prevent Level 3 biosafety pollution prevention measures to work with live viruses and international coordination to ensure standard safety routines.
SARS and MERS vaccines have been tested in non-human animal patterns.
By 2020, there is no vaccine for the treatment or prevention of SARS that has been proven immune and effective in humans.
According to research papers published in 2005 and 2006, the identification and development of new vaccines and drugs for the treatment of SARS has been a priority for governments and public health institutions around the world.
During the MERS outbreak, it was thought that current research on MERS would likely provide a useful format for the development of vaccines and drugs against MERS-CoV infection.
As of March 2020, there was a MERS (DNA-based) vaccine that had undergoed Phase I clinical trials in humans, and there were three other vaccines that were all carriers of the virus. There were also two adeno-MERS, BVRS-GamVac vector vaccines (MVA-MERS).
Views published on social media have promoted the conspiracy theory about the recognition of the COVID-19 virus and the existence of a vaccine.
Inventies listed on various social media refer to previous inventions related to genetic sequences and vaccines related to other types of coronavirus, such as the SARS coronavirus.
b‘Accidental Disease 2019 (Covid-19) is a type of infectious disease that is known for acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
Its general symptoms include fever, cough, and shortness of breath.
Other symptoms include fatigue, muscular pain, diarrhea, sore throat, hearing loss and abdominal pain.
The distance between exposure to the pathogen to symptoms is usually about five days but may vary from two to fourteen days.
While most cases lead to mild symptoms, some cases lead to viral pneumonia and multi-discipline defects.
As of April 17, 2020, more than 2.24 millionum cases were reported from 2,010 countries and territories, of which 15,3,0000 have resulted in deaths.
More than 568,000 people have recovered. The virus is mainly caused by close contact, often through tiny drops caused by cough, sneezing, or talking.
Although exhalation triggers the release of these droplets, the droplets usually fall on the ground or on the surfaces instead of a long-distance transfer.
Individuals may be affected by touching infected surfaces and then touching the eyes, nose, and mouth.
The virus can survive for up to 72 hours.
Of course, it is also possible to spread the virus before the appearance of symptoms and in the later stages of the disease. The standard method of diagnosis is the chain reaction of instant reverse transcription (rRT-PCR) from nasophorex.
The use of masks is recommended for people suspected of contracting the virus and those around them.
Recommending to wearing masks is different for the general public; so some officials oppose the use of masks, and some consider it mandatory to wear a mask.
Currently, there is no particular antiviral vaccine or antiviral treatment for COVID-19.
Local disease transfusion has been recorded in most countries in all six regions covered by the WHO.
People with the virus may be unmarked or have flu-like symptoms such as fever, cough, fatigue, and shortness of breath.
Animal symptoms include the difficulty of breathing, pain or constant pressure in the chest, dizziness, difficulty walking and bruising of the face or lips. If these symptoms do not go away, you should see a doctor immediately.
Symptoms related to the upper respiratory tract, such as wheezing, runny nose, or sore throat, have also been seen with less spread.
Stomatic-intestinal symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea have been observed in variable percentages.
Some cases in China in the early stage suffered only shortness of breath and severe heart palpitations.
In others, the disease may last up to the stage of pneumonia, multi-member defect, and even death.
This course is called the incubation period.
The COVID-19 incubation period is usually five to six days but may range from two to 14 days.
At 97.5% of people, the symptoms become apparent within 11.5 days of infection. Reports indicate that not all infected people necessarily have symptoms.
Of course, preliminary evidence suggests that these people can cause the spread of the disease.
The proportion of people without symptoms is currently unspecified and is under investigation. "KCDC Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (KCDC) have stated that 20% of all confirmed cases have remained unsigned during hospitalization.
The China National Health Commission (CNA) began to incorporate unmarked cases from April 1. Of the 166 cases of infection, 130 (78%) at the time of the test were without symptoms.
Both phlegm and saliva may carry the virus.
Talking loudly disperses more particles compared to speaking slowly.
Singaporean researchers have proven that not covering the mouth and nose during a cough may spread tiny droplets up to a distance of 4.5 meters (15 feet).
Although the virus isn’t usually aerobic, the National Academy of Sciences has stated that there is a possibility of airborne transmission, and in the air collectors, which are installed in the hallway outside of people’s rooms, there have been traces of a positive sample of the virus’s RNA.
Some medical routines, such as intubation and cardiovascular resuscitation (CPR), may result in the release of respiratory secretions and the spread of the virus through the air.
Although there are concerns about the transmission of the virus through the stool, this is assumed to be a small chance of transmitting the virus through people with symptoms.
The European Center for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC) has announced that each person usually infects two to three people despite the unspecified disease transmission mechanism.
In particular, the virus on the cardboard for one day, on plastic (polypropylene) and stainless steel (AISI 304) for three days, and on copper 99% for a maximum of four hours.
Of course, this period depends on humidity and temperature.
