Coronavirus severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is a new type of severe acute respiratory syndrome, which was first taken from three people with pneumonia and associated with acute respiratory disease cases in Wuhan.
All new SARS-CoV-2 symptoms are seen in coronavirus-related nature.
This virus disappears outside of the human body with homemade soap. Bubble soap removes its protection. SARS-CoV-2 is closely related to the original SARS-CoV.
Covid-19 has the greatest effect on the lungs because the virus enters the host cell through the enzyme 2 angiotensin (ACE2) transducers, which is abundant in the type II bubble cells of the lungs.
The virus uses a specific surface glycoprotein (ppleumer) to connect to ACE2 and enter the host cell.
Acute heart damage was observed in 12% of people infected and hospitalized in Wuhan Hospital. This problem is more common in severe cases of the disease.
The rate of cardiovascular symptoms due to the inflammatory response of cythmic and immune system abnormalities is high in the progression of the disease, but acute myocardial damage may also be related to ACE2 receptors in the heart.
ACE2 receptors are abundant in the heart and play a role in heart activity.
The high incidence of thrombosis (31%) and venous thermobabolism (25%) has been seen in ICU patients with COVID-19 infection and may be related to the weakness of the diagnosis. Dissections of people who have died from COVID-19 showed scattered bubble damage (DAD) and the infiltration of inflammation containing lymphocytes in the lungs.
While SARS-COV-2 tends to ACE2 coat cells in the respiratory duct, the symptoms of severe systemic inflammation have been seen in patients with COVID-19.
In particular, the relationship between GM-CSF pathogenic GM-CSF cells has been established by the absorption of inflammatory IL-6 secretting monocytes and severe lung pathology in COVID-19 patients.
The influence of lymphocytes has also been reported during the autopsy.
The WHO has released several test protocols for the disease.
The standard method of diagnosis is the chain reaction of instant reverse-transcription polymerase (rRT-PCR).
This test is usually performed on breathing samples obtained with nasophanerex swab. Of course, the nasal swab or phlegm is also used.
The results are usually ready within a few hours to two days.
Blood tests can also be used, but this requires taking two blood samples with a two-week interval. In this type of test, the result is not worth much.
Chinese scientists were able to isolate a species of coronavirus and release its genetic sequence. Labs around the world were able to conduct independent polymerase-responsive (PCR) chain-responding tests to detect a viral infection.
By April 4, 2020, antibody tests (which are capable of detecting active infections and a person's history) were under study but weren’t yet.
China’s experience in testing is only 60 to 70%.
In the U.S., the U.S. approved the first check-of-the-point of care for use at the end of that month.
The turbulence turbulence of a multi-glued mutte glass with a lateral, symmetrical and posterior distribution is used to diagnose in the early stages of the infection.
Subversion, the defective coating (the overturning of the blade of the beet with a bubble variable filling) and tightening may be a sign of the progression of the disease.
There’s not much to know about microscopic and pathophizological damage of COVID-19.
The most important pathological findings in dissections are:
Macroscopy: pneumonia, pericarde, heart hardening and respiratory edema
The four levels of viral pneumonia intensity are visible:
Mild pneumonia: Respiratory edema, pneumosite hyperplasia, uncommunicated pneumocyte, inflammation of the intestinal with lymphocyte penetration and the formation of a large multi-nuclear cell
Severe pneumonia: Sporadic bubble damage (DAD) with dispersed bubble secretions.
DAD is the cause of acute respiratory distress (ARDS) and severe hypoxemia.
Improved pneumonia: The formation of secretions in bubble cavities and respiratory fiscal fibrosis
Blood: Sporadic intravenous clot (DIC); Locoritroblastic reaction
Preventive measures to reduce the possibility of infection include staying at home, avoiding crowded places, washing your hands with soap for at least 20 seconds, observing respiratory hygiene and avoiding touching the eyes, nose or mouth with unwashed hands.
The CDC recommends covering the mouth and nose with a napkin when coughing and sneezing and using the inner part of the elbow.
Hand hygiene is strongly recommended after coughing or sneezing.
The CDC has recommended the use of cloth masks in public places to some extent to prevent the spread of the disease by people without symptoms. Social distancing strategies seek to reduce the contact of infected people with large groups by closing schools and workplaces, restricting travel, and abolition of large gatherings.
Distancing guidelines also comply with a distance of at least 6 feet (1.8 meters) between people.
There is no effective medication to prevent COVID-19. Since the discovery of the vaccine does not occur at least until 2021, an important part of the COVID-19 management process is an attempt to reduce the peak of the pandemic, or "the leveling of the curve."
The CDC also recommends that people wash their hands regularly with soap and water for at least 20 seconds. This washing becomes more necessary, especially after leaving the toilet or being infected with your hands, before eating and after nose, cough or sneezing.
The CDC also recommends the use alcoholic hand sanitizer with alcohol content of at least 60% if soap and water are not provided. For areas where regular hand detergents are not available, the WHO has provided two formulas for local production.
In these formulas, an anti-microbical agent is obtained from ethanol or isopropanole.
Hydrogen peroxide is used to remove microbial hoabs; “It’s not an active ingredient for hand disintegration.”
Glycerol is added as a moisture retainer.
Individuals are managed with supportive care, which may include fluid therapy, oxygen-support, and support for other damaged vital organs.
The CDC recommends that people who are suspected of being infected should wear a simple mask.
Extrospective membrane (ECMO) has been used to deal with the problem of respiratory failure, but the benefits are still being investigated.
Self-sacrificing personal hygiene and family lifestyle and dietary changes have been suggested to boost safety.
Supportive treatments may be beneficial for people with mild symptoms in the early stages of the infection. The WHO and the China National Health Commission have made proposals to take care of people hospitalized due to COVID-19.
Critical care professionals and lung specialists in the U.S. have compiled the therapeutic proposals of various institutions in the form of a free resource called IBCC.
By April 2020, there is no special treatment for COVID-19.
To relieve symptoms, some medical professionals have diagnosed parastamol (acetaminophen) for first-line use better than ibuprofen.
To minimize the possibility of transmitting the virus, it is necessary to take precautions, especially in therapeutic places where particulate-producing routines, such as piping or manual airation.
For medical professionals who take care of people with COVID-19, the CDC recommends keeping a person in an aerobic infection quarantine (AIIR) and the use of standard precautions, contact prevention measures, and aerobic precautions.
Recommended items are: PPE Gun, Respiratory Mask or Face Mask, Eye Protector and Surgical Gloves. If any, the use of a breathing mask (instead of a face mask) is a priority.
The N95 Respiratory Mask has been approved for industrial environments, but the FDA has allowed the use of the mask with an “emergency requirement” (EUA).
This mask is designed to protect against aerobic particles such as dust, but its effectiveness against specific biological factors is not ensured for consumption outside the original application.
If the mask isn’t available, the CDC recommends wearing a face protector, or as a last resort.
Most cases of COVID-19 are not so severe that it entails the use of mechanical air conditioning or alternative methods, but a percentage of them need these measures.
The type of respiratory support for people with respiratory insufficiency caused by COVID-19 is being investigated for hospitalized. Some evidence suggests that intubation can be prevented with high-flowing cannola or double-level positive pressure in the respiratory tract.
It’s still unclear whether each of these two methods will lead to similar benefits in sick patients.
Some doctors prefer to use invasive mechanical aerobilization, if any, because it restricts the dispersal rate of fine particles compared to high-flow nostrinal canola. The prevalence of severe cases is higher in the aging (people over the age of 60, and especially people over the age of 80).
In many developed countries, hospital beds are not enough per capita. As a result, the capacity of the health system is limited to managing a sudden increase in severe cases of COVID-19 who need to be hospitalized.
In a Chinese study, it was found that 5% of patients were hospitalized in the intensive care, 2.3% need mechanical air conditioning and losing 1.4%.
In China, about 30% of people who are hospitalized due to COVID-19 are eventually monitored in the ICU.
Mechanical aeration becomes more complicated as acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) in COVID-19 is intensified, and oxygenation becomes much more difficult.
To maximize oxygen delivery and reduce the risk of pulmonary damage related to the air transmitter and breast tail, it is necessary to use an aerator with high pressure and PPEP control modes.
Old aerospace machines may not have a high PEEP.
Investigating possible treatments began in January 2020 and several antiviral drugs are in the clinical trial phase.
Remdesivir appears to be the most promising among medicines.
Although the development of the new drugs will take until 2021, several of the drugs under-the-test have already been approved for other uses or are advanced in the testing phase.
Antiviral medication may be tested in people with severe illness.
The WHO has suggested that candidates participate in tests related to the efficacy and safety of possible drugs. The FDA has issued a temporary license to take recovery plasma as an experimental treatment, where a person's life is acute or completely at risk.
This drug has not undergone the necessary clinical studies to prove the effectiveness and immunity associated with the disease.
In February 2020, China unveiled a mobile app to manage the outbreak.
Users are asked to enter their name and identification number.
The program can identify ‘closest contact’ with the help of regulatory data and determine the likelihood of infection.
Each user can also check the status of the other three users.
If the potential danger is detected, the app will report the issue to local health authorities in addition to offering home quarantine. Large-data analysis on mobile phones, facial recognition technology, mobile phone tracking and artificial intelligence have been used to track infected people and people associated with them in South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore.
In March 2020, the Israeli government made it possible for security agencies to intercept mobile data from people suspected of contracting COVID-19.
The measure was taken to enforce quarantine and to protect people who may have been in contact with infected citizens.
In March 2020, Deutsche Telekom also provided mobile cumulative location data to the Robert Koch Institute of the Federal Government of Germany in order to allow research on the spread of the virus and prevent it.
Russia has used facial diagnosis technology to identify quarantine offenders.
Giulio Gallera, Italy’s regional health officer, has been informed by mobile operators that “40% of people continue to travel freely.”
The German government held a 48-hour-hour weekend involving more than 42,000 people.
Estonian President Kersti Kaljulaid also issued a call to attract creative strategies to prevent the spread of the coronavirus.
Quarantine, travel restrictions, side effects of treatment, or fear of infection may cause people to feel stressed.
"Increasing social isolation, loneliness, worry about health, mental pressure, and economic problems can change people's mental and physical health," the BBC quoted Rory O'Connor.
