Viral cytopatic changes have been seen in pneumocytes.
The image of the lung was similar to the image of the lung with acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).
At 11.8% of the deaths cited in China's National Health Commission's "National Health Commission", an increase in troponine rates or heart attacks have caused heart damage.
According to U.S. March data, 89% of hospitalized people have already been infected. The availability of medical resources and socio-economic status of the region also affects the death rate.
Estimating the death toll from the disease varies due to regional differences and methodological difficulties.
A small number of mild cases may lead to an overestimation of the loss rate.
Of course, the fact that deaths were the result of previous cases may mean that the current mortality rate has been underestimated.
Smokers, compared to non-smokers, have a 1.4-fold chance of severe symptoms of COVID-19 and require 2.4 times more intensive or dietary attention. The long-term consequences of the disease have raised concerns.
The Hong Kong Department of Hospitals reported a reduction of 20% to 30% of the lung capacity in some people who are recovering from the disease and the presence of symptoms of organ damage in a lung scan.
This condition may lead to special post-care syndrome after recovery.
By March 2020, it wasn’t clear whether a history of infection would provide effective and long-term impunity in people recovering from the disease.
Immunity is based on the behavior of other coronaviruses, but in some cases, the results of a coronavirus test have also been positive in people recovering from COVID-19.
It is believed that these cases are caused by the deterioration of the latent infection, not the infection again.
It is thought that this virus is normal and of animal origin, through overflowing infection.
The actual origin is unknown, but by December 2019, the prevalence of the infection was almost entirely based on human-to-human transmission.
By reviewing the first 41 confirmed COVID-19 cases, published in The Lancet in January 2020, the first of its symptoms will date back to December 1, 2019.
Formal World Publications have announced the date of the first cases of the emergence of signs on December 8, 2019.
To measure mortality statistics, different criteria are commonly used.
These figures will change according to the region and over time, affecting the volume of tests, the quality of the health system, treatment options, the time spent on the initial prevalence and demographic characteristics such as age, gender and overall health.
In late 2019, the WHO allocated U07.1 Emergency ICD-10 disease codes for confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection deaths in the laboratory and U07.2 for COVID-19-related deaths subject to a clinical or pediagnostic diagnosis without SARS-CoV-2 infection approved in the laboratory. The loss rate is obtained by dividing the number of deaths by the number of cases diagnosed within a specified timeframe.
According to Johns Hopkins University, as of April 17, 2020, the global fatality rate was 6.9% (153,822/2,240,191).
This figure differs in each region. Other measurement scales can be referred to the case mortality rate (CFR), which specifies the percentage of people who have been diagnosed by the disease. Infectious mortality (IFR) is another criterion that specifies the percentage of infected (diagnosed and undiagnosed).
These statistics are not limited to time and follow a specific population from the stage of the infection to the closure of the case.
Although not all infected people necessarily produce antibodies, the presence of antibodies may provide information about the number of infected people.
In a small 4,600-person village of Castiglione d'Adda, the epicenter of the virus outbreak in Italy has killed 80 tons (1.7%).
