The effect of this pandemic and its fatality rates vary between men and women.
In studies conducted in China and Italy, the mortality rate is higher in men.
The highest chance of infection in men at an older age of is 50. This gap between men and women in the age of 90 is eliminated.
In China, the death rate was 2.8 per cent for men and 1.7 per cent for women.
The reasons for this sexual difference are unclear, but genetic and behavioral factors may have an impact.
Gender-based cognitive immunological differences, lower prevalence of smoking among women, and the presence of most men with common diseases such as hypertension, at a younger age may play a role in the rate of male fatalities.
In Europe, 57% of infected people died and 72% who died from COVID-19 were men.
By April 2020, the U.S. government has not taken action to intercept gender-related data on COVID-19 infections.
Research has shown that viral diseases such as Ebola, HIV, influenza, and SARS have a different effect on men and women.
A higher percentage of the medical staff, especially nurses, are females and are more likely to be exposed to the virus.
On February 11, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) announced that "Covid-19" has been named the disease.
Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, the head of the WHO, announced that CO is the hysterical of the coronavirus, the virus-related virus, D abbreviation of the disease, and the time of its first identification: December 31, 2019.
To prevent referrals to specific geographical locations (such as China), animal species, or group of individuals, it has been named based on international proposals and has been chosen with the aim of preventing labeling. The COVID-19-based coronavirus is called severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
The WHO also uses the title of "covid-19 virus" and "covid-19 virus" in official communications.
The disease and the viole are commonly referred to as the Coronavirus.
In the early stages of the outbreak in Wuhan, China, the name "Coronavirus" and "Wuhan Coronavirus" were jointly given to the virus and the disease.
In January 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) considered 2019-nCov and acute respiratory disease of 2019-nCoV as temporary names of the disease. These names were selected according to the 2015 guidelines about avoiding the inclusion of places to name the virus and the disease.
Official names of COVID-19 and SARS-CoV-2 were released on February 11, 2020.
Due to capacity limitations in standard supply chains, some digital manufacturers produce sanitary items such as nasal swabs and aircraft parts.
For example, in one case, an Italian hospital had an unreassed valve and the supplier couldn't supply it in a timely manner. As a result, a local start-up began to reverse engineering and produce 100 valves at night.
After the early stages of the COVID-19 outbreak, conspiracy theories, misinformation and incomplete information about the origin, scale, prevention, treatment and other aspects of the disease were formed and quickly published online.
Apparently, the virus is transmitted from humans to some animals.
Studies have yielded evidence of replication of the virus in pigs, ducks, and poultry.
No drugs or vaccines have been approved to treat the disease.
International research on vaccines and COVID-19 drugs is underway among government agencies, scientific groups and industrial researchers.
In March, the World Health Organization implemented the “unity trial” to assess the therapeutic impact of the four existing antivirus compounds most promising.
There's no vaccine yet, but various institutions are actively investigating the proposed vaccines.
The results of previous SARS-CoV surveys are used because SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 both use ACE2 receptors to enter human cells.
Three vaccination strategies are under investigation.
First, the researchers are looking to develop a complete virus vaccine.
The use of viruses, inactivated or dead, aims to prevent an immune response in the human body against a new COVID-19 infection.
The second strategy, sub-unit vaccines are aimed at developing a vaccine that can make the immune system sensitive to some sub-unities of the virus.
In the case of SARS-CoV-2, this research is based on Spike S protein, which contributes to the virus in entering the ACE2 enzymatic receptor.
The third strategy relates to nucleic acid vaccines (DNA or RNA vaccines, the new method of vaccine preparation).
Experimental vaccines for each of these strategies should be examined in terms of safety and efficacy. On March 16, 2020, the first clinical trial of the vaccine began with the presence of four volunteers in Seattle.
The vaccine contains a harmless genetic code copied from the disease-causing virus. Antibody-based development has been cited as a potential vaccine development challenge for SARS-COV-2, but there's room for debate in this regard.
As of April 2020, more than 300 active clinical trials were underway.
Seven clinical trials evaluated treatments that had already been approved to treat malaria, including four research projects on hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine.
Antiviral drugs are subject to the subject of most Chinese research, which will be the result of nine Phase III trials on remdesivir in several countries by the end of April.
As of April 2020, there has been a survey of the clinical development dynamics of the COVID-19 vaccine and the proposed drugs. There are several antiviral drugs available for the treatment of COVID-19, including remdesivir, chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine, lopinavir/ritonavir, and lopinavir/ploinavir with the combination of beta interferon.
As of March 2020, there was inconclusive evidence of the remdesivir efficacy.
Clinical recovery was observed in patients who had been treated with unlicensed remdesivir.
Phase III clinical trials are underway in the U.S., China and Italy. Klerukin, previously used to treat malaria, was reviewed in China in February 2020, and its preliminary results were published.
Of course, the research community has been invited to criticize these results.
Korean and Chinese health agencies have proposed the use of chloroquine.
However, the Wuhan Institute of Virology, while recommending a daily intake of a gram, has stated that taking dosage twice as much is very dangerous and may be deadly.
On March 28, 2020, the FDA authorized the emergency use of hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine, at the discretion of physicians treating people with COVID-19.
Preliminary data shows that high-dose rebavirine consumption is necessary to prevent SARS-CoV-2 replication in the laboratory environment.
Nitazoxanide has been suggested for research in the human body after its low-concentrating impact on deterrence from SARS-CoV-2 replication. Research has shown that it is necessary to prepare the primary spike protein with trans membrane syrin protease (TMPRSSS2) for SARS-CoV-2 through interaction with the ACE2 receptor.
The study of chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine with/without azithromycin has major restrictions that have prevented the medical community from adopting these treatments. Osletamivir does not prevent SARS-CoV-2 in the laboratory and its role in the treatment of COVID-19 has not been proven.
Sytokine may be one of the side effects of the later stages of COVID-19 severe.
